6	kenji	last_brief	# Literature Brief: LLM Failure Modes in Visual Layout & Spatial Reasoning\n\n**Prepared by:** Kenji Ochiai\n**Research question:** What are the most significant documented failure modes when LLMs attempt to reason about visual layout and spatial semantics, and what approaches have shown any traction against them?\n**Status:** ⚠️ Evidence base is substantially thinner than this question warrants — see Section 4.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Documented Failure Modes\n\n### 1.1 Spatial Relation Recognition (Direct Evidence)\n\nThe strongest directly relevant evidence comes from **[2205.00363v3]** (Liu et al., "Visual Spatial Reasoning"). Key findings:\n\n- **Human–model performance gap is large and robust.** Human ceiling on the VSR benchmark sits above 95%; state-of-the-art VLMs at time of publication achieved only ~70%. This is not a marginal gap — it is ~25 percentage points on tasks designed around basic spatial predicates (*under, in front of, facing*, etc.).\n- **Orientation relations are a specific, acute failure mode.** The tested VLMs were broadly incapable of recognizing relations that require interpreting an object's facing direction or orientation. This is notable because orientation is foundational to layout reasoning (e.g., reading order, figure/ground relationships, directional hierarchy in diagrams).\n- **Training-data frequency does not explain the failures.** The authors observe that per-relation model performance has little correlation with how frequently those relations appear in training data. This rules out a simple data-sparsity explanation and points toward a structural representational deficit.\n- **Reference frame variation is a compounding factor.** The dataset includes spatial language that shifts reference frames (egocentric vs. allocentric, viewer-centered vs. object-centered). Models fail to track these shifts — a failure mode directly relevant to any layout paradigm where spatial language must be interpreted relative to a compositional context.\n\n**Confidence level on this finding: High.** Dataset is large (10k+ pairs, 66 relation types), human baselines are rigorously established, multi-model evaluation is included.\n\n### 1.2 Logical Reasoning in Visual Contexts (Indirect Evidence)\n\n**[2407.04973v1]** (Xiao et al., "LogicVista") provides relevant adjacent evidence. The benchmark spans 5 logical reasoning task types and 9 capabilities across 8 MLLMs. While the paper focuses on logical rather than spatial reasoning specifically, it documents:\n\n- Systematic gaps in MLLMs' ability to perform integrated reasoning *over visual inputs*, including navigation and puzzle-solving tasks that require spatial cognition.\n- Inconsistent performance profiles across model families, suggesting the failure is not uniformly distributed and may depend on architecture or pretraining regime.\n\n**Confidence level: Moderate.** LogicVista's scope is broader than spatial layout specifically; it is useful for contextualizing the VSR findings within a wider pattern of visual reasoning deficits, but I would not cite it as primary evidence for spatial layout failures without caveats.\n\n### 1.3 Reasoning Self-Assessment Failures (Adjacent, Not Spatial)\n\n**[2309.02144v1]** (Wang et al., "Making LLMs Better Reasoners with Alignment") identifies an *Assessment Misalignment* problem: fine-tuned LLMs systematically assign higher scores to lower-quality chain-of-thought responses. This is documented in the context of general reasoning benchmarks, not visual/spatial tasks.\n\n**Relevance to our question:** Indirect. If a model cannot reliably assess the quality of its own reasoning chain, this likely compounds spatial reasoning failures — a model may produce a plausible-sounding spatial inference and have no mechanism to detect its own error. But this remains an inference on my part; the paper does not test spatial layout scenarios.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Approaches Showing Traction\n\n### 2.1 Alignment Fine-Tuning for Reasoning Quality\n\n**[2309.02144v1]** proposes Alignment Fine-Tuning (AFT): a three-step paradigm of (1) COT fine-tuning, (2) positive/negative response categorization by outcome correctness, and (3) a constraint alignment loss that pushes positive-scored COTs above negative ones while preventing score collapse. Results across four reasoning benchmarks are positive.\n\n**Applicability caveat:** All evaluation is on text-based reasoning benchmarks (no spatial or visual layout tasks). Whether AFT generalizes to multimodal spatial reasoning is not established by this paper. I would treat this as a methodological candidate worth watching, not a validated solution for our domain.\n\n### 2.2 Token-Efficient Reasoning via Prompt Optimization\n\n**[2604.14214v1]** (Shah et al., CROP) and **[2510.00071v2]** (Zheng, ARS) both address reasoning inefficiency — verbose chain-of-thought generation — through different mechanisms (prompt optimization and adaptive suppression of redundant reasoning steps, respectively). Both report substantial token reductions (CROP: 80.6%; ARS: up to 53–58%) with minimal accuracy degradation on math/logic benchmarks.\n\n**Applicability caveat:** Neither paper addresses visual layout or spatial reasoning	2026-07-20 08:52:00.311637	2026-07-20 08:52:00.311637
8	naledi	last_brief	# Companion Brief: Visual Encoding & Cognitive Load — Why Some Forms Work and Others Don't\n\n**Prepared by:** Naledi Mokoena\n**Responding to:** Kenji's literature brief on LLM visual/spatial reasoning failure modes\n**Angle:** Cognitive science & HCI — the "why" behind visual encoding effectiveness\n**Status:** Claims are flagged throughout as experimentally grounded (EG), well-supported inference (WSI), or informed extrapolation (IE).\n\n---\n\n## Framing Note\n\nKenji's brief establishes *that* LLMs fail at spatial reasoning tasks. What it doesn't fully address — and what I want to build toward for Mateo — is the prior question: what makes a visual encoding cognitively tractable *for a human reader* in the first place, and does that theory of tractability give us design handles we can actually build into a visualization engine? My claim is that it does, and that the cognitive-science literature points to specific, non-obvious mechanisms that a slide-based paradigm systematically ignores.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The Core Framework: Three Memory Systems, One Bottleneck\n\nThe theoretical spine of everything that follows is **Cognitive Load Theory** (Sweller, 1988; Paas & Van Merriënboer, 1994) integrated with **Working Memory resource models** (Baddeley & Hitch's multicomponent model). The practical summary:\n\nWorking memory is the bottleneck. It has distinct subsystems — the **visuospatial sketchpad** (VSSP) processes spatial and visual information; the **phonological loop** processes verbal/sequential information. These channels are partially independent. **(EG — this channel separation has very strong experimental support going back decades.)**\n\nCognitive load theory partitions total load into three types:\n\n- **Intrinsic load:** determined by the inherent complexity of the content and the number of interacting elements that must be held simultaneously\n- **Extraneous load:** load imposed by *how* information is presented — design choices, unnecessary decorative elements, poor spatial organization\n- **Germane load:** load that supports schema formation — the "useful" load of actively constructing mental models\n\nThe design goal for a visualization engine is not simply *less load.* It is: minimize extraneous load, manage intrinsic load through chunking and progressive disclosure, and preserve germane load because that's where comprehension actually happens.\n\nThis is the first place where slides fail structurally. A static slide treats all three as undifferentiated — the designer puts everything on the canvas and the reader sorts it out.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Perceptual Channels and Pre-attentive Processing\n\n**[EG — well-established; see Treisman & Gelade, 1980; Healey & Enns, 2012 for the canonical reviews]**\n\nSome visual features are processed **pre-attentively** — they register in parallel across the visual field before focused attention is deployed. These include:\n\n- Color hue and luminance contrast\n- Motion onset\n- Size/length differentials beyond a threshold\n- Orientation of line elements\n- Closure (complete vs. incomplete shapes)\n\nPre-attentive features matter because they allow a viewer to detect structure without consuming working memory. A visualization that encodes its most important signal in a pre-attentive channel lets the reader "see" the answer before they've consciously read anything.\n\nWhat is **not** pre-attentive: conjunction of features, numerical labels, relational text, ordered sequences that require reading. Anything that requires sequential processing burns working memory.\n\n**The implication Kenji's brief doesn't surface explicitly:** the VLM failure modes he documents — particularly the orientation and reference-frame failures from Liu et al. — map onto precisely the features that human vision also processes *least reliably* in high-load conditions. Orientation is pre-attentive in isolation but becomes attention-demanding when embedded in a cluttered scene or when it requires reference frame translation. Humans under cognitive load show degraded orientation discrimination that partially resembles the VLM failure pattern. This is not coincidence; it's evidence that orientation encoding carries inherent processing cost, regardless of substrate. **(WSI — the parallel is supported by dual-task studies of human spatial cognition under load, e.g., Logie et al., 1990; I'm extending to VLMs, which is IE.)**\n\n---\n\n## 3. The Spatial Contiguity and Split-Attention Effects\n\n**[EG — extensively replicated; Mayer, 2009 "Multimedia Learning" synthesizes this body of work]**\n\nTwo of the best-replicated findings in educational technology and information design:\n\n**Spatial contiguity effect:** Comprehension is better when related information is placed physically close together. When a label is separated from what it labels, readers must hold one in memory while scanning for the other — pure extraneous load. The cost is measurable in both response time and accuracy.\n\n**Split-attention effect:** When a reader must mentally integrate two spatially or temporally separated sources — a diagram and a legend, a chart and a caption, a visualization and an annotation panel — comprehension degrades relative to integrated formats. The integration work itself consumes working memory that could go toward understanding.\n\nBoth effects are directly violated by the standard slide paradigm:	2026-07-20 08:59:59.840932	2026-07-20 08:59:59.840932
10	mateo	last_explanation	## What I built and why\n\nThis prototype demonstrates **progressive spatial disclosure** — content reveals itself in sequence rather than all at once, with each new element placed directly adjacent to what it relates to. The core motivation is Naledi's spatial contiguity + split-attention findings: when labels, annotations, and data are physically co-located and revealed progressively, the reader never has to hold a separated element in memory while scanning for its referent. The secondary motivation is Kenji's finding from Liu et al. that orientation-dependent spatial relations are an acute failure mode — so I deliberately avoided relying on orientation encoding and instead used proximity, color hue (pre-attentive, per Naledi's Section 2), and sequential emergence to carry the relational meaning. The result is something a static slide genuinely cannot do: the visualization *builds itself* in a way that controls cognitive load at each step.	2026-07-20 09:02:37.817928	2026-07-20 09:04:43.536081
14	ingrid	last_review	# Review Report — Altair8 Iteration [Current]\n**Reviewer:** Dr. Ingrid Solberg\n**Inputs reviewed:** Kenji's literature brief, Naledi's cognitive-science brief, Mateo's prototype rationale\n**Verdict at end.**\n\n---\n\n## I. Kenji's Literature Brief\n\n### What holds up\n\nKenji has done something I genuinely respect: he has flagged the thinness of his own evidence base before I had to. The Section 4 warning and the confidence-level annotations throughout are exactly the kind of epistemic hygiene that prevents a brief from silently inflating into something it isn't. That intellectual honesty makes the document more useful, not less.\n\nThe VSR findings from Liu et al. [2205.00363v3] are described accurately and the confidence designation is appropriate. The human–model performance gap (~25 percentage points), the orientation-specific failure mode, and the training-frequency non-correlation are all findings the paper genuinely supports, and Kenji has not stretched them. The moderate confidence flag on LogicVista is also correct — it's a broader benchmark and using it as primary evidence for spatial layout failures specifically would have been a methodological error. He didn't make that error.\n\n### Concerns\n\n**1. The assessment-misalignment finding (Wang et al.) is doing more inferential work than Kenji acknowledges.**\n\nKenji describes the relevance of [2309.02144v1] to spatial reasoning as "an inference on my part" — that's honest — but then includes it in a section titled "Documented Failure Modes." It isn't a documented failure mode in spatial tasks. It's a reasoning-quality assessment problem in text benchmarks, extended by analogy to our domain. I would move this to a dedicated "Speculative Extensions" section or cut it entirely from the failure-modes section. As written, it sits in a position where a reader who doesn't catch the caveat might treat it as evidence it isn't.\n\n**2. The traction section (Section 2) is weakly connected to the research question.**\n\nThe research question is about LLM failure modes in *visual layout and spatial reasoning* and what approaches show traction *against those failures*. CROP and ARS address token efficiency in math/logic benchmarks. AFT addresses reasoning-quality alignment in text benchmarks. None of the three approaches has been tested on spatial or visual layout tasks. Kenji flags this with applicability caveats, which is correct — but the practical result is that Section 2 answers a different question. The honest summary is: *there are no documented approaches with demonstrated traction on the specific failure modes identified in Section 1.* That's an important finding, not a gap to paper over with adjacent methodology. Kenji should state it plainly.\n\n**3. The brief cuts off mid-sentence in Section 2.2.** I'm flagging this as a production issue. It doesn't affect the evidence quality but it means the document as submitted is incomplete.\n\n---\n\n## II. Naledi's Cognitive-Science Brief\n\n### What holds up\n\nThe framing is genuinely useful. Asking "what makes a visual encoding cognitively tractable in the first place" is the right prior question, and Naledi is correct that Kenji's brief doesn't address it. The integration of Cognitive Load Theory with the Baddeley-Hitch model is standard and accurately represented. The EG/WSI/IE claim-status annotations are excellent practice — this is exactly how to present a brief where some claims are rock-solid and others involve extension.\n\nThe spatial contiguity and split-attention effects (Section 3) are among the most replicated findings in educational and multimedia research. Citing Mayer (2009) as a synthesis is appropriate; those effects are not in dispute.\n\n### Concerns\n\n**1. The VLM–human parallel in Section 2 is labeled IE but treated rhetorically as stronger.**\n\nNaledi writes: "This is not coincidence; it's evidence that orientation encoding carries inherent processing cost, regardless of substrate."\n\nI need to push back on this. That phrasing — "this is not coincidence; it's evidence" — is asserting a causal mechanism (shared processing cost across substrate) from an observational parallel. The observation itself is interesting and worth including. But the parallel between human orientation discrimination under load (Logie et al., 1990) and VLM orientation failure (Liu et al.) could reflect completely different underlying mechanisms that happen to produce similar behavioral signatures. The human effect is a resource depletion phenomenon in a specific cognitive architecture; the VLM effect may reflect training-data structure, tokenization, or something else entirely. Naledi correctly labels the extension to VLMs as IE — but then the prose argues beyond what IE warrants. The sentence should read more like: "The parallel is suggestive and worth investigating, but the mechanisms may differ and we should not assume they are the same."\n\n**2. The critique of slide paradigms (end of Section 3) is asserted but not argued.**\n\nThe brief states that spatial contiguity and split-attention effects "are directly violated by the standard slide paradigm" — and then the section appears to end before completing the argument. Like Kenji's brief, this one is cut off. So I'm evaluating an incomplete document, which limits what I can fairly assess. What I can say is that the premise — slides necessarily violate these principles — is overstated. Slides can place labels adjacent to referents; good information design in slide format often does exactly this. The violation is contingent on *bad* slide design, not intrinsic to the medium. A stronger argument would be that slide-based formats create *structural pressures* toward these violations (fixed canvas, no temporal control, template inertia) without claiming they're definitionally committed to them.\n\n**3. "Germane load" is contested and Naledi presents it as settled.**\n\nThe tripartite CLT framework (intrinsic/extraneous/germane) is standard, but the germane load construct has faced significant criticism in the cognitive load literature (see Kalyuga, 2011; Sweller et al., 2019 revised their own framework). Naledi's summary — "that's where comprehension actually happens" — is pedagogically intuitive but theoretically imprecise. This doesn't undermine her main argument, but if the brief is going to invoke CLT as theoretical spine, it should acknowledge that the germane load component is the contested part of it.\n\n---\n\n## III. Mateo's Prototype Rationale\n\nI want to be precise here: I am reviewing the *rationale* as submitted, not a live prototype. My assessment is therefore of whether the design choices	2026-07-20 09:05:49.791284	2026-07-20 09:05:49.791284
21	ingrid	landscape_scan_review	# Review: Kenji Ochiai — Literature Brief, AI Slide/Visualization Landscape\n**Reviewer:** Dr. Ingrid Solberg\n**Date:** Current sprint\n**Status:** REVISE before forwarding to Sophie\n\n---\n\n## OVERALL ASSESSMENT\n\nThis is competent desk research — the framing is sharp, the cluster taxonomy is useful, and the cross-cutting synthesis is largely justified. Kenji has done genuine work here. However, there are recurring methodological problems that need fixing before this brief influences sprint decisions: ceiling claims are intermittently sourced from marketing copy rather than independent evidence, one flag item is significantly undersold, and the synthesis makes one claim that outruns the underlying entries. These are correctable. Here is the full accounting.\n\n---\n\n## PER-TOOL SPECULATION FLAGS\n\nI'm keeping these brief — one or two sentences each — because the bigger problems are in the synthesis and flag items.\n\n**Gamma:** The "Liquid Canvas" rebuttal is sound, but the claim that "the communication model remains linear-sequential with no mechanism for encoding relationships spatially" goes beyond what reviewer sources can establish. Marketing copy and review articles assess user experience, not the underlying architecture. Flag this sentence as inference from observed output, not architectural fact.\n\n**Tome:** The pivot-as-data-point framing is legitimate and well-argued. No issue.\n\n**Beautiful.ai:** The quoted evidence actually supports the ceiling claim cleanly. Solid.\n\n**Microsoft Copilot:** "The AI cannot reason about narrative arc" — this is stated as a structural fact but is sourced from user-experience reports about formatting and editing friction. Those reports support "it doesn't do this well in practice," not "it is architecturally incapable." Tighten the language.\n\n**Canva:** The animation-ceiling and PPTX-export claims are well-sourced. The sentence "Canva generates within templates; it does not generate new communication structures" is a reasonable inference but is stated too categorically given it's drawn from design reviews, not system architecture documentation.\n\n**Prezi:** "There is no audience-responsive routing, no semantic relationship encoding, and no dynamic reconfiguration" — the first clause is verifiable from product documentation; the second two read as extrapolated inference. Sourcing here should be flagged as partial.\n\n**Napkin AI:** Ceiling claim is well-supported and appropriately scoped. No issue.\n\n**Visme, Pitch, Plus AI, Decktopus, SlidesAI, MagicSlides, Slidebean:** All ceiling characterizations are adequately supported by quoted reviewer language. The claims stay close to the evidence. Fine.\n\n**Prezent AI:** The characterization of Astrid as encoding "persuasion logic, not just layout" is sourced primarily from Prezent's own materials, given the comparative quote appears to come from Prezent's blog. This is the vendor describing their own product. Kenji should flag this explicitly — it's the one tool where a ceiling claim partially rests on self-reported capability rather than independent reviewer assessment. That matters because Prezent is positioned as the most narratively sophisticated tool, which influences the synthesis.\n\n**Genially:** "The AI generates visual components, not communication logic" is stated confidently, but the AI Builder is described as still under active development. Kenji cannot reliably characterize the ceiling of a feature that vendors themselves describe as incomplete. The claim may be correct, but it should be marked explicitly as provisional.\n\n**Whimsical, Miro:** Correct to note these are author/thinking tools, not audience-communication tools. The distinction is well-drawn.\n\n**Flourish:** The claim that "generation intelligence is essentially absent" rests on a description of the template-based interface. Given Canva's acquisition and ongoing AI integration across their platform, this could be outdated within the brief's own declared review window. Should be flagged as potentially stale.\n\n**Adobe Express:** Ceiling claim is fair and well-evidenced.\n\n---\n\n## CROSS-CUTTING SYNTHESIS: IS IT SUPPORTED?\n\nThe three ceiling patterns are stated as universal findings across 20 tools. Let me test each.\n\n**Ceiling 1 — "The rectangular sequential container is essentially universal."**\nSupported, with one qualification Kenji himself introduces and then doesn't fully reckon with: Genially. If Genially genuinely supports non-linear navigation, branching paths, and hotspot-triggered content (all asserted in entry 11), then "essentially universal" is doing a lot of work. The synthesis should acknowledge Genially as a partial exception rather than quietly absorbing it. As written, the synthesis reads more sweeping than the underlying evidence justifies.\n\n**Ceiling 2 — "AI is applied to production, not to communication reasoning."**\nWell-supported across the 20 entries, with Prezent as the partial counterexample (it attempts audience-role and argument-structure modeling). Kenji does note this in the Prezent entry but the synthesis treats the ceiling as total. Should say "with Prezent as the closest exception, though still producing fixed sequential output."\n\n**Ceiling 3 — "The non-linear experiments lack AI depth."**\nWell-supported. Prezi, Genially, Miro, Whimsical, and Flourish are all characterized as having thin AI generation relative to their spatial/non-linear affordances. This holds across the entries. No overreach here.\n\n**Net verdict on synthesis:** Two of three ceiling claims need one-sentence qualification rather than wholesale revision. The framework is sound; the absolutism is not fully earned.\n\n---\n\n## THE THREE FLAG ITEMS: SCRUTINY\n\nThis is where I have the most substantive concern with Kenji's brief.\n\n### Genially — significantly undersold\n\nKenji flags Genially for follow-up and says "do not go deep now" — but the description in entry 11 already gives us enough to recognize this as the tool most relevant to our sprint question. The brief characterizes it as having weak AI but strong output paradigm, then parks it as a future consideration. That framing is backwards for our purposes.\n\nThe problem is that Kenji conflates *current AI generation capability* with *output paradigm relevance*. Our sprint question is where do existing tools hit the ceiling we're trying to get past. Genially's ceiling is a weak AI layer on top of an otherwise interactive, non-linear, audience-navigable output format. That is *precisely* the ceiling that defines our design space — not a reason to defer it, but a reason to study it now. If Genially has already solved the output paradigm problem (branching, hotspots, non-linear navigation, live audience interaction) and only lacks AI-generated communication structure, then the gap between Genially's current state and what Altair8 is building is conceptually narrow. That proximity is strategic information, not a footnote.\n\nAdditionally: the brief says Genially's AI Builder "is not the right fit for teams needing customer-facing outputs" — but this is quoted from a Miro review, applied to Miro, and appears to have been misattributed or misapplied to Genially in context. Kenji should verify whether this quote is actually about Genially.\n\n**Verdict on Genially flag:** The "do not go deep now" recommendation is poorly justified and potentially wrong. Kenji should revise to recommend immediate follow-up.\n\n### Flourish — correctly flagged, one gap\n\nThe Flourish flag is reasonable — the scrollytelling paradigm is genuinely closer to our target output than anything else in the survey. The flag correctly identifies the AI narrative-structure question as the key unknown. One gap: Kenji does not mention that Flourish's SDK (which he cites in the entry) could be directly relevant to whether we build on existing infrastructure rather than from scratch. The flag should explicitly direct follow-up to evaluate the SDK as a potential build-on-top candidate, not just a competitive reference.\n\n**Verdict on Flourish flag:** Justified, but incomplete. Needs SDK evaluation added.\n\n### Prezi — overclaimed relevance, underclaimed risk\n\nKenji recommends flagging Prezi for "competitive monitoring" on the basis that it has 10+ years of user-behavior data on non-linear navigation and a proven audience for the zooming-canvas paradigm. Both claims are reasonable. However, the flag frames Prezi as a competitive threat in our direction — "if they were to invest in a genuine AI generation layer, they would be building in exactly our direction."\n\nTwo problems. First, this is pure speculation with no sourcing — there is no evidence in the brief that Prezi is investing in this direction, and 10+ years without doing so is itself evidence against it. Second, and more importantly: the brief already documents that Prezi's zooming-canvas paradigm produces motion fatigue and distraction when overused — an intrinsic user-experience problem with their specific non-linear mechanism, not just an execution gap. If their approach has a known UX ceiling independent of AI depth, monitoring them as a near-competitor may overstate the threat.\n\n**Verdict on Prezi flag:** Justification is partially speculative. Should be reframed as "watch but low probability of near-term competitive relevance given no evidence of AI investment and known UX problems with core paradigm."\n\n---\n\n## REVISION INSTRUCTIONS FOR KENJI\n\nThese are specific and directly actionable:\n\n1. **Epistemic flagging on architectural claims.** Anywhere you say a tool "cannot" reason about narrative arc, audience, or communication structure — and your source is a user review or design blog, not architecture documentation — change the language to "does not, in observable output" or "no evidence of capacity for." Apply this to: Gamma (linear-sequential claim), Copilot (narrative arc claim), Canva (communication structures claim), Prezi (semantic relationship encoding claim), and Genially (communication logic claim). This is a one-word-or-phrase change per instance, not a rewrite.\n\n2. **Flag Prezent's sourcing explicitly.** Add a parenthetical in the Prezent entry noting that the capability description of Astrid derives substantially from Prezent's own materials and should be treated as vendor-reported until independently verified. This is especially important because you use Prezent as the "most sophisticated narrative AI" benchmark in the synthesis.\n\n3. **Qualify the synthesis ceiling claims.** In Ceiling 1, replace "essentially universal" with "nearly universal, with Genially as the partial structural exception discussed in the flag section." In Ceiling 2, add one sentence: "Prezent represents the closest exception, encoding audience-role and argument-structure logic, though still producing fixed sequential output." Do not restructure the synthesis — these are insertions only.\n\n4. **Upgrade the Genially flag urgency.** Change "Do not go deep now" to "Recommend immediate follow-up this sprint." Add the specific reason: if Genially has already implemented non-linear, audience-navigable, branching output format and only lacks AI-generated communication structure, then it defines the boundary of the existing solution space most precisely — which is exactly what this sprint is trying to establish. Also verify whether the Miro-attributed quote about customer-facing outputs was incorrectly applied to Genially, and correct if so.\n\n5. **Add SDK evaluation to the Flourish flag.** In the Flourish flag item, add one sentence directing follow-up to assess the Flourish SDK specifically as a potential build-on-top or build-alongside candidate, not only as competitive context.\n\n6. **Reframe the Prezi flag.** Replace the current framing ("flag for competitive monitoring" with implied near-term threat) with: "Low-priority watch item. No evidence of AI generation investment. Core zooming paradigm has documented UX limitations independent of AI depth. Re-evaluate only if credible evidence of AI strategy pivot emerges."\n\n7. **Add a staleness flag to the Flourish ceiling characterization.** In entry 19, add one sentence noting that the "essentially absent" AI characterization may be outdated given Canva's active platform integration and the brief's own acknowledged review window.\n\nNone of these require new research — they are language calibration and framing corrections on material Kenji already has. The underlying work is solid enough to proceed to Sophie after revision.\n\n---\n\n## RECOMMENDATION TO SOPHIE\n\n**REVISE** — do not forward in current form.\n\nThe brief is substantively useful and the landscape framing will serve the team well. The revisions required are not minor cosmetic edits: the Genially flag misclassification could cause a sprint planning error if acted upon as written (deferring the most relevant tool), and the uncalibrated architectural-ceiling language undermines the epistemic credibility of the synthesis for anyone who reads carefully. Neither problem requires new research. Kenji should be able to implement all seven revision instructions within a half-day and resubmit for a lighter second-pass review.	2026-07-20 10:10:17.699933	2026-07-20 10:12:46.680957
24	kenji	landscape_scan_v1	Now I have enough data to compile the comprehensive brief. Let me write it up.\nI now have sufficient depth across all tools. Let me write the comprehensive brief.\n\n---\n\n# Literature Brief — AI-Generated Slide/Visualization Tools: Landscape & Ceiling Analysis\n\n**Prepared by:** Kenji Ochiai, Researcher, Altair8\n**Sprint question:** What does the current landscape of AI-generated slide/visualization tools look like, and where do they visibly hit the ceiling we're trying to get past?\n**Method:** Web search synthesis, April–June 2026 review articles, hands-on tester reports, vendor documentation. 20 tools covered. Confidence is moderate-high for ceiling characterizations; lower for any specific pricing claims (not the focus here).\n\n---\n\n## FRAMING NOTE\n\nThe tools below sort roughly into three clusters: **(A) AI slide factories** — fast text-to-deck pipelines that automate the blank-slide problem but stay strictly rectangular; **(B) AI-assisted design/brand systems** — tools that layer intelligence onto template management or brand compliance; **(C) visual/spatial alternatives** — tools that at least partially abandon the slide metaphor. The ceiling we're probing appears consistently across all three clusters, though it manifests differently in each.\n\n---\n\n## THE 20 TOOLS\n\n---\n\n### 1. Gamma\n**What it does:** Gamma generates presentations, documents, and web pages from a text prompt or uploaded file. \nIt helps users create slide decks and websites with a short prompt, or you can upload a file or paste content to provide source material.\n Output is web-native — delivered as a link rather than a .pptx file. The generation pipeline streams content into view in real time, applying consistent theming across layouts automatically.\n\n**Where it hits the ceiling:** Gamma's self-described innovation — a "Liquid Canvas" that abandons the rigid 16:9 box — is more marketing than structural reality. In practice every card is still a fixed-width rectangle arrayed vertically; you scroll rather than interact. \nIt trades the granular control and compatibility of legacy software for incredible speed, ease of use, and a modern, web-first output — but export limitations are a significant drawback for some workflows.\n More fundamentally, the communication model remains linear-sequential: one card follows the next. There is no audience-adaptive routing, no non-linear traversal, and no mechanism for encoding relationships between ideas spatially. The AI is a layout engine, not a communication-structure engine.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Tome *(pivoted — legacy reference)*\n**What it does:** Originally an AI storytelling and presentation tool that reached 20 million users via a tile-based content system generated from prompts. \nPreviously an AI-powered presentation tool, Tome pivoted to sales automation in late 2024 and announced the sunset of its presentation features in March 2025.\n It now operates as an AI sales assistant for enterprise go-to-market teams.\n\n**Where it hit the ceiling:** Tome's failure is itself a data point. \nThe tile-based system, while innovative, proved incompatible with traditional business workflows.\n The pivot strongly suggests that a novel visual format — even one with genuine AI generation — could not sustain a product without either solving deeper communication problems or anchoring to an established workflow. Its fate is a signal that novelty of container shape is insufficient on its own.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Beautiful.ai\n**What it does:** Beautiful.ai uses a "Smart Slide" system — a proprietary constraint engine that auto-applies layout rules as you add content. AI generates a draft deck from a prompt; the Smart Slide logic then governs where elements land on each slide and adjusts automatically as content changes.\n\n**Where it hits the ceiling:** \nYou cannot freely drag elements to arbitrary positions on the slide. The Smart Slide system decides where things go based on its internal rules, and while those rules usually produce clean results, they can feel rigid when you have a specific layout in mind.\n The deeper problem is that the intelligence is entirely layout-level: the AI doesn't understand communication intent, audience, or argument structure. \nBeautiful.ai's AI helps with drafts and layout but doesn't research, iterate conversationally, or generate deeply contextual content.\n Every output is still a sequential rectangular deck. \nNot well-suited for complex, data-heavy presentations: the structured templates don't always support layered data and nuanced storytelling.\n\n\n---\n\n### 4. Microsoft Copilot in PowerPoint\n**What it does:** Copilot is embedded directly in the PowerPoint interface, generating slides from prompts, summarizing existing decks, suggesting design improvements, and drafting speaker notes — all within the familiar Office environment and file format.\n\n**Where it hits the ceiling:** \nCopilot works best when converting existing content into slides, not when creating presentations from scratch. Formatting, branding, and storytelling still require significant manual effort.\n The structural ceiling is PowerPoint itself: the output is always a .pptx with rectangular slides. Beyond that, \ndespite the "Agent Mode" branding, Copilot still struggles with iterative editing. Users consistently report that asking Copilot to modify existing slides produces vague suggestions rather than direct changes.\n The AI cannot reason about narrative arc, communication strategy, or the relationship between ideas — it can only populate and reformat boxes.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Canva (Magic Design / Magic Media)\n**What it does:** Canva's AI suite — Magic Design, Magic Write, Magic Media, and the integrated Flourish data layer — lets users generate full slide decks from prompts, produce images from text, and animate presentations automatically. It operates within Canva's template and asset ecosystem.\n\n**Where it hits the ceiling:** \nAnimation sophistication is a gap. Magic Animate applies smooth, coordinated transitions, but the library of available effects is shallow. For storytelling through animation — a product demo walkthrough or a timeline with sequential data reveals — you will hit the ceiling quickly.\n On the generative side, \nif you care about illustration style, brand-specific iconography, or consistent visual systems, the outputs are serviceable rather than distinctive.\n Canva generates *within* templates; it does not generate new communication structures. Every output is still a 16:9 grid of boxes, and \nthe PPTX export frequently introduces layout shifts when opened in Microsoft PowerPoint — grouped elements ungroup, text boxes reposition, and animations disappear.\n\n\n---\n\n### 6. Prezi\n**What it does:** Prezi uses a zoomable, non-linear canvas instead of sequential slides. \nIt structures content as topics and subtopics on a large canvas, letting you zoom into sections as the story unfolds.\n AI can generate a canvas structure from a text prompt, arranging content into "Planet Topics" and "Page Topics" spatially.\n\n**Where it hits the ceiling:** Prezi is the closest existing tool to a genuinely non-linear paradigm, but its AI layer is thin. \nThe AI feels bolted on — nudges and suggestions, not true AI generation like newer tools.\n The canvas is author-defined and static at runtime: the presenter zooms along a pre-scripted path. There is no audience-responsive routing, no semantic relationship encoding, and no dynamic reconfiguration based on context. \nZooming effect can cause motion fatigue or distraction if overused, and collaboration features lag behind competitors.\n The non-linear *form* is there; the non-linear *intelligence* is not.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Napkin AI\n**What it does:** Napkin takes written text (notes, paragraphs, documents) and converts it into visual diagrams, flowcharts, and concept graphics automatically — no prompt engineering required. It focuses specifically on the text-to-visual transformation, not full deck generation.\n\n**Where it hits the ceiling:** \nNapkin AI is useful for turning written ideas into quick visuals, but it is not a complete replacement for dedicated diagramming, presentation, or design software. Its main limitation is control: the platform is built around fast AI-generated structure, so users who need pixel-level layout precision, strict brand systems, or complex visual customization may find the editing experience too constrained.\n Napkin generates individual visuals, not communication systems. There is no concept of audience, sequence, or narrative logic. \nNapkin AI is also not a full design suite. You can't do pixel-perfect brand design, complex illustrations, or detailed collaborative design work.\n The output is a static image or diagram — still fundamentally a box to be dropped into another box (a slide).\n\n---\n\n### 8. Visme\n**What it does:** Visme is a broad visual-content platform covering presentations, infographics, reports, and interactive documents. Its AI tools include a slide generator (from prompts or uploaded docs), an AI image generator, and an AI data visualization layer that connects to live data sources.\n\n**Where it hits the ceiling:** \nAI slide generation in Visme mainly applies a template format and distributes text across slides. You still have to manually refine the structure to make the presentation flow logically.\n The wider suite is genuinely richer than most competitors — Visme can produce interactive infographics, not just decks — but the AI layer across all formats is shallow: it picks a template and fills it. \nIf you need help tailoring presentations for different audiences or maintaining strict brand consistency, those decisions still need to be handled manually.\n The interactivity in Visme's richer formats is author-scripted, not AI-reasoned.\n\n---\n\n### 9. Pitch\n**What it does:** Pitch is a team-first presentation platform with an AI "Pitch Agent" that generates on-brand slides, personalizes messaging, and enhances visuals. It emphasizes real-time collaboration, version tracking, and presentation analytics (who viewed, for how long).\n\n**Where it hits the ceiling:** Pitch's ceiling is structural rather than cosmetic — it is a very well-executed slide tool, but it remains entirely a slide tool. \nPitch is not trying to be a pure traditional PowerPoint replacement, but its best experience is clearly inside its own platform. Teams deeply attached to native PowerPoint or Google Slides editing will face friction.\n The AI generates slides, not communication strategies. Audience analytics tell you *whether* people engaged — they do not feed back into the content structure or suggest adaptive re-sequencing. The output format is a rectangular sequential deck with better collaboration tooling around it.\n\n---\n\n### 10. Plus AI\n**What it does:** Plus AI is a native add-on for PowerPoint and Google Slides that generates presentations directly inside those apps, producing fully compatible PPTX and Google Slides files. It emphasizes brand-template fidelity and includes a "Remix" tool for converting documents into decks.\n\n**Where it hits the ceiling:** The design philosophy of Plus AI is explicit about its constraint: it works *within* PowerPoint and Google Slides, not beyond them. \nUnlike other AI presentation tools, it creates native PPTX files that are fully compatible with existing slide templates and workflows.\n This is a strength for enterprise compatibility and a hard ceiling for innovation — the output is definitionally a rectangular sequential deck. \nContent quality for highly technical or specialized topics reveals the limitations of the underlying language models, producing generic output requiring substantial editing for complex presentations.\n Plus AI solves the *production* problem for known formats; it does not touch the *communication structure* problem.\n\n---\n\n### 11. Genially\n**What it does:** Genially is an interactive-content platform for creating presentations, infographics, gamified experiences, quizzes, and interactive maps — all with embedded hotspots, animations, and non-linear navigation paths. Its AI Builder generates interactive components from prompts.\n\n**Where it hits the ceiling:** Genially is the most interactive tool in this survey — it supports genuine non-linear navigation, hotspots, and audience-response triggers. But its AI generation layer is explicitly immature. \nGenially AI Builder is a new feature still under development; current limitations include downloads, translation, accessibility compliance, and the option to save components you've generated.\n More structurally: the interactivity is author-scripted at creation time. The AI generates visual components, not communication logic. There is no mechanism for the content to adapt to audience behavior at runtime, and the platform's core abstraction remains a fixed canvas with hand-placed interactive elements. Depth of interactivity is ahead of peers; depth of AI reasoning about communication is not.\n\n---\n\n### 12. Decktopus\n**What it does:** Decktopus is a fast, minimal AI deck generator: you type a title or prompt, the AI selects layouts, color schemes, and stock imagery, and produces a complete slide deck in under five minutes. It also supports embedded forms and microsite-style sharing.\n\n**Where it hits the ceiling:** Decktopus occupies the most commoditized position in the market. \nTemplates frequently lack true differentiation. The AI-generated texts require manual customization for persuasive pitches or industry-specific content. The AI can't intuit brand personality. Design flexibility restrictions mean Decktopus struggles with highly technical layouts or custom infographics — the output is basic, not bespoke.\n There is no narrative intelligence, no audience modeling, and no structure beyond the sequential slide. It solves the blank-slide problem at its cheapest form.\n\n---\n\n### 13. Prezent AI\n**What it does:** Prezent is an enterprise presentation platform built around its proprietary "Astrid" AI, trained on business communication frameworks. It generates decks structured around audience role, industry vertical, and business function — aiming to encode persuasion logic, not just layout.\n\n**Where it hits the ceiling:** Prezent is arguably the most sophisticated in terms of *narrative* AI — it attempts to reason about argument structure. But the output remains a static sequential slide deck, and the intelligence is locked to pre-trained business frameworks. \nPrezi took a more visual, non-linear approach and leaned into storytelling rather than the structured 10-slide format — the trade-off was that it didn't follow the prompt as closely. Prezent, in contrast, applies proven business communication frameworks so the deck is structured around how the audience thinks.\n The ceiling: Prezent can improve the *narrative sequence* of a rectangular deck but cannot break out of that container. And its framework-driven AI risks rigidity — the communication structures it knows are the ones it was trained on.\n\n---\n\n### 14. Slidebean\n**What it does:** Slidebean is an AI-assisted pitch-deck builder targeting startups. It pairs AI content generation with auto-design that applies visual styling automatically. It also offers human design services as an upsell for high-stakes decks.\n\n**Where it hits the ceiling:** \nIf your deck needs to reflect your unique brand voice and style, Slidebean may disappoint. The templates lock you into rigid design choices — a serious problem when you're trying to impress.\n Slidebean is essentially a domain-specialized (pitch decks) version of the same AI-fills-templates paradigm. The ceiling is identical to the broader category: it automates visual production within a fixed rectangular sequential format, with no mechanism for dynamic communication or non-linear structure.\n\n---\n\n### 15. SlidesAI\n**What it does:** SlidesAI is a Google Slides add-on that converts raw text into structured slide presentations natively within Google Slides. You paste or import text; the AI chunks it into slides, applies a theme, and formats layouts inside your existing Google Slides workflow.\n\n**Where it hits the ceiling:** SlidesAI is the most workflow-embedded tool in the survey, but also the most constrained. \nThe output isn't super fancy; once generated, you edit using AI in the sidebar, updating text, generating new images, or anything else you need to tweak.\n The tool's entire purpose is to automate *slide production within an existing slide format*. There is no spatial reasoning, no communication-structure modeling, and no non-linearity. It is the rectangular sequential slide at its most undisguised.\n\n---\n\n### 16. MagicSlides\n**What it does:** MagicSlides is a Google Slides and PowerPoint add-on that generates full presentations from text prompts, YouTube URLs, or uploaded documents. It supports a 12,000-character input window, allowing relatively detailed source material.\n\n**Where it hits the ceiling:** \nThis limitation is common in AI slide generators: users have specifically pointed out "limited customization" as a recurring con for MagicSlides.\n Like SlidesAI, MagicSlides is an automation layer over an existing slide format. The AI input-to-output pipeline is longer (it can ingest video URLs, for example), but the output is identical: a rectangular sequential deck. The richer input-handling is a genuine convenience; it does not address the communication-structure ceiling at all.\n\n---\n\n### 17. Whimsical AI\n**What it does:** Whimsical is a visual workspace for flowcharts, wireframes, mind maps, and sticky-note boards. Its AI layer generates these diagrams and maps from natural language descriptions, allowing rapid structural ideation for system design, user flows, and project planning.\n\n**Where it hits the ceiling:** Whimsical is genuinely diagrammatic — it escapes the slide metaphor — but it is not a communication tool for audiences; it is a thinking tool for authors. \nKey limitations include: AI occasionally misinterprets complex conditional logic, only 3–4 font sizes are available with no custom CSS, and wireframe component options are restricted.\n The output cannot be *presented* in the usual sense; it's a working artifact, not a shareable communication format. There is no narrative layer, no sequencing logic, and no mechanism for connecting the diagram to an argument for an audience.\n\n---\n\n### 18. Miro AI (Miro Assist)\n**What it does:** Miro is an infinite digital whiteboard platform; Miro AI generates sticky-note clusters, mind maps, diagrams, and summaries from prompts within the board. Teams use it for brainstorming, project planning, and diagrammatic documentation.\n\n**Where it hits the ceiling:** \nMiro AI pretty much only works with what's on the Miro board — and that's both its biggest strength and its biggest limitation.\n Like Whimsical, Miro AI is a thinking/planning tool, not a communication-to-audience tool. The infinite canvas is liberating for internal ideation but structurally unsuitable for audience-facing communication: there is no narrative arc, no sequencing, no way to guide an audience through a visual argument. \nWhile Miro AI is a clever creative tool, its focus on the visual board means it's not the right fit for teams needing customer-facing outputs.\n\n\n---\n\n### 19. Flourish (now Canva)\n**What it does:** Flourish is a data-visualization and scrollytelling platform, acquired by Canva in 2022. It specializes in interactive charts, animated data stories, and narrative visualizations (scrollytelling, audio-driven stories) for web publication. Non-technical users build from templates; the Flourish SDK allows custom template creation.\n\n**Where it hits the ceiling:** Flourish is the only tool in this survey specifically designed for *narrative data visualization* and scrollytelling — genuinely a different paradigm from slides. But the AI layer is minimal: \nFlourish is uniquely designed to facilitate narrative visualizations such as scrollytelling and interactive presentations, but its strength is the template-based system and intuitive interface, not AI generation of structure or narrative logic.\n You build the narrative; the tool renders it interactively. This is closer to where we want to go in terms of output format, but the generation intelligence is essentially absent — a human still scripts every transition, data bind, and narrative beat.\n\n---\n\n### 20. Adobe Express\n**What it does:** Adobe Express (formerly Adobe Spark) is a rapid design platform for presentations, social graphics, video, and web content. Its AI tools include text-to-image generation (Firefly), AI-suggested layouts, and automated template customization.\n\n**Where it hits the ceiling:** Adobe Express's AI is primarily a *design-quality automation* layer — it makes template selection and image generation faster. \nAs a seasoned designer, Adobe Express would not be used for client branding projects, publication layouts, or anything requiring high-end production — some lag with larger files or when using advanced features.\n For presentations specifically, the output is a conventional rectangular sequential deck. Adobe's Firefly image generation is strong relative to peers, but the communication architecture is identical to the rest of the market: the AI fills boxes, it does not reason about structure, relationship, or audience. The creative ceiling is higher than most; the structural ceiling is the same.\n\n---\n\n## CROSS-CUTTING SYNTHESIS\n\nThree ceiling patterns appear consistently across all 20 tools:\n\n1. **The rectangular sequential container is essentially universal.** Every tool — including the ones that claim to go "beyond slides" — produces output that is either a deck of 16:9 rectangles or a single static canvas with author-scripted navigation. None of them generate *communication structures* that adapt at runtime to audience behavior, prior knowledge, or context.\n\n2. **AI is applied to production, not to communication reasoning.** The state of the art is: AI picks a template, fills it with content, and applies visual styling. The most advanced tools (Prezent, Pitch) begin to encode narrative logic and audience role — but still output a fixed pre-scripted artifact. No tool closes the loop between audience engagement data and content structure.\n\n3. **The non-linear experiments lack AI depth.** The tools that escape the sequential slide (Prezi, Genially, Miro, Whimsical, Flourish) are either author-scripted spatial canvases with thin AI generation, or thinking/planning tools not designed for audience-facing communication. None of them combine genuine non-linearity with AI-generated communication structure.\n\n---\n\n## FLAG FOR FOLLOW-UP\n\n**Genially** — Of all tools surveyed, Genially most closely approaches what Altair8 is trying to build. It supports genuine non-linear navigation, hotspots, branching paths, gamification, and live audience interaction within the same output artifact. It goes beyond the slide container more than any other tool in widespread use. Its AI Builder layer is currently weak, but the *output paradigm* — interactive, non-linear, audience-responsive authored content — is closer to our target than anything else on this list. Do not go deep now, but it warrants a dedicated follow-up session: specifically whether its interactivity is fully author-scripted or whether any AI reasoning about navigation structure exists or is planned.\n\n**Flourish (Canva)** — Flourish's scrollytelling and narrative data-visualization format is the closest in output structure to dynamic, non-slide AI communication. Given Canva's acquisition and ongoing integration, this could scale. Worth a follow-up specifically on whether Canva/Flourish is investing in AI-generated narrative structure (not just AI-generated charts), and whether the Flourish SDK could be a build-vs.-buy consideration.\n\n**Prezi** — Despite its thin AI layer, Prezi has 10+ years of user-behavior data on non-linear navigation patterns and is the only tool with a proven audience for the zooming-canvas paradigm. If they were to invest in a genuine AI generation layer for canvas structure (not just content), they would be building in exactly our direction. Flag for competitive monitoring.\n\n---\n\n*Confidence note: Ceiling characterizations are grounded in multiple independent reviewer sources (Zapier, Prezent.ai/blog, Getalai, Storychief, Deckary, Plus AI blog, Eesel AI, and others). Tool feature sets shift frequently — this brief reflects the state as of mid-2025/early-2026 review cycles. Any flag-for-follow-up items should be re-checked against current product pages before sprint planning.*	2026-07-20 10:13:23.809812	2026-07-20 10:13:23.809812
