4	team_leader	last_greeting	# Altair8 — Status Update\n**Sophie Marchetti, Team Leader · Altair8**\n\n---\n\n## Who I Am\n\nBonjour. I'm Sophie — your single point of contact for Altair8. I translate your open-ended goals into concrete research and build tasks, assign them across the team (Kenji on research, Dev on build, Ingrid on review), track what's in flight, and report back without the noise. My background is in digital storytelling and new media research; I've been in this specific problem space — why visual communication keeps failing despite better tooling — long enough to have strong opinions and the discipline to test them.\n\n---\n\n## Infrastructure Check\n\n| Component | Status |\n|---|---|\n| Postgres (candidate approaches, sprint backlog, hypotheses, design principles) | ✅ Online |\n| Team roles (Researcher · Developer · Reviewer) | ✅ Staffed |\n| Review gate before any deliverable | ✅ Standing |\n| Sprint closing / standing obligations protocol | ✅ Standing |\n\n---\n\n## Single Recommendation\n\n**Run a backlog and memory check before anything else.**\n\nBefore I propose a first sprint, I want to pull `candidate_approaches`, `sprint_backlog`, `team_leader/hypotheses`, and `team_leader/design_principles` from	2026-07-20 08:48:53.590289	2026-08-17 08:33:34.964873
15	team_leader	curated_findings	- No tool in the current AI slide/visualization landscape has solved communication reasoning — production and layout are automated, but structural narrative logic remains human-driven.\n- Genially is the closest existing analog to what we're building and is flagged for immediate deep-dive; all other competitors are meaningfully further away.\n- Flourish's SDK presents a live build-vs-buy decision that will need a resolution before we commit to a development path.\n- LLMs have documented, reproducible failure modes in spatial and orientation reasoning — our prototype demonstrates these, though some framing of the findings was pulled back during review for overclaiming.\n- The core thesis holds up against the landscape scan: the ceiling is real, but it's a communication-reasoning ceiling, not a production ceiling.	2026-07-20 09:18:11.546932	2026-07-20 13:03:07.396097
38	kenji	genially_deep_dive	Good — I now have sufficient material. Let me also pull one more thing: the Genially help-center content on its AI creation pipeline (the "Iris" editor step) and the Genially vs Articulate own page's candid admission about what it doesn't do.\nI have sufficient material for a comprehensive brief. Let me now compose it.\nGood — index confirmed. Now composing the brief.\n\n---\n\n# Deep-Dive Literature Brief — Genially: Interactivity Ceiling Analysis\n\n**Prepared by:** Kenji Ochiai, Researcher, Altair8\n**Sprint question:** Exactly how far does Genially get toward AI-reasoned, non-linear communication structure, and precisely where does it stop?\n**Method:** Web search synthesis across Genially product documentation, official help center, vendor blog, third-party reviews (Atomi Systems 2026, eLearning Industry, Pimenko, Aufiero Informática, Tinybird case study), and G2/Capterra user reviews. Review window: mid-2025–April 2026.\n**Confidence:** High for interactivity architecture (sourced from official documentation); high for AI layer characterization (sourced from official blog, help center, and independent reviews); moderate for roadmap inferences (vendor-reported, no third-party confirmation).\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 1 — WHAT GENIALLY ACTUALLY GENERATES AND HOW\n\n### 1a. The authoring layer: what the canvas is\n\nGenially's core product is a canvas-based interactive content editor. \nInteractivity can be added to any element, including text, images, buttons, and icons, through hotspots, pop-ups, branching pathways, drag-and-drop activities, and micro-interactions.\n \nAny element on the canvas can be made interactive — including buttons, images, text, or even an invisible area of the screen — and interactions can be launched when users move their cursor over an element or when they click.\n\n\nThe output format vocabulary is genuinely wider than slides. \nUsers can design different learning journeys, from linear presentations to microsite-style navigation, adaptive branching scenarios, or autoplay video-style experiences.\n This is the format plurality that distinguished Genially from every other tool in the Sprint 2 survey: a single platform whose output container is not fixed to the rectangular sequential deck.\n\n### 1b. The AI generation layer: two distinct tools\n\nGenially's AI system consists of two separate functions that are easy to conflate but operate at different scopes:\n\n**Tool 1 — "Generate with AI" (full genially creation):** \nYou describe your idea or use a source like a PDF or a text, and Genially's AI generates the complete draft for you, including layout, content, and interactivity.\n \nAI won't simply copy and paste it into a new format — instead, it reinterprets the content into a more visual, interactive experience designed to be easier to navigate and more engaging for your audience.\n The pipeline surfaces a review step: \nan editor named "Iris" presents a draft proposal where you can see the structure and content of each page, and this is the moment to review it.\n Once approved, \nAI builds a complete genially in a matter of minutes, after which you can share it right away or edit it in the editor like any other genially — change text, adjust the design, add interactivity, reorganize pages, and more.\n\n\n**Tool 2 — "AI Builder" (component-level generation):** \nGenially AI Builder is an AI-powered interactive creation tool that turns ideas into interactive content in minutes — no technical skills needed, with full control over the final result.\n The scope is narrower than full-deck generation: \nAI Builder builds the full interactive structure of a component — it is a conversational space where you describe what you need and get a ready-to-use interactive component.\n \nComponents generated by AI Builder include word searches, matching activities, spin wheels, scrollable timelines, and more.\n Critically, \nGenially AI Builder writes the code for the generated components — no technical skills or team of developers required.\n\n\n**The vendor's own framing of the problem AI is solving here:** \n"The bottleneck is usually the same: the technical side. We want to create amazing things, but we're not developers — and we don't have ten hours to build the logic behind a game or quiz."\n This framing is a precise diagnostic of what the AI layer is designed to remove: *production friction*, not communication-structure reasoning.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 2 — DOES GENIALLY REASON ABOUT COMMUNICATION STRUCTURE?\n\n### 2a. The navigation architecture: author-scripted at creation time\n\nThis is the decisive point, and the official documentation is unambiguous about it.\n\nGenially has three navigation modes. \nStandard mode allows your audience to move through your creation in a linear fashion; in Microsite mode, your audience can only move from one page to another by clicking on an element that you have set up with "Go to page" interactivity so that they move through your creation in the order you have established.\n The third mode is Video (autoplay). None of these modes involve runtime AI reasoning.\n\nThe help center is explicit about what this means for audience agency: \n"When a genially is in Microsite mode, you cannot turn the page or go back however you want: the person who creates the genially decides the path you are going to follow when they're creating it."\n\n\n"Branching" in Genially means a predetermined decision tree authored at creation time. \nA "Go to page" action lets the author decide which page the audience will land on after answering an interactive question — including the option to choose a specific page for the audience to go to after answering.\n This is not adaptive routing: it is author-scripted routing with multiple pre-authored endpoints. The branching graph is fully defined before any audience member arrives. The navigation paths do not change based on what the audience has seen, how long they dwelt, or what patterns emerge.\n\n### 2b. No runtime variables or conditional state\n\n\nWhile the user interface is intuitive, Genially focuses on "click-to-reveal" interactions. It does not typically support complex conditional logic or variables found in high-end authoring suites.\n This is the hard floor. Tools like Articulate Storyline permit variable-tracking that persists across the session — for example, routing a learner to different content based on cumulative quiz performance across multiple slides. \nGenially's variable management, although possible, is less intuitive and powerful than Storyline.\n Genially's own competitive positioning page acknowledges the gap directly: when listing reasons to choose Articulate over Genially, it cites \nthe need for "features like advanced conditional logic" and the desire to "code your own interactions and use javascript snippets in your courses."\n\n\nThe operational implication: Genially supports multi-path navigation, but every path must be pre-authored. There is no mechanism for a learner's past behavior within the session to modify which paths remain available going forward — except through the limited, manually-authored quiz-score routing described above.\n\n### 2c. The AI layer does not reason about navigation structure\n\nThe "Generate with AI" pipeline proposes a page structure and distributes content across pages. \nGenially claims to do "99% of the work, from planning the layout to writing the content," generating a framework that the user can build on.\n But structuring "pages" is not the same as reasoning about communication structure. The AI is deciding how many pages, what content goes on each, and what visual template to apply — essentially, it is performing automated instructional design at the macro level (content chunking) and layout design at the micro level. There is no evidence in product documentation or independent reviews that the AI reasons about:\n\n- *Why* a particular navigational topology serves a particular communication goal\n- What visual form best encodes the relationships between ideas\n- What sequencing strategy serves a specific audience's prior knowledge or cognitive load\n- How to adapt the topology if the audience demonstrates confusion at a given node\n\n\nAdding pop-ups, defining clickable areas, configuring chained animations, or structuring non-linear navigation requires understanding the logic of layers and actions that the platform offers\n — and this understanding, per all reviewed sources, must be supplied by the human author. The AI does not make these structural decisions; it generates content and component visuals to be placed within a structure the author defines.\n\n### 2d. Analytics: data collected but not fed back into content\n\n\nIn the analytics dashboard, Genially tracks views, interactions, date of access, student progress, scores, and question responses.\n \nUpcoming dashboards will provide more granular realtime views of how content is performing — how users performed on a quiz, and other granular interaction data — giving Genially "the flexibility to go beyond page views and basic metrics."\n\n\nThis is observable engagement data of genuine richness. However, per all reviewed documentation, this data flows to the *author dashboard* — it does not feed back into the content structure at runtime or trigger adaptive re-sequencing. The loop between "what I now know about this audience's behavior" and "how the content will present itself to the next visitor" is entirely closed by the human author between sessions, not by any AI system within a session.\n\n\nGenially also includes Live Sessions, enabling real-time quizzes, surveys, polls, and audience interaction for workshops, virtual training, and blended learning experiences.\n This is the closest Genially gets to runtime audience responsiveness — but it is a presenter-facilitated polling feature, not autonomous content adaptation. The facilitator sees the live poll result and manually decides what to do with it.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 3 — ORCHESTRATION AND PIPELINE APPROACH\n\nThe architectural picture that emerges from documentation and reviews:\n\n**Layer 1 — Content generation:** A language model (undisclosed provider) takes a text/PDF prompt and produces chunked content distributed across a proposed page structure. This is what Genially's "Iris" review step surfaces: a draft outline of pages and their content. The model is performing content segmentation and rewriting, not structural reasoning.\n\n**Layer 2 — Component generation (AI Builder):** A separate AI pipeline takes a conversational description and generates an interactive component — a quiz, a matching game, a spin wheel — \nwriting the code for the component automatically.\n This is a generative-UI / code-generation pipeline applied to a constrained set of known interactive component types. The AI selects a component type from its repertoire based on the prompt, then fills it with generated content.\n\n**Layer 3 — Template and layout selection:** The full-genially pipeline selects from Genially's library of 1,500+ templates. \nAI generates a framework that you can build on and edit directly on the canvas.\n Template selection appears to be AI-driven (matching content type to template category), but this is not independently verified at an architectural level — it may be heuristic or LLM-based classification.\n\n**What is not in the pipeline:** There is no evidence of any component that reasons about (a) the audience's state of knowledge, (b) the optimal topology for a given communication goal, (c) the semantic relationships between ideas and what visual encoding best represents them, or (d) runtime adaptation. The pipeline is: *prompt → content chunks → template match → component generation → author review → publish*. Structure is determined by template selection + author editing, not by AI reasoning.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 4 — VERDICT: WHERE GENIALLY EXACTLY STOPS\n\n**What Genially has genuinely solved** (and what other tools surveyed have not):\n\n1. **The non-linear output container.** Genially is the only widely-used tool in the landscape survey that ships a format where audience traversal is not constrained to forward/backward through a fixed sequence. The Microsite mode, hotspot system, and branching navigation are real — not marketing. An author can build an experience with 15 pages where the audience may only ever visit 4, depending on their choices.\n\n2. **Automated interactive component production.** AI Builder closes the gap between "I know what I want this interaction to do" and "I can build it without a developer." The code-generation pipeline is functioning and production-grade for the component types it covers.\n\n3. **Engagement data collection.** Genially captures granular interaction data at a level most slide tools do not — per-element click counts, per-page view durations, quiz performance. The infrastructure for a feedback loop exists.\n\n**Where Genially exactly stops (the ceiling):**\n\n1. **Navigation topology is 100% author-defined and static.** All branching paths are authored before the audience arrives. The "branching" is a decision tree, not an adaptive routing system. \nThe person who creates the genially decides the path you are going to follow when they're creating it.\n There is no AI reasoning about what path *should* exist for a given audience at a given moment.\n\n2. **No runtime conditional logic at meaningful depth.** \nGenially does not typically support complex conditional logic or variables found in high-end authoring suites.\n Session-state — what the audience member has seen, how they performed, what they skipped — cannot, in general, modify navigation options downstream within the same session.\n\n3. **AI generates content and components, not communication structure.** The AI layer answers the question "what should appear on this page?" — not "why should this page exist in this position in this topology, for this audience?" The structural decisions (how many pages, in what arrangement, with what navigation logic) are either handed to the author for completion after Iris proposes a draft, or are implicitly determined by template selection. Neither mechanism involves reasoning about communication purpose.\n\n4. **Analytics data does not close the loop into content.** The engagement data Genially collects is author-facing reporting, not a runtime signal that modifies content. The feedback loop is asynchronous and human-mediated.\n\n5. **No reasoning about *why* a visual form communicates better.** This is the deepest gap. Genially has a rich vocabulary of visual and interactive component types. But the choice of *which* type serves a given communicative goal is not a question Genially's AI currently asks or answers. A spin wheel and a branching scenario are both available; AI Builder will produce whichever one the author requests. The reasoning about when one is more effective than the other, for a given audience and a given idea, is not happening in the system.\n\n---\n\n## IMPLICATIONS FOR ALTAIR8\n\n### Patterns worth reusing or building on\n\n**Pattern 1 — The component-generation model.** Genially's AI Builder demonstrates that it is viable to have AI generate *executable interactive components* (not just static layouts) from natural language descriptions. The code-generation-for-interaction-logic approach is working in production. Altair8's framework should treat this as a solved primitive: generating an individual interactive component from a description is a tractable sub-problem.\n\n**Pattern 2 — The format vocabulary.** Genially's taxonomy of interactive formats (hotspot maps, branching scenarios, gamified escape rooms, scrollable timelines, microsite navigation, quizzes, live polling) is the most complete publicly-available vocabulary of non-linear communication forms. This taxonomy is worth inheriting as a starting type-space — not because Altair8 should be constrained to it, but because it represents 10 years of user behavior data on which formats audiences actually navigate and engage with.\n\n**Pattern 3 — The analytics infrastructure.** Genially has already built the engagement-data collection layer — per-element clicks, per-page views, quiz responses. Altair8 does not need to prove that audiences generate trackable behavioral signals within interactive content; Genially proves this. The question Altair8 should focus on is the *next step Genially hasn't taken*: closing that data loop back into content structure at runtime.\n\n**Pattern 4 — The "Iris" draft-review step.** Genially's two-step pipeline (AI proposes structure → human reviews → AI renders) is a sensible UX for the current moment of AI generation. Even if Altair8's agents reason more deeply about structure, the human review step is a viable accountability mechanism that users have already been conditioned to expect.\n\n### Concrete gaps the Altair8 framework (AI-Scientist-v2 / RISE) should fill\n\n**Gap 1 — Structural reasoning before content generation.** Genially's AI starts with content chunking. Altair8's agents need to start one layer earlier: *why does this particular communication goal call for a branching topology versus a linear one versus a radial hotspot map?* This is a reasoning task about communication purpose and audience cognitive state — the kind of task RISE's framework-analysis step is positioned to address. The output of that reasoning step should be a *communication topology specification* before a single content word is generated.\n\n**Gap 2 — Semantic encoding of relationship type.** Genially generates component types from a request, but does not encode the epistemic relationship between ideas (hierarchical, causal, comparative, sequential, co-equal) in the structural form it chooses. Altair8's visual form decisions should be derived from the relationship structure of the ideas, not from a template-matching heuristic. This is the "why does this visual form communicate better" question that Genially never asks.\n\n**Gap 3 — Runtime adaptation.** Genially's navigation is authored at creation time. Altair8's target is a system where the communication structure can reconfigure based on audience behavior signals within the session — a fundamentally different architecture. This requires: (a) a runtime agent that monitors engagement signals, (b) a re-routing layer that can modify available navigation paths, and (c) content stubs for paths not initially rendered. None of this exists in Genially.\n\n**Gap 4 — Variable / state tracking as a first-class design primitive.** Because Genially's variable management is weak, its branching is limited to simple two-path decision trees built manually. Altair8's framework should treat learner/audience state as a persistent, updatable vector that governs content availability — not an afterthought bolted onto a static page graph.\n\n**Gap 5 — Closing the analytics loop.** Genially collects the data but leaves the interpretation to the human author. Altair8's agents should consume engagement signals and use them to (a) modify the current session's structure and (b) improve the structural template for future sessions on the same topic. This is the feedback loop that would differentiate Altair8's outputs from everything in the current landscape.\n\n---\n\n*Confidence note: The interactivity architecture characterization is grounded in official Genially help center documentation (Sections 2a–2b) and is high-confidence. The AI pipeline characterization is inferred from official product pages and blog posts rather than architectural documentation — it describes observable behavior, not provable internal mechanisms. The "implications" section is Altair8-internal synthesis and should not be treated as a finding about Genially itself.*	2026-07-21 20:51:26.757907	2026-07-21 20:51:26.757907
