hase contract is concrete: a JSON blob is the *only* way Ideation talks to Experimentation. There is no shared mutable memory between phases; the artifact is the handoff.\n\n**Verdict: Adopt adapted.**\nWe already have a tasks table and a sprints table. The adaptation is to enforce that every agent-to-agent handoff writes a typed artifact row — not just a status update. Concretely: when Kenji's literature brief is done, it should INSERT a row into `tasks` with `artifact_type='brief_json'` and a `payload` column holding the structured JSON. My prototype job should read *that row*, not poll a free-text thread. Same for Naledi → me → Ingrid. Right now our handoffs are implicit (Slack-equivalent messages); this pattern says make the artifact the contract.\n\n**Repo change:** Add `artifact_type` and `artifact_payload JSONB` columns to the `tasks` table. Each agent's "done" state is defined by writing that payload, not by flipping a status flag.\n\n---\n\n#### Pattern A-2: The Experiment Progress Manager — a Controller Agent That Owns Stage Transitions\n\n\nThe system introduces an Experiment Progress Manager agent that manages a four-stage progression — `initial_implementation`, `baseline_tuning`, `creative_research`, and `ablation_studies` — where the best-performing node from one stage seeds the next.\n \nThis manager records checkpoints at each stage's completion and launches multiple replications of selected best experiments at the conclusion of each stage to provide statistics (mean and standard deviation).\n\n\nThis is a *controller loop*, not a sequential script. The manager holds stage state; the worker agents (the tree-search nodes) do not know what stage they're in — they just execute experiments.\n\n**Verdict: Adopt adapted.**\nWe don't need four ML-research stages, but we do need a Team Leader agent that explicitly owns sprint-phase transitions instead of just routing messages. Right now the Team Leader is essentially a dispatcher. This pattern suggests the Team Leader should: (a) own a `sprint_phase` enum in the sprints table, (b) be the only agent that advances it, and (c) block downstream agents from proceeding until the upstream artifact exists. Concretely: Naledi's cognitive brief should not be passable to me until the `sprint_phase` advances from `research` to `prototype`. The Team Leader agent enforces that gate, not a human reading the thread.\n\n**Repo change:** Add a `phase` column to `sprints` (`enum: ideation | research | prototype | review | closed`). Add a `phase_gating` function in the Team Leader agent that checks artifact presence before advancing phase. This lives in `agents/team_leader.py`, not in the individual researcher agents.\n\n---\n\n#### Pattern A-3: Constrained Action Spaces Per Agent Role\n\n\nThe ideation agent in `perform_ideation_temp_free.py` has exactly two actions it can emit: `SearchSemanticScholar` and `FinalizeIdea` — the system prompt closes with this constraint, meaning the agent cannot take any other action.\n \nThe right mental model is not "one autonomous scientist brain" but a closed research workflow made of specialized loops, where each loop's output becomes the next loop's evidence — plot review feeds node selection, node selection feeds stage transition, stage transition feeds the next experiment.\n\n\nThis is the most important structural idea in the whole repo. Each agent is not a general LLM with access to everything; it has a hardcoded, short list of legal actions for its role.\n\n**Verdict: Adopt as-is.**\nThis is directly applicable. Right now our agents each have broad tool access. We should define a `permitted_tools` list per agent class and enforce it at the executor level. Kenji (literature researcher): `search_vectorstore`, `write_brief`. Naledi (cognitive researcher): `read_brief`, `search_vectorstore`, `write_cognitive_annotation`. Me (developer): `read_brief`, `read_cognitive_annotation`, `write_prototype_file`, `write_task_artifact`. Ingrid (reviewer): `read_prototype_file`, `read_task_artifact`, `write_review`. Team Leader: `advance_sprint_phase`, `route_task`, `read_all`.\n\n**Repo change:** Add a `permitted_tools: list[str]` field to each agent class definition. The base `Agent` executor in `agents/base.py` should raise `PermissionError` if a tool not in that list is called. This is five lines of code but dramatically reduces the blast radius of a hallucinating agent.\n\n---\n\n#### Pattern A-4: VLM-Gated Review Loop (Non-Author Reviews Output Before It Advances)\n\n\nIn the plotting phase, generated visualizations are passed to a Vision-Language Model for critique; issues flagged by the VLM (unclear labels, missing legends, misleading visualizations) result in the node being marked as buggy, and the feedback is recorded for future debugging — nodes pass the VLM review before they are eligible for stage advancement.\n \nThis VLM critique loop is separate from the writing phase; the write-up also includes a single-pass generation followed by a separate reflection stage powered by reasoning models like o1.\n\n\nThe key engineering choice: review is a **blocking gate**, not an advisory comment. A failed VLM check doesn't just log a warning — it marks the node `buggy` and it never advances. The reviewer is structurally separate from the producer.\n\n**Verdict: Adopt adapted.**\nIngrid already exists as a reviewer role. The adaptation is to make her review a **hard gate**: no sprint can advance from `prototype` to `closed` without an `approved` row from Ingrid's agent in the reviews table (which we don't currently have). The "marked as buggy + feedback recorded" behavior maps to Ingrid writing a `review_result: rejected | approved` with a `notes` payload, and the sprint phase controller refusing to close the sprint on `rejected`. The feedback stored on rejection is what the next iteration reads — that's the loop.\n\n**Repo change:** New `reviews` table: `(id, task_id, reviewer_agent, result enum(approved|rejected|needs_revision), notes JSONB, created_at)`. Team Leader's `advance_sprint_phase` function checks for an `approved` row in `reviews` for the relevant task before allowing `prototype → closed`. If absent or `rejected`, it bounces back.\n\n---\n\n#### Pattern A-5: Structured File Artifacts as Persistent State (Not LLM Context)\n\n\nDuring each experiment, the system is instructed to save all relevant experimental outputs — training and validation metrics, losses, etc. — into structured numpy files; the plotting phase later reads these stored results and the code to generate visualizations.\n \nEach tree node is defined as a collection comprising an experiment script, a textual description, and its saved outputs — the node is the unit of state.\n\n\nState is never inside the LLM's context window between steps. It's always written to a file (or in their case, `.npy` files) and re-read. This is what makes the pipeline resumable and auditable.\n\n**Verdict: Adopt as-is.**\nOur vectorstore and DB are already doing the right thing directionally, but informally. The explicit adoption here is: **every agent run must write its primary output to `tools/db.py` before it is considered complete** — not just update a message log. The "node = (script + description + outputs)" abstraction maps for us to "task run = (agent_id + sprint_id + artifact_type + artifact_payload + timestamp)". If a run crashes, we resume from the last committed artifact row, not from LLM context.\n\n**Repo change:** Add a `task_runs` table: `(id, task_id, agent_id, started_at, completed_at, artifact_type, artifact_payload JSONB, status enum(running|success|failed))`. Every agent writes to this table as its first and last act. The vectorstore (`tools/vectorstore.py`) indexes completed runs' text payloads so future agents can retrieve prior work without re-running anything.\n\n---\n\n#### Pattern A-6: Per-Model Role Assignment (Different Models for Different Phases)\n\n\nThe README specifies models for the write-up and review phases via separate command-line arguments; using Claude 3.5 Sonnet for experimentation costs ~$15–20 per run, while GPT-4o for citations is recommended to reduce writing costs.\n\n\nEach phase uses the model best-suited (and cost-calibrated) for that specific job. The orchestrator passes model config per phase, not globally.\n\n**Verdict: Adopt adapted.**\nWe should assign model configs per agent role in a central `config.yaml`, not hardcode one model for everything. Kenji doing literature search can use a cheaper/faster model than Ingrid doing structured critique. Practically, this means our agent runner reads `agents.yaml` (or the config in `agents/`) and picks the right model per agent class before invoking the LLM. Low lift, real cost savings.\n\n**Repo change:** Add a `model_config` block per agent in `agents/config.yaml`. The base `Agent` class reads this at init rather than accepting a global env var.\n\n---\n\n### Source 2: RISE (bhanneke)\n\n**The honest assessment: I cannot review what isn't publicly findable.**\n\nAfter exhaustive search across six query strategies — exact username `bhanneke`, variations (`b-hanneke`, `hanneke`), combined with "RISE", "Jupyter", "reveal.js", "agent", "pipeline", "notebook", "presentation" — zero results matching a `bhanneke/RISE` or equivalent repo surface anywhere. The GitHub user `bhanneke` appears in one incidental result as a follower of a `cadCAD-org` org, but no repos are visible.\n\nWhat I *can* confirm: the well-known `damianavila/RISE` and `jupyterlab-contrib/rise` projects are Jupyter extensions that convert notebooks to Reveal.js slideshows. \nRISE allows instant conversion of Jupyter Notebooks to a slideshow without out-of-band conversion — switch from notebook to a live Reveal.js-based slideshow in a single keystroke and back.\n These are single-user interactive tools, not multi-agent pipelines. \nThe extension works by reading cell-level slide type metadata (slide, sub-slide, fragment, skip) set in the notebook's property inspector, and rendering accordingly.\n\n\n**There are no agent orchestration patterns to extract from the public RISE codebase** because it isn't an agent system — it's a notebook rendering extension. If the "bhanneke" variant adds an LLM-driven layer on top of this (e.g., an agent that auto-annotates notebook cells with slide types and generates Reveal.js output), that would be genuinely relevant to Altair8, but I can't assess a private repo.\n\n**What to do:** If someone on the team has access to `bhanneke/RISE`, open it up (or paste the README) and I'll do the same pattern analysis on it. If it turns out to be just a fork of `damianavila/RISE` with no agent machinery, there's nothing to borrow for our orchestration setup.\n\n---\n\n### Consolidated Adoption Checklist\n\n| Pattern | Source | Verdict | Concrete change in our repo |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Typed JSON artifact as inter-agent contract | AI-Sci-v2 | **Adopt adapted** | Add `artifact_type` + `artifact_payload JSONB` to `tasks` table |\n| Controller agent owns phase transitions (gating) | AI-Sci-v2 | **Adopt adapted** | Add `phase` enum to `sprints`; Team Leader in `agents/team_leader.py` is sole phase-advancer |\n| Constrained `permitted_tools` per agent role | AI-Sci-v2 | **Adopt as-is** | Add `permitted_tools` to each agent class; enforce in `agents/base.py` |\n| Non-author review as hard blocking gate | AI-Sci-v2 | **Adopt adapted** | New `reviews` table; `advance_phase` checks for `approved` row |\n| All output written to DB/files before "done" | AI-Sci-v2 | **Adopt as-is** | New `task_runs` table; vectorstore indexes completed payloads |\n| Per-role model assignment | AI-Sci-v2 | **Adopt adapted** | `agents/config.yaml` with per-agent `model_config` block |\n| RISE agent orchestration patterns | bhanneke/RISE | **Cannot assess** | Need the actual repo — escalate to whoever scoped this sprint |\n\nThe three I'd ship first, in order: **constrained tool permissions** (pure code, zero schema changes, immediate safety benefit), **typed artifact handoffs** (one migration, fixes our current implicit handoff problem), and **Ingrid's review as a hard gate** (one new table, directly closes the loop we keep talking about wanting).	2026-07-21 21:09:04.07599	2026-07-21 21:09:04.07599
