facts (two retrieval rounds failed) are Kenji's; the prescription (proceeding without search = building on unverified claim) is Sophie's. **Requires [SOPHIE]** on that clause, or the sentence should be split.\n\n> "[SOPHIE] C6 is the concept the team did not find blind — it required Naledi's survey to surface — which means it is the one with the most distance from the failure-data-derived reasoning that generated C1 through C5. Whether that distance is a strength (genuinely different angle) or a risk (less grounded in the sprint's specific failure analysis) is not established by the sprint."\n\nMarked. Fine.\n\n---\n\n### CHECK 2: MARKED SENTENCES THAT HAVE CLEAR REFERENTS\n\nLooking for [SOPHIE] used where the sprint actually established the point.\n\n> "[SOPHIE] Choosing between these is not primarily a technical choice — it is a choice about which variable the team believes is most underweighted in current AI communication systems..."\n\nThis is genuinely Sophie's framing — no agent made this observation. Mark is correct.\n\n> "[SOPHIE] These are different points of intervention in the generation pipeline, and they imply different assumptions about what the human knows well enough to correct. A human who does not know their audience well cannot improve C2; a human who does not understand the decision their audience must make cannot improve C4; a human who does not know their social position accurately cannot improve C5."\n\nThe first sentence is Sophie's synthesis. The second sentence — the three specific "cannot improve" statements — are derivable from Pass 1: C2 §7 names the failure mode (human must hold accurate beliefs about audience), C4 §2 presupposes the human knows the decision structure, C5 §7 names the invisible failure mode (miscalibrated relational self-model). These are traceable to the passes. Sophie could reasonably remove the mark from the second sentence. But leaving it marked does not damage the mechanism — it is not presenting a sprint finding as her own observation. Mark is used conservatively here. **Acceptable but slightly over-marked.** This is the less dangerous direction; I will note it without requiring a fix.\n\n> "[SOPHIE] C3 and C6 are in Category C for structurally different reasons that matter to how they could be recovered. C3 has a specific problem: retrieved evidence directly contradicts the stated claim. Recovery requires reframing the concept to articulate what is different from APT and the visualization recommendation literature — difficult but possible. C6 has a different problem: absence of evidence is not evidence of absence, and the relevant literature simply was not reached. Recovery requires targeted search in ACM Digital Library proceedings (CHI, UIST, DIS), data journalism collections, and design history sources."\n\n"C3 has a specific problem: retrieved evidence directly contradicts the stated claim" — this is Kenji's verdict, not Sophie's observation. The mark here is covering a mix: the framing of the distinction (Sophie's synthesis) and the recovery prescription (Sophie's prescription). The underlying facts (C3 contradicted by evidence, C6 retrieval failure) are Kenji's. The mark is covering too much — but the direction of error is conservative (Sophie marking something as hers that is partly Kenji's), not dangerous. **Minor over-marking; not a priority fix.**\n\n> "[SOPHIE] The buildability of the falsification test and the buildability of the concept itself are different questions — C4's prototype may be buildable even if the full falsification study is not."\n\nThis is Sophie's observation. Mateo did not make this distinction; he rated the test not buildable without commenting on the prototype separately. Mark is correct.\n\n**Summary on Check 2:** Two instances of mild over-marking (Sophie claiming credit for observations partly traceable to Kenji or Pass 1). Neither is dangerous — the over-marking direction does not mislead the founder about what the sprint found. No corrections required on this dimension, but Sophie should know.\n\n---\n\n### CHECK 3: NO-NEW-CLAIMS CONSTRAINT — DISAGREEMENTS AND WINNER-PICKING\n\n**Did Sophie resolve any disagreement by picking a side?**\n\nThere are no explicit disagreements between agents in the source passes — Passes 2 and 3 build on Pass 1 without contradicting it, and Mateo's assessments are independent. The closest case is the tension between Pass 2's introduction of C6 (Naledi's meta-finding) and the question of whether C6 is well-grounded in the failure data. Sophie names this tension explicitly in Section 4 and does *not* resolve it: *"Whether that distance is a strength (genuinely different angle) or a risk (less grounded in the sprint's specific failure analysis) is not established by the sprint."* This is correct handling.\n\n**Did Sophie recommend a winner?**\n\nNo. Section 4 is structured as six parallel conditional blocks ("if the team builds X first, the team commits to Y"). Each block states what building that concept would commit to, what the outstanding issues are, and in several cases a [SOPHIE]-marked observation about fit with the sprint brief. No ranking of concepts appears. No concept is recommended over another.\n\nThe [SOPHIE] observations in Section 4 do shade toward some concepts more than others — the C1 block notes it is "the most targeted response" to failures 4 and 5; the C2 block calls it "a structurally different design move." These are characterisations, not recommendations. A careful reader could infer Sophie's preferences, but she has not stated a preference and has not picked a winner. **Constraint obeyed.**\n\n---\n\n### CHECK 4: SECTION 3 — DOES IT STATE WHAT THE SPRINT DID NOT ESTABLISH?\n\n**Does it plainly say no concept reached Category A?**\n\nYes. The first substantive line: *"Kenji placed no concept in Category A."* This is unambiguous. It is in bold. It precedes the rest of the section. **Pass.**\n\n**Does it soften Kenji's verdicts?**\n\nOn C3: Kenji's verdict is "NOT NOVEL AS CURRENTLY STATED — VERDICT CHANGED." Section 3 states the APT lineage "constitute a 40-year research programme doing exactly this. The concept cannot be presented without full reframing." This accurately reflects the severity. The softening risk would be phrases like "requires minor adjustment" — none present. **Pass, with the caveat that "full reframing" is Sophie's prescription language (flagged above in Check 1).**\n\nOn C4: Kenji's verdict is "NARROWLY NOVEL — VERDICT CHANGED (narrowed further)." Section 3 states: "The current novelty framing does not survive these. The concept has a viable narrow claim... but the concept description must be rewritten." Accurate. **Pass.**\n\nOn C6: Kenji's verdict is that "no prior art found" is a retrieval failure. Section 3 states this explicitly: *"The verdict 'no prior art found' is a retrieval failure, not a finding. The concept cannot be shown as novel on current evidence."* Kenji's own language. **Pass.**\n\n**Does it present Kenji's verdicts as more settled than his retrieval logs support?**\n\nThis is the most important sub-question. Let me check each case where Kenji flagged residual uncertainty.\n\n- C2: Kenji explicitly flagged that the NLG planning literature was not reached as primary sources. Section 3 includes: *"The NLG planning literature (1990s–2000s) was not fully reached for C2. Kenji explicitly flagged that works by Paris, Moore, Reiter and Dale on user-model-driven generation were not retrieved as primary sources — only in survey form. This remains an open gap in the C2 prior-art picture."* **Retained. Pass.**\n\n- C3: Kenji noted Bertin and Munzner's nested model were not reached as primary documents. Section 3 does not mention this specific gap — it states the APT finding confidently. This is a gap in Section 3's coverage of Kenji's stated uncertainty. Kenji's verdict says: "The verdict on the selection-mechanism claim therefore rests partly on recollection." Section 3 does not carry this caveat forward. **Partial failure** — the C3 block presents the verdict more confidently than Kenji's own log supports.\n\n- C5: Kenji flagged the politeness-theory computational literature as unreached. Section 3 includes: *"C5 carries an unresolved retrieval gap. The politeness-theory computational literature (Brown and Levinson implementations in dialogue systems) was not reached in either retrieval round due to query vocabulary failures. Kenji's Round 2 note that this literature exists but is unretrieved remains unresolved. The concept's partial novelty verdict holds on current evidence but cannot be confirmed without reaching that literature."* **Retained. Pass.**\n\n- C6: Kenji's log says two complete retrieval misses. Section 3 includes: *"Two retrieval rounds failed to reach the diegetic display literature, the Isotype tradition, and data journalism practice."* **Retained. Pass.**\n\nThe C3 gap is a real issue: Kenji said his verdict "rests partly on recollection" for Munzner and Bertin. Section 3 presents the APT contradiction with confidence but does not carry this qualification forward. The effect is that C3's verdict reads as more firmly grounded than Kenji's own log supports — exactly the risk this check was designed to catch.\n\n---\n\n### FINDINGS SUMMARY\n\n**Check 1 — Unmarked sentences requiring [SOPHIE] marks:**\n\n1. *"C3 and C6 build artifacts calibrated to the information or domain itself, with the reader as secondary."* — Sophie's framing, not any agent's.\n\n2. *"All five Pass 1 concepts route human control through natural language."* — Sophie's cross-concept count, not stated by any agent.\n\n3. *"C2 is in Category B with a more urgent restatement requirement"* — the "more urgent" comparative is Sophie's ranking, not Kenji's.\n\n4. In the C3 block of Section 3: *"The concept cannot be presented without full reframing that engages explicitly with this prior art."* — The recovery prescription is Sophie's, not Kenji's verdict language.\n\n5. In the C1 block of Section 3: *"state the concept's contribution specifically as: continuous confidence scoring across four categories, visual weight from confidence rather than rhetorical emphasis, and minimum-spanning-argument scaffold"* — the packaging of the restated claim is Sophie's formulation, not Kenji's.\n\n6. In Section 4, C4 block: *"The concept by design requires pairing with other concepts if the team's goal is to serve the full goal space."* — Sophie's inference from the table.\n\n7. In Section 4, C6 block: *"proceeding to build without that search means building on an unverified novelty claim"* — Sophie's inference from the retrieval failure; the clause needs marking.\n\n**Check 1 — Section 3 partial failure (distinct from marking):**\n\nThe C3 block does not carry forward Kenji's explicit caveat that his verdict "rests partly on recollection" for Bertin and Munzner. The section presents C3's Category C verdict more firmly than Kenji's retrieval log supports. This is not a marking issue — it is a selective omission of Kenji's stated uncertainty.\n\n**Check 2:** Two instances of mild over-marking (conservative direction, not dangerous). No corrections required.\n\n**Check 3:** No disagreements picked; no winner recommended. Constraint obeyed.\n\n**Check 4:** Section 3 plainly states no concept reached Category A. Verdicts are not generally softened. One specific failure: C3's verdict is presented more confidently than Kenji's own log warrants (the Munzner/Bertin recollection caveat is dropped).\n\n---\n\n### RECOMMENDATION TO SOPHIE\n\n**REVISE** before this goes to the founder.\n\nRequired fixes, in order of importance:\n\n**1.** Add to the C3 block in Section 3: Kenji's explicit caveat that his verdict on the selection-mechanism claim "rests partly on recollection" for Bertin's Sémiologie Graphique and Munzner's nested model, which were not retrieved as primary documents. The block currently presents C3's Category C verdict as more firmly grounded than the retrieval log supports. This is the most consequential error — it affects how the founder reads the strength of the most severe verdict.\n\n**2.** Mark the seven sentences identified in Check 1. Five of these are in the comparison body (Sections 2 and 3), where unmarked observations are most likely to be read as sprint findings.\n\n**3.** The "more urgent restatement requirement" comparative for C2 either needs a [SOPHIE] mark or the word "more urgent" should be removed. As it stands, it implies Kenji ranked C2's restatement need above C1's, which he did not.\n\nNone of these require re-running retrieval or revisiting any agent's output. They are marking and caveat additions only. The synthesis is otherwise well-constructed — the dangerous direction (Check 1) has seven instances but none are substantively misleading claims about what the sprint found; they are mostly Sophie's reasonable synthesis presented as if the sprint produced it. The Section 3 C3 omission is the most substantive problem.\n\n**MARKING NOT HONEST** — the specific sentences above must be marked or corrected first.	2026-08-20 08:17:45.687144	2026-08-20 08:17:45.687144
146	team_leader	sprint10_synthesis_v1	# SPRINT 10, PASS 4 — ASSEMBLED COMPARISON\n\n*Sophie Marchetti, Team Leader, Altair8*\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 1 — THE COMPARISON TABLE\n\n| Concept | What it would build | Generative input form derives from | Goal space served | Kenji's verdict / category | Falsification buildable? | Single biggest named risk |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **C1: Commitment Audit** | Argument map where node visual weight encodes agent's confidence in each claim's epistemic status; reader can expand/collapse branches; minimum-spanning path is default | Agent's epistemic self-audit of its own content (confidence scores across evidence / inference / assumption / assertion) | Persuade a sceptical decision-maker or small team | ⚠️ Partially novel — **Category B** (restate claim re: PaperTrail, Epistemic Blinding) | **Buildable** | Design Principle 3 tension: interactive filter layer assumes analytical reading literacy the concept cannot guarantee |\n| **C2: Audience-in-the-Loop Simulation** | Artifact plus visible, reader-facing simulation trace showing agent's predicted audience attention, questions, and next action; trace is contestable via natural language | Agent's explicit, discrete audience model (role, prior knowledge, stakes), computed before form selection | Persuade/align where audience is specific and known | ⚠️ Narrowly novel — **Category B** (PosterMate and Proxona are prior art for author-facing simulation; claim must be restated to reader-facing trace + structurally prior form selection) | Cannot judge without knowing whether ground-truth audience response corpus exists | Audience simulation may be a plausible-sounding hallucination if agent's model performs at or near chance (Pass 1 falsification threshold: <50% agreement with expert-rated actual responses) |\n| **C3: Dimensionality-First Rendering** | Spatial HTML5 environment assembled from composable primitives (position, scale, proximity, containment, sequence, path) selected by dimensionality analysis; no chart-type selection | Intrinsic dimensional structure of the information (number of independent variables, variable types, primary relationship structure) | Inform a working team with genuinely multidimensional content | ⚠️ Not novel as stated — **Category C** (APT 1986 retrieved; core mechanism is a 40-year research programme; claim must be entirely reframed before showing to anyone) | **Buildable** | APT and visualization recommendation literature directly contradict the stated novelty claim; concept requires full reframing before any external presentation |\n| **C4: Decision-Surface Rendering** | Interactive criteria-space map; options positioned on it; reader drags sliders to reweight criteria and watches option positions shift; recommendation robustness shown in real time | Decision geometry of the communicative goal (option space, criteria space, criteria weights, recommendation with confidence) | Persuade-to-decide only (explicitly does not serve inform, align, or report) | ⚠️ Narrowly novel — **Category C** (NL2INTERFACE covers NL-to-interactive-visualization; MCDM literature covers computational operations; novelty claim must be rewritten to communicative/audience-oriented framing before showing to anyone) | **Not buildable** (expert-rated decision quality across two participant pools + expert raters cannot be recruited, run, and scored in one sprint) | Narrow scope by design: adopting this concept as primary leaves inform, align, and report unaddressed |\n| **C5: Relational Register Adaptation** | Artifact whose rhetorical structure — what must be established before the main claim, what can be asserted, what the closing move is — is derived from a four-axis relational map (authority direction, expertise direction, trust baseline, action threshold); visual form follows structure | Social and epistemic relationship between sender and receiver | All four goal regions; strongest where relational stakes are high | ⚠️ Partially novel — **Category B** (computational register synthesis is documented; four-axis explicit correctable map as structurally prior to rhetorical organization not described in retrieved literature; politeness-theory computational literature not reached) | **Not buildable** (48-hour action-rate observation window eats minimum two days inside a sprint before recruitment, sessions, or follow-up can occur) | Concept is only as good as the human's self-knowledge about their social position; miscalibrated relational map produces confidently wrong artifact with no internal detection mechanism |\n| **C6: Diegetic Data Embedding** | Domain world model in which data is encoded into visual properties of domain entities themselves (route thickness = cost, pulse rate = throughput); no separate chart panel; data inseparable from domain depiction | Agent's world model of the domain (entities, relationships, natural visual forms) plus data-property mapping | Inform and align where audience is familiar with the domain | ⚠️ No prior art found in this retrieval — **Category C** (two retrieval rounds failed to reach diegetic display literature, Isotype tradition, data journalism practice; verdict is retrieval failure, not evidence of novelty) | Not assessed by Mateo | World model must be recognisable to the audience; if not, artifact is irrelevant rather than confusing; agent-generated world model for arbitrary business domains is the hardest generation problem in the full set |\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 2 — WHERE THE CONCEPTS GENUINELY DIFFER\n\n### Axis 1: What the agent reasons about before generating anything\n\nThe six concepts split on what computation precedes form selection.\n\nC1 has the agent audit its own claims — classifying each as evidence, inference, assumption, or assertion and scoring confidence. The form is downstream of that self-examination. C2 has the agent simulate the audience — predict what they will attend to, question, and do. The form is downstream of that prediction. C3 has the agent analyse the information's intrinsic dimensional structure — how many independent variables, what types, what relationships. C4 has the agent decompose the decision the reader must make — options, criteria, weights, recommendation robustness. C5 has the agent map the social relationship between sender and receiver. C6 has the agent construct a world model of the domain itself.\n\n[SOPHIE] Choosing between these is not primarily a technical choice — it is a choice about which variable the team believes is most underweighted in current AI communication systems: the agent's epistemic self-awareness (C1), its model of the reader (C2), the information's own structure (C3), the decision the reader must make (C4), the social context of the exchange (C5), or the domain being talked about (C6). These are genuinely different diagnoses of why current systems fail.\n\n### Axis 2: Who the artifact is built for\n\nC1, C4, and C5 build artifacts calibrated to a specific, known reader or small group. C3 and C6 build artifacts calibrated to the information or domain itself, with the reader as secondary. C2 is the only concept that makes the calibration to a specific reader structurally visible *in the artifact itself* — the simulation trace is reader-facing, not just author-facing. This is the distinction that separates C2 from PosterMate and Proxona, per Kenji's revised verdict.\n\n### Axis 3: What the human controls\n\nAll five Pass 1 concepts route human control through natural language. What the natural language targets differs: in C1, it targets the confidence threshold for what appears in the default reading path. In C2, it targets the audience model directly. In C3, it targets spatial instructions parsed as dimensionality reductions. In C4, it targets criteria weights and option visibility. In C5, it targets the relational parameters (trust baseline, authority direction). In C6, it targets either the world model structure or the data-property mapping. [SOPHIE] These are different points of intervention in the generation pipeline, and they imply different assumptions about what the human knows well enough to correct. A human who does not know their audience well cannot improve C2; a human who does not understand the decision their audience must make cannot improve C4; a human who does not know their social position accurately cannot improve C5.\n\n### Axis 4: Which goal region each serves\n\nC1, C2, C4, and C5 are strongest for persuasion and alignment goals where the audience is specific and known. C3 and C6 are strongest for informing goals where the content has genuine structure the reader needs to apprehend correctly. C4 explicitly does not serve inform, align, or report. C5 is claimed to serve all four regions, though Pass 1 acknowledged it is weakest for distributed audiences where the relational map cannot be accurately specified.\n\n[SOPHIE] This axis matters for how the team frames the research programme: a concept that serves only one goal region requires either scope limitation or pairing with another concept; a concept that serves all four risks being optimised for none.\n\n### Axis 5: Novelty and prior art exposure\n\nC1 and C5 are in Category B — viable, specific claims, carry forward with restated framing. C2 is in Category B with a more urgent restatement requirement: PosterMate and Proxona are 2025 published work that directly overlaps the unqualified version of the claim. C3 and C4 are in Category C — cannot be shown to anyone in current form. C6 is in Category C for a different reason: the retrieval simply did not reach the relevant prior-art domains, so neither a novelty claim nor a prior-art acknowledgement is defensible yet.\n\n[SOPHIE] C3 and C6 are in Category C for structurally different reasons that matter to how they could be recovered. C3 has a specific problem: retrieved evidence directly contradicts the stated claim. Recovery requires reframing the concept to articulate what is different from APT and the visualization recommendation literature — difficult but possible. C6 has a different problem: absence of evidence is not evidence of absence, and the relevant literature simply was not reached. Recovery requires targeted search in ACM Digital Library proceedings (CHI, UIST, DIS), data journalism collections, and design history sources. This is a resolvable gap if the right search is run.\n\n### Axis 6: Implementation difficulty\n\nPass 2 named implementation gravity explicitly for C3 (the gap between ideal arbitrary spatial environments and what Mateo can build in a sprint using three or four primitives) and C6 (world model construction for arbitrary business domains is the hardest generation problem in the full set). C1, C2, C4, and C5 do not have the same architectural gap flagged, though C4's falsification test was rated not buildable by Mateo. [SOPHIE] The buildability of the falsification test and the buildability of the concept itself are different questions — C4's prototype may be buildable even if the full falsification study is not.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 3 — WHAT THE SPRINT DID NOT ESTABLISH\n\n**State plainly.**\n\n**Kenji placed no concept in Category A.** Every concept in this sprint carries either a prior-art qualification, a retrieval-failure caveat, or both. The comparison table above will read more decisively than the evidence under it. The verdicts in the table are Kenji's best assessment across two retrieval rounds totalling approximately 835 results at a ~93.5% noise rate; they are not confirmations.\n\n**Three concepts cannot be shown externally in current form.**\n\n- **C3 (Dimensionality-First Rendering):** The stated novelty claim — that deriving form from dimensionality analysis using composable primitives is not standard practice — is directly contradicted by retrieved evidence. APT (1986), Grammar of Graphics, VizML, and the visualization recommendation literature constitute a 40-year research programme doing exactly this. The concept cannot be presented without full reframing that engages explicitly with this prior art.\n\n- **C4 (Decision-Surface Rendering):** NL2INTERFACE (retrieved, Round 2) covers natural-language-to-interactive-visualization generation. The MCDM literature covers criteria-weighted option positioning. The current novelty framing does not survive these. The concept has a viable narrow claim — communicative, audience-oriented, persuasion-framed decision artifact as distinct from analytical tools — but the concept description must be rewritten to make that the stated claim.\n\n- **C6 (Diegetic Data Embedding):** Two retrieval rounds failed to reach the diegetic display literature (game studies, HCI venues), the Isotype tradition (design history), and data journalism practice. The verdict "no prior art found" is a retrieval failure, not a finding. The concept cannot be shown as novel on current evidence.\n\n**Two concepts require restated novelty claims before external presentation.**\n\n- **C1 (Commitment Audit):** Must acknowledge PaperTrail (claim-evidence provenance mapping) and Epistemic Blinding (inference-time epistemic auditing) as prior art, and state the concept's contribution specifically as: continuous confidence scoring across four categories, visual weight from confidence rather than rhetorical emphasis, and minimum-spanning-argument scaffold. The unqualified version of the claim does not hold.\n\n- **C2 (Audience-in-the-Loop Simulation):** Must acknowledge PosterMate and Proxona (2025 published work) as prior art for LLM audience simulation as a design tool, and state the concept's contribution specifically as: simulation trace as a reader-facing artifact component exposed in the final output, and audience model as structurally prior to form selection rather than posterior feedback. The unqualified version of the claim is misleading.\n\n**C5 carries an unresolved retrieval gap.** The politeness-theory computational literature (Brown and Levinson implementations in dialogue systems) was not reached in either retrieval round due to query vocabulary failures. Kenji's Round 2 note that this literature exists but is unretrieved remains unresolved. The concept's partial novelty verdict holds on current evidence but cannot be confirmed without reaching that literature.\n\n**Two falsification tests are not buildable in one sprint** (C4, C5). One cannot be judged without knowing whether a ground-truth audience response corpus exists (C2).\n\n**The NLG planning literature (1990s–2000s) was not fully reached for C2.** Kenji explicitly flagged that works by Paris, Moore, Reiter and Dale on user-model-driven generation were not retrieved as primary sources — only in survey form. This remains an open gap in the C2 prior-art picture.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 4 — WHAT A DECISION HERE WOULD ACTUALLY COMMIT TO\n\n### If the team builds C1 (Commitment Audit) first\n\nThe team commits to the agent's epistemic self-model as the primary design variable. Mateo would build a prototype with four epistemic category types, continuous confidence scoring, confidence-weighted visual layout, and a minimum-spanning-argument default path with expandable branches. The novelty claim must be restated relative to PaperTrail and Epistemic Blinding before any external presentation. The falsification test is buildable in one sprint per Mateo. The interaction log component of that test — tracking whether users expand the minimum-spanning path anyway — is a secondary falsifier that requires the prototype to include logging. [SOPHIE] This is the concept most tightly coupled to failures 4 and 5 (confabulated content passing structural checks), which were central to the sprint brief's failure data. If that is the team's sharpest pain point, this is the most targeted response.\n\n### If the team builds C2 (Audience-in-the-Loop Simulation) first\n\nThe team commits to the audience model as structurally prior to form selection, and to making that model visible and contestable in the final artifact. The novelty claim must be restated relative to PosterMate and Proxona — the restatement is more urgent here than for any other Category B concept because the overlap with existing 2025 HCI work is the most direct. Building the prototype requires resolving what the simulation mechanism actually is and whether historical ground-truth audience response data exists to run the falsification test; Mateo cannot judge buildability without this. [SOPHIE] The concept is the only one in the set that places the agent's reasoning about the reader into the reader's hands — that is a structurally different design move from the other five, and it is the one most directly aligned with the team's thesis that the reader-artifact relationship is the primary design space, per Naledi's meta-finding in Pass 2.\n\n### If the team builds C3 (Dimensionality-First Rendering) first\n\nThe team commits to reframing the concept entirely before building. The current novelty claim cannot be used. The concept would need to be repositioned as something that APT and the visualization recommendation literature do not already describe — which Kenji proposes could be the communicative framing (selecting primitives to serve a communicative goal for a specific audience, not to express data relationships for an analyst). Mateo rates the falsification test as buildable, but the constrained prototype (three or four primitives rather than arbitrary spatial environments) risks reintroducing chart-type conventions through the back door if the primitive set is too small, per Pass 1's named tension. [SOPHIE] Building C3 without first resolving the APT question would produce a prototype whose intellectual positioning is unclear, which is a different kind of risk from the other concepts — not a technical risk but a research validity risk.\n\n### If the team builds C4 (Decision-Surface Rendering) first\n\nThe team commits to a concept that explicitly does not serve inform, align, or report. The concept would need to be rewritten to foreground the communicative/audience-oriented framing as its novelty claim, distinguishing it from analytical MCDM tools. Mateo rates the falsification test as not buildable in one sprint; the team would need a smaller pilot test or a different evaluation approach. The concept by design requires pairing with other concepts if the team's goal is to serve the full goal space. [SOPHIE] The decision surface's interactive criteria-reweighting feature — where the reader can stress-test the recommendation against their own value weights — is the specific feature that Pass 1 identified as not described in prior art; that feature is also the one most at risk from NL2INTERFACE's coverage of NL-to-interactive-visualization generation, and the rewrite would need to address this directly.\n\n### If the team builds C5 (Relational Register Adaptation) first\n\nThe team commits to social and epistemic relationship as the primary generative variable. The four-axis relational map (authority, expertise, trust, action threshold) would need to be formalized and tested. The falsification test is not buildable in one sprint due to the 48-hour action-rate observation window. Pass 1 explicitly named that this concept has a category of failure — the human's relational self-model being wrong — that the architecture cannot self-correct and that the cheapest experiment cannot reach. Design Principle 4 tension was named: a relational register calling for restraint may produce a visually conventional artifact, and Pass 1's position was that relational appropriateness should take precedence over visual complexity in that case. [SOPHIE] C5 is the only concept in the set whose primary failure mode is not detectable from inside the system — the artifact produced by a miscalibrated relational map looks exactly correct. Building this concept first would require the team to make an early decision about whether that invisible failure mode is acceptable or whether an internal detection mechanism is a prerequisite.\n\n### If the team builds C6 (Diegetic Data Embedding) first\n\nThe team commits to resolving the retrieval gap before building. Two retrieval rounds have not reached the relevant prior-art domains; proceeding to build without that search means building on an unverified novelty claim. Pass 2 identified the concept as the most direct implementation of the insight that the reader-artifact relationship needs redesigning — and also named world model construction for arbitrary business domains as the hardest generation problem in the full set. The constrained prototype (three or four pre-selected domain types) is not the same concept at full scope, per Pass 2's explicit naming of this gap. [SOPHIE] C6 is the concept the team did not find blind — it required Naledi's survey to surface — which means it is the one with the most distance from the failure-data-derived reasoning that generated C1 through C5. Whether that distance is a strength (genuinely different angle) or a risk (less grounded in the sprint's specific failure analysis) is not established by the sprint.\n\n---\n\n*The table in Section 1 and the axes in Section 2 are the founder's scanning surface. Section 3 is the constraint on how decisively any of it should be read. Section 4 is what acting on it would mean.*	2026-08-20 08:20:32.442234	2026-08-20 08:20:32.442234
