185	\N	CORTEX: A Capability-Driven Reasoning Framework for Zero-Shot Large Language Model Selection	Kaikai Zhao, Zhaoxiang Liu, Zipeng Wang, Xin Wang, Heng Yang, Ning Wang, Kai Wang, Shiguo Lian	The proliferation of large language models (LLMs) makes real-world model selection nontrivial due to intertwined trade-offs among capability, cost, latency, and hardware constraints. To address this issue, we propose CORTEX, a scalable and automatic framework designed for model selection in real-world applications. It intelligently recommends the most suitable model based on a user’s natural language description of their application requirements, accompanied by reasonable explanations. CORTEX is a multi-agent system that integrates knowledge of 15 types of model capabilities, 105 application scenarios, and the capability boundaries of mainstream models. It includes a task decomposition agent (TDA), a knowledge retrieval agent (KRA), and a reasoning & ranking agent (RRA). The TDA utilizes DeepSeek-R1 to convert natural language description of the application requirements into a structured representation incorporating model capabilities and scenario knowledge. The KRA then retrieves a list of candidate models that satisfy these requirements based on the capability boundaries knowledge. Finally, the RRA utilizes DeepSeek-R1 to comprehensively evaluate the candidates based on performance, efficiency, cost, and user constraints, ultimately recommending the best model with highly interpretable reasoning. Comparative Experiments demonstrate that CORTEX achieves a model selection accuracy of 92.3%, significantly outperforming several comparison methods. As a practical and scalable solution to the critical "last-mile" problem of LLM selection and deployment, CORTEX is publicly accessible as a live service.	https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6dbb15239df9f36ea225b0c16f1703c716a24984	2026-07-27 13:14:03.240568	semantic_scholar	6dbb15239df9f36ea225b0c16f1703c716a24984
