477	\N	Citizens as Sensors for Small Communities	X. Ye, D. Jourdan, Chanam Lee, Galen D. Newman, Shannon Van Zandt	The increasing use of Global Positioning Systems (GPS) and location-aware mobile devices has created unprecedented opportunities to depict human behaviors in cities. Cities, as hubs of human activities, are composed of interrelated networks through a variety of ways: they may (1) be physically connected through transportation infrastructures, (2) exchange freight or information, (3) share similar social or environmental characteristics, or (4) be related through urban actors (e.g., persons, companies, and organizations). The strength of these relationships or networks within and between cities varies over time. In this increasingly connected and restless world, new places and new relations among places continue to emerge. Urban societies are composed of many interrelated contexts where individuals influence and interact with one another over space and time. Real-time human activity data (e.g., human mobility and social media) allow researchers and decision makers to detect how places in cities are preferred, used, and related. While these detailed activity and location data are increasingly available, smaller communities and towns often lack access to or resources to develop such data. For example, a reliable, punctual, and spatially accurate data set of the pedestrian and biking network (e.g., sidewalks, bike lanes, trails) is vital for identifying, analyzing, and visualizing where improvements can be made to the built environment to expand multi-modal accessibility, social connectedness, and physical activity of community residents. Whereas larger municipalities typically have internal resources to develop and maintain local transportation infrastructure data, many small communities with populations under 10,000 lack resources to have such local data. They, therefore, often rely on public data sets and mapping tools such as OpenStreetMap (OSM). Walkability and bikeability require detailed considerations of land uses, infrastructure conditions and amenities, including human-scale streetscape features. However, such detailed geospatial data especially relevant to walking and bicycling activities are either non-existent or inaccessible in many small communities and towns due to shortage of IT infrastructure and staff to use new computing and data technologies. A solution to this issue would be a scalable data collection tool. However, as noted, for many communities, it is beyond their ability to develop or implement these tools due to technological, financial, and human resource limitations. An alternative will be to tap into the increasingly available situational data that is publicly available through mobile devices and social media in the form of text, pictures, audio, and video. Despite their potential to enhance the volume and variety of community data, these social media data come with problems related to privacy, reliability, and validity. First, spatial location is often not given or not accurate enough to locate relevant environmental features such as sidewalks or bike lanes. Second, social media data are often absent in remote areas. Third, the incoming data can be noisy, making it not always applicable for real-time tasks. Hence, a platform to incorporate the notion of citizens as sensors may potentially provide the community with the capability to not only promote healthy behaviors that include getting regular physical activity, but also support the evaluation and documentation of the infrastructure that enables this activity. In addition to the infrastructure geometry data, we should also integrate visual information (video, images) and geonarratives (descriptions and comments about locations) to enable smarter and better-connected small communities and towns. Every GPS-enabled device, building, vehicle, and person can be used as a sensor to examine urban dynamics. Analyzing such data requires advances in computational methods and tools to decipher meanings embedded in the data. Rich and diverse information can be associated with movements and locations, and the data are often large, heterogeneous, dynamic, and unstructured. To obtain insights from such data, researchers must conduct iterative, evolving information foraging and sense making, and implement the process with the help of domain knowledge. With conventional transportation design software, such as TransCAD, Cube, and EMME, researchers can visualize, analyze, and model human activity–related data. However, there are few tools for researchers to conduct urban studies using emerging locationbased data. In particular, the challenges lie in the diversity of data types and methods. Various methods have been developed for particular datasets (e.g., a machine learning method for retrieving trajectories from point data). However, how these methods can be applied to other datasets for similar analysis is unknown. Moreover, studies that use these data to advance urban studies often focus only on one or two types of data to derive a particular metric of urban spatial structure (e.g., the mix of land uses). This becomes more problematic when increasing amounts of location-based data become available from various public and private organizations. Hence, new methods for citizens as sensors, as illustrated above, are needed to bridge the gap between the diversity of researcher needs and data infrastructure challenges often encountered in the small communities and towns.	https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0b6b5f4c1ab9ea34eef414b8e0d4d3bcf63b4efa	2026-08-20 07:55:28.540799	semantic_scholar	0b6b5f4c1ab9ea34eef414b8e0d4d3bcf63b4efa
