COPY public.sprints (id, sprint_number, question, outcome, status, started_at, completed_at) FROM stdin;
1	1	What are the most significant documented failure modes when LLMs attempt to reason about visual layout and spatial semantics, and what approaches have shown any traction against them?	Kenji documented LLM spatial/orientation-reasoning failure modes (arXiv-sourced); Naledi translated findings into cognitive-load design principles; Mateo built an HTML prototype demonstrating them. Ingrid's review confirmed the core findings but flagged overclaims and incomplete drafts.	completed	2026-07-20 10:17:17.029287	2026-07-20 10:17:17.04108
2	2	What does the current landscape of AI-generated slide/visualization tools look like, and where do they visibly hit the ceiling we're trying to get past?	Kenji surveyed the top 20 AI slide/visualization tools (web search) — confirmed none have solved AI-reasoned, non-linear communication structure; production is automated, communication reasoning is not. Ingrid's review required revision (speculative ceiling claims, Genially flag under-prioritized); Kenji revised. Genially now flagged for immediate follow-up as the closest existing analog; Flourish's SDK flagged as a build-vs-buy question; Prezi downgraded to low-priority watch.	completed	2026-07-20 10:17:24.56151	2026-07-20 10:17:24.567161
4	3	How far does Genially get toward AI-reasoned, non-linear communication structure, and what patterns from AI-Scientist-v2/RISE should Altair8 reuse to go further?	Kenji's Genially deep-dive: Genially solved the non-linear output container, AI component-code-generation, and engagement analytics, but navigation topology is 100% author-scripted at creation time, no meaningful runtime conditional logic, and the AI never reasons about why a visual form communicates better -- it answers 'what goes on this page,' never 'why this topology for this audience.' Mateo's AI-Scientist-v2 review (RISE parked -- repo not publicly findable): 6 orchestration patterns extracted with adopt/adapt verdicts, prioritized rollout is permitted-tools-per-agent first, typed artifact handoffs second, Ingrid's review as a hard gate third; phase-tracking and a full task-runs ledger deferred to a later sprint. Ingrid reviewed both deliverables: PROCEED with minor precision/labeling revisions on each, no re-review required. Open design question flagged for Sprint 4: whether Ingrid's review gate should be human-supervised or fully agent-automated.	completed	2026-07-21 21:36:24.791933	2026-07-21 21:36:24.804772
3	3	How far does Genially get toward AI-reasoned, non-linear communication structure, and what patterns from AI-Scientist-v2/RISE should Altair8 reuse to go further?	Superseded duplicate row -- log_sprint.py had a bug where it always created a new sprint row instead of completing the existing in_progress one for sprint_number 3 (fixed 2026-07-22, see id=4 for the real outcome).	completed	2026-07-21 20:45:08.004901	2026-07-21 22:24:59.618685
5	4	Have foundation-model providers (Anthropic, OpenAI, Google, Microsoft) already solved our core problem -- reasoning about why a visual form communicates better -- via their own skills/capabilities (not third-party tools)? Separately: how do these providers technically implement skill/capability selection and loading?	Kenji: not solved by any provider's documented first-party capabilities. Anthropic's dataviz skill has the most developed form-reasoning artifact found in this research program (a genuine data-job-to-chart-type heuristic with a runnable colorblind-safety validator), but it is scoped to charts/dashboards only, has no audience model, and does not generalize to Altair8's broader goal. OpenAI, Google, and Microsoft show capable production automation but no documented communicative form-reasoning layer. Explicit scope caveat added per Ingrid's review: this only assesses documented capabilities, not whether base models have internalized equivalent reasoning implicitly through training -- that would require behavioral testing, flagged as an open decision for Sophie before Sprint 5. Mateo: 10 orchestration patterns identified for how these providers route and load capabilities; two new vs Sprint 3 -- executable validators shipped alongside instructions (adopt the principle, design work required to classify deterministic vs judgment-call constraints first) and lazy-loaded deferred capability registries (independently converged on by Anthropic and OpenAI, adopt adapted). Priority order: validators first, trigger metadata second, deferred registry third, hierarchical context layers fourth. Ingrid's review required revision on both deliverables (precision issues, not structural failures); revisions applied and spot-checked against her 6 specific instructions before closing.	completed	2026-07-23 09:14:13.348562	2026-07-23 09:34:10.056233
10	9	Phase A (Naledi, Kenji supplements if needed) + Phase B (Mateo, deferred): What visual-communication patterns already exist in adjacent fields -- data journalism, motion graphics, spatial/AR interfaces, game UI, scientific visualization beyond charts -- that exploit zoom, motion, true multi-dimensionality, or interactivity (design principle #4), but were never applied to business communication? Scope: covered = the 5 fields above; excluded = classic infovis research literature (Sprint 5) and pure graphic-design theory without a communication angle. Phase A produces a catalog of 8-12 concrete patterns (field, pattern, what audience/goal need it solves, whether business communication currently lacks an equivalent) -- not prototypes yet. Phase B (Mateo) selects 2-3 candidates against explicit criteria (solves a real audience/goal gap; technically buildable without special infrastructure; categorically different from existing chart types, not just styling) and builds minimal standalone demos. Rigorous behavioral validation (same paired-scenario method as Sprint 6/7/8) is deliberately deferred to Sprint 10. DSR hypothesis: communicatively effective visual patterns exist outside the current business-chart taxonomy and are feasibly portable -- falsified if Phase A produces nothing meeting criterion (c), i.e. everything found is just restyled existing chart types. Connects to backlog #11 (first prototype, Sprint 8) as its natural continuation, and to candidate_approaches #4 (NL2INTERFACE/SmartMLVs -- interactive multi-view assemblies).	Phase A: Naledi cataloged 11 patterns across the 5 fields (no web access, explicitly disclosed, confidence-tiered). Standouts: scrollytelling with pinned visual state, annotation-led reading path, force-directed relationship graphs, diegetic information display (data embedded in a depiction of the subject, borrowed from game UI). Naledi's meta-finding: the most interesting innovations across all 5 fields are new relationships between reader and artifact (pacing, positioning, disclosure triggers), not new chart shapes. Ingrid's review (approved, with real Phase-B scoping conditions rather than text revisions): 3 of 4 picks well-justified against Naledi's own criteria; diegetic display needed re-risking (requires the agent to generate a 'world model' of the subject, a categorically harder generation problem) rather than dropping -- scoped down to pre-specified entities for Phase B; the meta-finding was only supported by 4-5 of 11 patterns, carried forward scoped down; flagged a concrete DP4 violation risk specific to scrollytelling (could be built by hand-authoring the scroll logic and having the agent only generate text, faking the pattern without testing the real generative capability). Phase B: Mateo built all 4 as standalone prototypes (prototype/patterns/), each prompt-engineered so the actual generative decision under test is unavoidable per Ingrid's condition. Real generated content, not placeholders: annotation-led isolated one specific anomalous data point with justification; force-directed gave a genuine relational-vs-magnitude rationale before extracting nodes/edges; diegetic display produced internally coherent values telling an actual bottleneck story. Founder reviewed all 4 in a real browser: all have some flaws, but fundamentally work -- founder chose to close Sprint 9 with flaws documented rather than debug now, since these are proof-of-concept prototypes, not production artifacts. Rigorous behavioral validation of these patterns against conventional forms (same paired-scenario method as Sprint 6/7/8) is deferred to Sprint 10, as originally sequenced.\n\nAMENDMENT (2026-08-17): Founder-initiated follow-up test, added to Sprint 9 rather than as a new sprint (same pattern as Sprint 6's Gemini addition) since it closes a related but distinct question: not audience/goal-driven form selection (Sprint 6-8) and not novel-form generation (this sprint's four Phase-B patterns), but raw generative-reproduction fidelity -- given an existing image, can the agent reconstruct it as vector code at all. Built as a real script, prototype/patterns/template_reproduction.py, using a new vision-input capability added to prototype/pipeline/providers.py (complete_with_image, Anthropic-only for now). Two source images tested, holding subject constant (a rocket) while varying visual complexity: a flat vector-icon style, and a monochrome tilted illustration with fine linework. Hard requirement enforced in code (verify_no_raster_reinsertion): the output must be pure vector markup, no <img>/<image> tag and no embedded raster data URI of the source -- the same no-fake-scaffolding condition Ingrid applied to the other four Phase-B prototypes, applied here up front. Both runs passed that check on the first attempt. Explicitly a 2-item pilot: single run per image, fidelity judged by side-by-side visual comparison on the website, not a scoring rubric. Not yet formally reviewed by Ingrid -- flagged openly rather than silently treated as approved. Observed result: the flat-icon reproduction was reasonably faithful (proportions, colors, composition); the complex tilted illustration was not -- the reconstruction kept the general silhouette but lost the tilted pose (came out upright) and collapsed the fine cross-hatched linework into a much simpler black shape. Fidelity clearly degrades with visual complexity; a 2-item pilot cannot say where the line is, only that one exists. Review cycle added (2026-08-17): v1 was compared directly against the original images and specific gaps were written up (same review-then-revise pattern as every other sprint); that critique plus the original image went back to the agent for a v2 attempt. v2 fixed the simple icon's specific issues (off-center window, a rendering artifact, an unclosed seam). For the complex illustration, v2 substantially improved the two biggest gaps -- correct diagonal tilt, shift from solid-black fill toward line-based rendering -- but still does not fully match the source (cockpit reads as a gauge icon, not the source's lens shape; lower-body silhouette still diverges). One review cycle measurably closed part of the fidelity gap, not all of it.	completed	2026-08-06 15:35:46.281376	2026-08-17 08:52:35.5702
6	5	Full market analysis (all 4 required categories) of who has already solved -- or come closest to solving -- reasoning about why a visual form communicates better: fill the remaining gaps from Sprints 1-4 (broad literature search across all 4 databases, open-source frameworks beyond RISE).	Confirmed the persistent gap across all 4 categories and 5 sprints: no system -- commercial, first-party, open-source, or academic -- reasons about visual-form selection at the presentation level with an explicit audience model. New ground: RISE finally found (repo exists, architecturally irrelevant to our question, gap closed negatively); PPTAgent/PPTEval (EMNLP 2025) is the strongest open-source analog found (evaluates content/design/coherence separately, but post-hoc not pre-hoc); LLM4Vis and DracoGPT are the closest academic work found, generating real explanations for chart-type choices and probing whether LLMs internalized visualization preferences -- but confirmed to operate one abstraction level below our presentation-level question (chart-selection, not presentation-form selection). Microsoft Copilot gap from Sprint 4 closed: it elicits audience info but shows no documented downstream reasoning on it. Live database reliability was a real problem this sprint -- only OpenAlex worked, arXiv/Semantic Scholar/IEEE Xplore all failed -- Ingrid required this be caveated explicitly in the body (not just a footnote) and confidence downgraded from High to Medium accordingly; revision applied and confirmed. An open follow-up (task 35) is logged: targeted arXiv+Semantic Scholar re-run for forward-citation checks on LLM4Vis/DracoGPT once those sources are stable. Sprint genuinely closes the Sprint 2/3 scope gap the founder caught; the scope-checklist process fix worked as designed.	completed	2026-07-27 09:28:03.368366	2026-07-27 09:41:16.085417
7	6	Behavioral testing: do base foundation models (Claude, GPT) show principled reasoning when selecting a visual form for a communication goal + audience, or is the reasoning post-hoc confabulation that does not actually shift when the goal/audience changes? Answers Ingrid's open question from Sprint 4.	Pilot (3 paired scenarios, 6 variants, Claude + GPT, 1 run each) found reasoning that is cognitively coherent, variable-sensitive, and discriminates between alternatives in most cases -- more consistent with partially principled reasoning than pure confabulation, but not causally provable from API output alone. Strongest evidence: Claude's plain-prose choice for new support reps, GPT's heatmap choice for urgency detection, both models' structural-fit reasoning in the content-type control. Weakest case: GPT defaulting to a chart for a narrative-comprehension audience ('medium-frame anchoring'). Ingrid's review (needs_revision -> approved after correction) removed a false-precision 60/40 confidence estimate, marked cross-model verdicts as provisional/pairing-specific (not portable without Gemini + multiple runs), and redesigned the Sprint 7 follow-up probe around adversarial pairs (non-canonical form + goal/audience override) to fix a flaw in the original form-prediction test. This pilot is sufficient to inform Sophie's framing of the founder's solution-direction choice, not to decide it, and is not sufficient to make Direction-A architecture decisions on its own -- Sprint 7's adversarial-pairs study is the next step for that.\n\nAMENDMENT (2026-08-02): The Gemini gap flagged above was closed as a follow-up once a Google API key became available -- documented here under Sprint 6 since it directly resolves a gap Sprint 6 itself raised, not as a separate sprint. Gemini was run against the same 6 variants (agents/researcher_visual/add_gemini_to_behavioral_test.py) and folded into Naledi's brief as a third model. This happened in two real steps (Claude+GPT reviewed/closed first, Gemini added second) -- that sequencing is disclosed in the brief itself, not hidden. Ingrid's review of the addition first required revision (a drafting artifact, an underspecified Claude failure-mode label, an incomplete appendix, and one inconsistently-hedged label), then approved after Naledi's revision fixed all four (review_id 8 -> 9). Net finding: the three-model picture is more informative than the two-model one -- each model shows a distinct behavioral signature (GPT: medium-frame anchoring; Gemini: a candidate, single-pair form-conservatism pattern; Claude: no clear failure mode in this dataset) -- still directional evidence only, not a quantifiable finding. Sprint 7's adversarial-pairs design now explicitly spans all three models.	completed	2026-07-27 16:51:01.357183	2026-07-28 07:29:14.226441
8	7	Two gated tracks: (1) Naledi runs the adversarial-pairs behavioral test (Claude/GPT/Gemini, canonical-form-obvious-but-audience/goal-calls-for-non-canonical scenarios) plus the tool-access-vs-no-tool-access dimension (backlog #9) -- research track, can proceed immediately. (2) Founder reads TVIR (arxiv.org/abs/2606.02320) and the other open candidate_approaches entries, then answers: is TVIR's architecture close enough to Altair8's target that Direction (a) -- extend it with a goal/audience-aware reasoning layer -- is the right path, or does it reveal a structural ceiling only Direction (b) -- a genuinely new visualization language -- can break through? This decision track gates backlog #1/#2. Sprint 7 does not close until BOTH tracks are done -- research alone is not sufficient to close this sprint.	Research track: Naledi ran 6 adversarial pairs (12 variants) across Claude/GPT/Gemini -- scenarios where the canonical visual form is textbook-obvious but audience/goal call for a categorical medium switch. All 6 pairs documented their canonical-form rationale and override mechanism explicitly, addressing Ingrid's own Sprint 6 concern about construction validity. Findings: Claude's Sprint 6 pattern (no clear failure mode) held and strengthened, showing 'form-as-signal' reasoning in the strongest cases. GPT's 'medium-frame anchoring' persisted only as a residual tendency (5/6 pairs scored cleanly). Gemini's Sprint 6 'form-conservatism' concern did NOT strongly replicate under adversarial pressure -- weaker and more selective than expected. Naledi self-identified the most important methodological limitation: the 'culturally-scripted-scenario problem' -- some adversarial scenarios (finance audit, VP status check) are recognizable professional scripts models could pattern-match on rather than genuinely reasoning from audience/goal; the less-scripted pairs (channel-rendering constraint, semiotic register mismatch) produced more diagnostic evidence. Ingrid's review required one revision pass (5 documentation/framing corrections, including a real construction-validity gap in one pair's canonical-form claim); all fixed and approved. Verdict: this design is a genuine methodological improvement over Sprint 6's approach, and the combined Sprint 6+7 evidence is sufficient to INFORM (not decide) the solution-direction question -- still directional, not conclusive; 15-20 pairs with multiple runs and genuinely novel (non-scripted) scenarios are needed for a robust study. Decision track: founder read TVIR and found it the closest existing analog to Altair8's goal (its multi-stage text-to-chart/image generation pipeline specifically), but explicitly declined to commit to Direction A vs B on that basis. His reasoning: a more fundamental question is still open and unaddressed by any research so far -- what IS the target output medium (a polished static report, as TVIR produces, vs. something like a dynamic HTML5 experience, vs. another format entirely)? Decision: do not commit early. Keep TVIR as one candidate approach alongside the others already catalogued, and adopt an iterative multi-approach exploration strategy for the Design & Development phase -- prototype multiple candidates (including new ones), let the direction emerge from what actually works, rather than settling it on paper first. This resolves backlog items #1 and #2, and replaces them with a new, more concrete open item: resolve the target-output-medium question and build the first iterative prototype (TVIR-inspired multi-stage generation, as a starting candidate, not a foregone conclusion).	completed	2026-08-06 07:59:08.63709	2026-08-06 10:42:15.313815
9	8	First sprint of DSR phase Design & Development. Backlog #11: resolve the target output medium empirically by building, and produce a scoped, reasoned first-prototype proposal -- a TVIR-inspired multi-stage pipeline that generates text + charts/images from content, studied from the actual github.com/NJU-LINK/TVIR implementation (Apache 2.0), not just the paper. Mateo proposes before building: what should the first prototype target as its output medium, grounded in design principle #4 (full visual freedom, not 2D-box-constrained) and the hypothesis/addendum about implementation-ease risk (can pick what ships first, must not quietly lower ambition). Backlog #9 (tool-access vs. no-tool-access) folds in only if the prototype naturally involves code execution -- not forced in artificially.	Fetched TVIR's real GitHub implementation directly and confirmed its actual output is static Markdown with embedded images -- exactly the 'polished paper' format the founder was skeptical of. Mateo proposed the first prototype's output medium instead: a rendered, interactive HTML page with client-side Vega-Lite charts, not Markdown -- explicitly defended against design principle #4's addendum (implementation ease can pick what ships first, but the gap to full ambition must be named). Ingrid's architecture review (before any code) found 3 real specification gaps -- undecided Stage 2 data-sourcing, an understated Stage 3 context-model rebuild, a missing intermediate representation -- all fixed and re-approved. The 4-stage pipeline (Planner -> Visual Asset Generator -> Component Writer -> HTML Renderer) was built at prototype/pipeline/ and actually run end to end multiple times. Three real bugs were found and fixed by reading the pipeline's own output rather than trusting automated checks: a token-truncation JSON failure; a missing content_summary data-flow link that caused Stage 2 to generate completely off-topic chart content (homelessness-service data in a heat-mitigation document -- structurally perfect, substantively confabulated); and a chart-width overflow bug whose first fix (container-based responsive sizing) silently broke rendering entirely in a real browser, caught only because the founder opened the file, and reverted in favor of a safer static-width clamp. A content-grounding check (an independent Claude judge checking whether GPT-generated chart data actually matches its stated topic) was built specifically because structural validation alone could not catch the confabulation bug -- a concrete, real-artifact demonstration of exactly the risk Sprint 6 and 7 investigated abstractly through text-only behavioral tests. PROCESS NOTE, added retroactively at Sprint 9's close: this sprint was never formally closed via log_sprint.py when the work finished -- it sat as 'in_progress' in Postgres with no completed_at timestamp until Process Review #2 caught it. This is also why the standing website-update-after-close rule silently failed to fire for this sprint. Closed now, retroactively, with the gap documented rather than quietly backdated.	completed	2026-08-06 12:03:44.385489	2026-08-06 16:10:07.573017
11	10	Design & Development, design half: what rival concepts exist for the communication layer, and how would each actually work? Four isolated passes -- Priya blind (no prior-art seeds), Priya sighted (seeds shown), Kenji per-concept prior-art verification, Sophie synthesis into a comparative decision input. Generates alternatives before building or validating any of them.	First sprint in ten to design ALTERNATIVES rather than implementations. Sixth agent hired (Priya Raghunathan, concept designer; no web_search by design so she cannot self-certify novelty). Six concepts produced across a blind/sighted structure: five generated with no prior-art seeds, one added after the seeds were shown. The seeding experiment's result -- the reading list produced exactly one new concept (C6, from Naledi's Sprint 9 diegetic pattern), one revision, no withdrawals. None of the five blind concepts is a new visual form; all are architectures for what should DRIVE form selection, in categorical contrast to Sprint 9, which surveyed adjacent fields and produced only new visual forms. Kenji ran real retrieval (18 + 21 queries, ~835 results, ~93.5% noise), judged his own round 1 inadequate for 4 of 6 concepts, and requeried. Mackinlay's APT (1986) was retrieved and directly contradicts C3's novelty claim. PosterMate/Proxona (2025) are prior art for C2. NO concept reached Category A. Priya restated the three Category B claims, narrowing all three and volunteering a prior-art risk to C1 that Kenji missed. Success criterion (b) FAILED as written -- 3 of 5 falsification tests are unrunnable because they need human readers the team cannot recruit; Ingrid judged this a drafting failure in the criterion, not an execution failure, and noted honestly that capability limits shaped the viable field. FOUNDER CHOSE C1 (The Commitment Audit): the agent audits its own claims as evidence/inference/assumption/assertion and renders an argument map where visual weight derives from confidence rather than rhetorical emphasis. Public comparison at /sprint-reports/sprint-10-comparison.html. Four Ingrid conditions carry into Sprint 11.	completed	2026-08-19 15:54:34.598597	2026-08-20 09:46:29.799633
12	11	Build the C1 Commitment Audit prototype -- an agent that audits its own claims as evidence / inference / assumption / assertion with continuous confidence, and renders an argument map where visual weight derives from confidence rather than rhetorical emphasis. Two Ingrid conditions gate the build: a targeted argument-visualization prior-art search before any external framing, and a formal specification of the minimum-spanning-argument scaffold before Mateo starts.	Built and browser-verified a rendering and interaction prototype for C1 (prototype/commitment_audit/): a navigable argument map where visual weight derives from the audit's own confidence band, with a primary reading path selected by the PRP algorithm. Deterministic throughout -- no LLM call anywhere in the render path, so the output is reproducible and inspectable rather than regenerated each run.\n\nThe audit CONTENT is hand-authored, not agent-generated. This sprint tested whether the rendering model works, not whether an agent can produce an honest audit of its own claims. The sprint question as written says "an agent that audits its own claims" -- that half is NOT answered here and remains open as backlog #24, which the team's own assessment rates the more important question.\n\nESTABLISHED: the PRP algorithm is implemented and correct against Mateo's spec; the schema enforces Priya's two rulings in code rather than by convention (confidence ordinal, never combined, propagated or averaged; no claim can exist without its category and confidence, foreclosing content-first-annotate-later); stepped bands, expand/collapse and suppress-in-place filtering behave as specified; the spec'd no-path failure state is implemented, including the Case 3 load-bearing confession. Two bugs were found by building: "structurally unavoidable" defined globally matched almost nothing and would have shipped dead (redefined locally as load-bearing), and the sample audit collapsed two structural test cases onto one claim so the propagation warning never fired (split in two).\n\nNOT ESTABLISHED: that the concept works with an agent as author (#24); that the confidence encoding is legible in the default collapsed state -- the browser verification found it is not, and the design decision is open (#27); edge geometry is hard-coded rather than measured (#26).\n\nPROCESS: first prototype in this project verified in a real browser BEFORE review rather than after (headless Chrome, three states, workspace/outputs/prototype-runs/verification/). Six defects found; Ingrid blocked the close on three; fixed and re-verified; approved on the second pass. The sprint had sat in_progress and unreviewed for 26 days -- Process Review #2's Standing Obligations Check only fires at sprint planning, never between sprints (#30, #31). Mateo's spec was found to contradict itself on the failure state ("root alone" vs a dictated banner reading "Showing full map"); Ingrid ruled root-alone correct and the wording the error, and the ruling is in the record. Ingrid's own Section 5 finding about a truncated prior-art brief was struck by her at the confirmation pass: the truncation was introduced by Sophie's close-review script slicing Kenji's brief at 16000 characters, not by Kenji and not by the process.	completed	2026-08-20 10:06:07.443249	2026-09-15 14:09:41.706688
