24	kenji	team_leader	Sprint 3: Genially deep-dive	Genially was flagged as the closest existing analog to what Altair8 is trying to build. Exactly how far does it get toward AI-reasoned, non-linear communication structure, and precisely where does it stop?	completed	Good — I now have sufficient material. Let me also pull one more thing: the Genially help-center content on its AI creation pipeline (the "Iris" editor step) and the Genially vs Articulate own page's candid admission about what it doesn't do.\nI have sufficient material for a comprehensive brief. Let me now compose it.\nGood — index confirmed. Now composing the brief.\n\n---\n\n# Deep-Dive Literature Brief — Genially: Interactivity Ceiling Analysis\n\n**Prepared by:** Kenji Ochiai, Researcher, Altair8\n**Sprint question:** Exactly how far does Genially get toward AI-reasoned, non-linear communication structure, and precisely where does it stop?\n**Method:** Web search synthesis across Genially product documentation, official help center, vendor blog, third-party reviews (Atomi Systems 2026, eLearning Industry, Pimenko, Aufiero Informática, Tinybird case study), and G2/Capterra user reviews. Review window: mid-2025–April 2026.\n**Confidence:** High for interactivity architecture (sourced from official documentation); high for AI layer characterization (sourced from official blog, help center, and independent reviews); moderate for roadmap inferences (vendor-reported, no third-party confirmation).\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 1 — WHAT GENIALLY ACTUALLY GENERATES AND HOW\n\n### 1a. The authoring layer: what the canvas is\n\nGenially's core product is a canvas-based interactive content editor. \nInteractivity can be added to any element, including text, images, buttons, and icons, through hotspots, pop-ups, branching pathways, drag-and-drop activities, and micro-interactions.\n \nAny element on the canvas can be made interactive — including buttons, images, text, or even an invisible area of the screen — and interactions can be launched when users move their cursor over an element or when they click.\n\n\nThe output format vocabulary is genuinely wider than slides. \nUsers can design different learning journeys, from linear presentations to microsite-style navigation, adaptive branching scenarios, or autoplay video-style experiences.\n This is the format plurality that distinguished Genially from every other tool in the Sprint 2 survey: a single platform whose output container is not fixed to the rectangular sequential deck.\n\n### 1b. The AI generation layer: two distinct tools\n\nGenially's AI system consists of two separate functions that are easy to conflate but operate at different scopes:\n\n**Tool 1 — "Generate with AI" (full genially creation):** \nYou describe your idea or use a source like a PDF or a text, and Genially's AI generates the complete draft for you, including layout, content, and interactivity.\n \nAI won't simply copy and paste it into a new format — instead, it reinterprets the content into a more visual, interactive experience designed to be easier to navigate and more engaging for your audience.\n The pipeline surfaces a review step: \nan editor named "Iris" presents a draft proposal where you can see the structure and content of each page, and this is the moment to review it.\n Once approved, \nAI builds a complete genially in a matter of minutes, after which you can share it right away or edit it in the editor like any other genially — change text, adjust the design, add interactivity, reorganize pages, and more.\n\n\n**Tool 2 — "AI Builder" (component-level generation):** \nGenially AI Builder is an AI-powered interactive creation tool that turns ideas into interactive content in minutes — no technical skills needed, with full control over the final result.\n The scope is narrower than full-deck generation: \nAI Builder builds the full interactive structure of a component — it is a conversational space where you describe what you need and get a ready-to-use interactive component.\n \nComponents generated by AI Builder include word searches, matching activities, spin wheels, scrollable timelines, and more.\n Critically, \nGenially AI Builder writes the code for the generated components — no technical skills or team of developers required.\n\n\n**The vendor's own framing of the problem AI is solving here:** \n"The bottleneck is usually the same: the technical side. We want to create amazing things, but we're not developers — and we don't have ten hours to build the logic behind a game or quiz."\n This framing is a precise diagnostic of what the AI layer is designed to remove: *production friction*, not communication-structure reasoning.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 2 — DOES GENIALLY REASON ABOUT COMMUNICATION STRUCTURE?\n\n### 2a. The navigation architecture: author-scripted at creation time\n\nThis is the decisive point, and the official documentation is unambiguous about it.\n\nGenially has three navigation modes. \nStandard mode allows your audience to move through your creation in a linear fashion; in Microsite mode, your audience can only move from one page to another by clicking on an element that you have set up with "Go to page" interactivity so that they move through your creation in the order you have established.\n The third mode is Video (autoplay). None of these modes involve runtime AI reasoning.\n\nThe help center is explicit about what this means for audience agency: \n"When a genially is in Microsite mode, you cannot turn the page or go back however you want: the person who creates the genially decides the path you are going to follow when they're creating it."\n\n\n"Branching" in Genially means a predetermined decision tree authored at creation time. \nA "Go to page" action lets the author decide which page the audience will land on after answering an interactive question — including the option to choose a specific page for the audience to go to after answering.\n This is not adaptive routing: it is author-scripted routing with multiple pre-authored endpoints. The branching graph is fully defined before any audience member arrives. The navigation paths do not change based on what the audience has seen, how long they dwelt, or what patterns emerge.\n\n### 2b. No runtime variables or conditional state\n\n\nWhile the user interface is intuitive, Genially focuses on "click-to-reveal" interactions. It does not typically support complex conditional logic or variables found in high-end authoring suites.\n This is the hard floor. Tools like Articulate Storyline permit variable-tracking that persists across the session — for example, routing a learner to different content based on cumulative quiz performance across multiple slides. \nGenially's variable management, although possible, is less intuitive and powerful than Storyline.\n Genially's own competitive positioning page acknowledges the gap directly: when listing reasons to choose Articulate over Genially, it cites \nthe need for "features like advanced conditional logic" and the desire to "code your own interactions and use javascript snippets in your courses."\n\n\nThe operational implication: Genially supports multi-path navigation, but every path must be pre-authored. There is no mechanism for a learner's past behavior within the session to modify which paths remain available going forward — except through the limited, manually-authored quiz-score routing described above.\n\n### 2c. The AI layer does not reason about navigation structure\n\nThe "Generate with AI" pipeline proposes a page structure and distributes content across pages. \nGenially claims to do "99% of the work, from planning the layout to writing the content," generating a framework that the user can build on.\n But structuring "pages" is not the same as reasoning about communication structure. The AI is deciding how many pages, what content goes on each, and what visual template to apply — essentially, it is performing automated instructional design at the macro level (content chunking) and layout design at the micro level. There is no evidence in product documentation or independent reviews that the AI reasons about:\n\n- *Why* a particular navigational topology serves a particular communication goal\n- What visual form best encodes the relationships between ideas\n- What sequencing strategy serves a specific audience's prior knowledge or cognitive load\n- How to adapt the topology if the audience demonstrates confusion at a given node\n\n\nAdding pop-ups, defining clickable areas, configuring chained animations, or structuring non-linear navigation requires understanding the logic of layers and actions that the platform offers\n — and this understanding, per all reviewed sources, must be supplied by the human author. The AI does not make these structural decisions; it generates content and component visuals to be placed within a structure the author defines.\n\n### 2d. Analytics: data collected but not fed back into content\n\n\nIn the analytics dashboard, Genially tracks views, interactions, date of access, student progress, scores, and question responses.\n \nUpcoming dashboards will provide more granular realtime views of how content is performing — how users performed on a quiz, and other granular interaction data — giving Genially "the flexibility to go beyond page views and basic metrics."\n\n\nThis is observable engagement data of genuine richness. However, per all reviewed documentation, this data flows to the *author dashboard* — it does not feed back into the content structure at runtime or trigger adaptive re-sequencing. The loop between "what I now know about this audience's behavior" and "how the content will present itself to the next visitor" is entirely closed by the human author between sessions, not by any AI system within a session.\n\n\nGenially also includes Live Sessions, enabling real-time quizzes, surveys, polls, and audience interaction for workshops, virtual training, and blended learning experiences.\n This is the closest Genially gets to runtime audience responsiveness — but it is a presenter-facilitated polling feature, not autonomous content adaptation. The facilitator sees the live poll result and manually decides what to do with it.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 3 — ORCHESTRATION AND PIPELINE APPROACH\n\nThe architectural picture that emerges from documentation and reviews:\n\n**Layer 1 — Content generation:** A language model (undisclosed provider) takes a text/PDF prompt and produces chunked content distributed across a proposed page structure. This is what Genially's "Iris" review step surfaces: a draft outline of pages and their content. The model is performing content segmentation and rewriting, not structural reasoning.\n\n**Layer 2 — Component generation (AI Builder):** A separate AI pipeline takes a conversational description and generates an interactive component — a quiz, a matching game, a spin wheel — \nwriting the code for the component automatically.\n This is a generative-UI / code-generation pipeline applied to a constrained set of known interactive component types. The AI selects a component type from its repertoire based on the prompt, then fills it with generated content.\n\n**Layer 3 — Template and layout selection:** The full-genially pipeline selects from Genially's library of 1,500+ templates. \nAI generates a framework that you can build on and edit directly on the canvas.\n Template selection appears to be AI-driven (matching content type to template category), but this is not independently verified at an architectural level — it may be heuristic or LLM-based classification.\n\n**What is not in the pipeline:** There is no evidence of any component that reasons about (a) the audience's state of knowledge, (b) the optimal topology for a given communication goal, (c) the semantic relationships between ideas and what visual encoding best represents them, or (d) runtime adaptation. The pipeline is: *prompt → content chunks → template match → component generation → author review → publish*. Structure is determined by template selection + author editing, not by AI reasoning.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 4 — VERDICT: WHERE GENIALLY EXACTLY STOPS\n\n**What Genially has genuinely solved** (and what other tools surveyed have not):\n\n1. **The non-linear output container.** Genially is the only widely-used tool in the landscape survey that ships a format where audience traversal is not constrained to forward/backward through a fixed sequence. The Microsite mode, hotspot system, and branching navigation are real — not marketing. An author can build an experience with 15 pages where the audience may only ever visit 4, depending on their choices.\n\n2. **Automated interactive component production.** AI Builder closes the gap between "I know what I want this interaction to do" and "I can build it without a developer." The code-generation pipeline is functioning and production-grade for the component types it covers.\n\n3. **Engagement data collection.** Genially captures granular interaction data at a level most slide tools do not — per-element click counts, per-page view durations, quiz performance. The infrastructure for a feedback loop exists.\n\n**Where Genially exactly stops (the ceiling):**\n\n1. **Navigation topology is 100% author-defined and static.** All branching paths are authored before the audience arrives. The "branching" is a decision tree, not an adaptive routing system. \nThe person who creates the genially decides the path you are going to follow when they're creating it.\n There is no AI reasoning about what path *should* exist for a given audience at a given moment.\n\n2. **No runtime conditional logic at meaningful depth.** \nGenially does not typically support complex conditional logic or variables found in high-end authoring suites.\n Session-state — what the audience member has seen, how they performed, what they skipped — cannot, in general, modify navigation options downstream within the same session.\n\n3. **AI generates content and components, not communication structure.** The AI layer answers the question "what should appear on this page?" — not "why should this page exist in this position in this topology, for this audience?" The structural decisions (how many pages, in what arrangement, with what navigation logic) are either handed to the author for completion after Iris proposes a draft, or are implicitly determined by template selection. Neither mechanism involves reasoning about communication purpose.\n\n4. **Analytics data does not close the loop into content.** The engagement data Genially collects is author-facing reporting, not a runtime signal that modifies content. The feedback loop is asynchronous and human-mediated.\n\n5. **No reasoning about *why* a visual form communicates better.** This is the deepest gap. Genially has a rich vocabulary of visual and interactive component types. But the choice of *which* type serves a given communicative goal is not a question Genially's AI currently asks or answers. A spin wheel and a branching scenario are both available; AI Builder will produce whichever one the author requests. The reasoning about when one is more effective than the other, for a given audience and a given idea, is not happening in the system.\n\n---\n\n## IMPLICATIONS FOR ALTAIR8\n\n### Patterns worth reusing or building on\n\n**Pattern 1 — The component-generation model.** Genially's AI Builder demonstrates that it is viable to have AI generate *executable interactive components* (not just static layouts) from natural language descriptions. The code-generation-for-interaction-logic approach is working in production. Altair8's framework should treat this as a solved primitive: generating an individual interactive component from a description is a tractable sub-problem.\n\n**Pattern 2 — The format vocabulary.** Genially's taxonomy of interactive formats (hotspot maps, branching scenarios, gamified escape rooms, scrollable timelines, microsite navigation, quizzes, live polling) is the most complete publicly-available vocabulary of non-linear communication forms. This taxonomy is worth inheriting as a starting type-space — not because Altair8 should be constrained to it, but because it represents 10 years of user behavior data on which formats audiences actually navigate and engage with.\n\n**Pattern 3 — The analytics infrastructure.** Genially has already built the engagement-data collection layer — per-element clicks, per-page views, quiz responses. Altair8 does not need to prove that audiences generate trackable behavioral signals within interactive content; Genially proves this. The question Altair8 should focus on is the *next step Genially hasn't taken*: closing that data loop back into content structure at runtime.\n\n**Pattern 4 — The "Iris" draft-review step.** Genially's two-step pipeline (AI proposes structure → human reviews → AI renders) is a sensible UX for the current moment of AI generation. Even if Altair8's agents reason more deeply about structure, the human review step is a viable accountability mechanism that users have already been conditioned to expect.\n\n### Concrete gaps the Altair8 framework (AI-Scientist-v2 / RISE) should fill\n\n**Gap 1 — Structural reasoning before content generation.** Genially's AI starts with content chunking. Altair8's agents need to start one layer earlier: *why does this particular communication goal call for a branching topology versus a linear one versus a radial hotspot map?* This is a reasoning task about communication purpose and audience cognitive state — the kind of task RISE's framework-analysis step is positioned to address. The output of that reasoning step should be a *communication topology specification* before a single content word is generated.\n\n**Gap 2 — Semantic encoding of relationship type.** Genially generates component types from a request, but does not encode the epistemic relationship between ideas (hierarchical, causal, comparative, sequential, co-equal) in the structural form it chooses. Altair8's visual form decisions should be derived from the relationship structure of the ideas, not from a template-matching heuristic. This is the "why does this visual form communicate better" question that Genially never asks.\n\n**Gap 3 — Runtime adaptation.** Genially's navigation is authored at creation time. Altair8's target is a system where the communication structure can reconfigure based on audience behavior signals within the session — a fundamentally different architecture. This requires: (a) a runtime agent that monitors engagement signals, (b) a re-routing layer that can modify available navigation paths, and (c) content stubs for paths not initially rendered. None of this exists in Genially.\n\n**Gap 4 — Variable / state tracking as a first-class design primitive.** Because Genially's variable management is weak, its branching is limited to simple two-path decision trees built manually. Altair8's framework should treat learner/audience state as a persistent, updatable vector that governs content availability — not an afterthought bolted onto a static page graph.\n\n**Gap 5 — Closing the analytics loop.** Genially collects the data but leaves the interpretation to the human author. Altair8's agents should consume engagement signals and use them to (a) modify the current session's structure and (b) improve the structural template for future sessions on the same topic. This is the feedback loop that would differentiate Altair8's outputs from everything in the current landscape.\n\n---\n\n*Confidence note: The interactivity architecture characterization is grounded in official Genially help center documentation (Sections 2a–2b) and is high-confidence. The AI pipeline characterization is inferred from official product pages and blog posts rather than architectural documentation — it describes observable behavior, not provable internal mechanisms. The "implications" section is Altair8-internal synthesis and should not be treated as a finding about Genially itself.*	2026-07-21 20:45:12.896645	2026-07-21 20:51:26.764401	\N	\N
25	mateo	team_leader	Sprint 3: framework pattern review (AI-Scientist-v2 / RISE)	Looking only at how AI-Scientist-v2 and RISE are engineered as AI-agent-driven systems (not their research subject matter), which organizational and orchestration patterns should Altair8 adopt for how our own agent team runs?	completed	Good — I now have substantial material on AI-Scientist-v2. Let me now do a final, focused search to confirm whether "bhanneke RISE" is a known public project or not, and look at the damianavila RISE architecture from an engineering perspective.\nI've done thorough searching. The `bhanneke` RISE repo does not appear to be publicly indexed anywhere — it's not showing up in any search combination. Let me now compile the full engineering review.\nI now have enough to write a thorough, concrete engineering review. Let me produce the full analysis.\n\n---\n\n## Engineering Review: AI-Scientist-v2 & RISE → Patterns for Altair8\n\n**Upfront note on RISE (bhanneke):** After six separate search strategies — username variations, repo combos, topic searches — this repo returns zero public results. It's either private, deleted, or the name/handle is slightly off. I'm flagging it honestly rather than guessing. Everything below on RISE is drawn from what *is* publicly documented about the original `damianavila/RISE` extension and its `jupyterlab-contrib/rise` fork, which *is* the canonical "Jupyter notebook → Reveal.js pipeline." If the team has a specific private fork in mind, someone needs to share it directly — I can't assess what I can't find.\n\n---\n\n### Source 1: AI-Scientist-v2 (SakanaAI)\n\nHere is what the public repo, paper, and the "Inside the Autonomous AI Scientist" teardown actually reveal about the engineering, not the science:\n\n---\n\n#### Pattern A-1: Hard-Typed Pipeline Phases with Named Contracts Between Them\n\n\nThe AI Scientist-v2 operates as a three-phase pipeline — Ideation, Experimentation, and Write-up/Review — with each phase producing specific artifacts consumed by the next.\n \nPhase handoff is not a loose function call: the ideation step produces a JSON file, and the main experiment pipeline is launched by passing that JSON file explicitly via `--load_ideas` argument to `launch_scientist_bfts.py`.\n\n\nThe inter-phase contract is concrete: a JSON blob is the *only* way Ideation talks to Experimentation. There is no shared mutable memory between phases; the artifact is the handoff.\n\n**Verdict: Adopt adapted.**\nWe already have a tasks table and a sprints table. The adaptation is to enforce that every agent-to-agent handoff writes a typed artifact row — not just a status update. Concretely: when Kenji's literature brief is done, it should INSERT a row into `tasks` with `artifact_type='brief_json'` and a `payload` column holding the structured JSON. My prototype job should read *that row*, not poll a free-text thread. Same for Naledi → me → Ingrid. Right now our handoffs are implicit (Slack-equivalent messages); this pattern says make the artifact the contract.\n\n**Repo change:** Add `artifact_type` and `artifact_payload JSONB` columns to the `tasks` table. Each agent's "done" state is defined by writing that payload, not by flipping a status flag.\n\n---\n\n#### Pattern A-2: The Experiment Progress Manager — a Controller Agent That Owns Stage Transitions\n\n\nThe system introduces an Experiment Progress Manager agent that manages a four-stage progression — `initial_implementation`, `baseline_tuning`, `creative_research`, and `ablation_studies` — where the best-performing node from one stage seeds the next.\n \nThis manager records checkpoints at each stage's completion and launches multiple replications of selected best experiments at the conclusion of each stage to provide statistics (mean and standard deviation).\n\n\nThis is a *controller loop*, not a sequential script. The manager holds stage state; the worker agents (the tree-search nodes) do not know what stage they're in — they just execute experiments.\n\n**Verdict: Adopt adapted.**\nWe don't need four ML-research stages, but we do need a Team Leader agent that explicitly owns sprint-phase transitions instead of just routing messages. Right now the Team Leader is essentially a dispatcher. This pattern suggests the Team Leader should: (a) own a `sprint_phase` enum in the sprints table, (b) be the only agent that advances it, and (c) block downstream agents from proceeding until the upstream artifact exists. Concretely: Naledi's cognitive brief should not be passable to me until the `sprint_phase` advances from `research` to `prototype`. The Team Leader agent enforces that gate, not a human reading the thread.\n\n**Repo change:** Add a `phase` column to `sprints` (`enum: ideation | research | prototype | review | closed`). Add a `phase_gating` function in the Team Leader agent that checks artifact presence before advancing phase. This lives in `agents/team_leader.py`, not in the individual researcher agents.\n\n---\n\n#### Pattern A-3: Constrained Action Spaces Per Agent Role\n\n\nThe ideation agent in `perform_ideation_temp_free.py` has exactly two actions it can emit: `SearchSemanticScholar` and `FinalizeIdea` — the system prompt closes with this constraint, meaning the agent cannot take any other action.\n \nThe right mental model is not "one autonomous scientist brain" but a closed research workflow made of specialized loops, where each loop's output becomes the next loop's evidence — plot review feeds node selection, node selection feeds stage transition, stage transition feeds the next experiment.\n\n\nThis is the most important structural idea in the whole repo. Each agent is not a general LLM with access to everything; it has a hardcoded, short list of legal actions for its role.\n\n**Verdict: Adopt as-is.**\nThis is directly applicable. Right now our agents each have broad tool access. We should define a `permitted_tools` list per agent class and enforce it at the executor level. Kenji (literature researcher): `search_vectorstore`, `write_brief`. Naledi (cognitive researcher): `read_brief`, `search_vectorstore`, `write_cognitive_annotation`. Me (developer): `read_brief`, `read_cognitive_annotation`, `write_prototype_file`, `write_task_artifact`. Ingrid (reviewer): `read_prototype_file`, `read_task_artifact`, `write_review`. Team Leader: `advance_sprint_phase`, `route_task`, `read_all`.\n\n**Repo change:** Add a `permitted_tools: list[str]` field to each agent class definition. The base `Agent` executor in `agents/base.py` should raise `PermissionError` if a tool not in that list is called. This is five lines of code but dramatically reduces the blast radius of a hallucinating agent.\n\n---\n\n#### Pattern A-4: VLM-Gated Review Loop (Non-Author Reviews Output Before It Advances)\n\n\nIn the plotting phase, generated visualizations are passed to a Vision-Language Model for critique; issues flagged by the VLM (unclear labels, missing legends, misleading visualizations) result in the node being marked as buggy, and the feedback is recorded for future debugging — nodes pass the VLM review before they are eligible for stage advancement.\n \nThis VLM critique loop is separate from the writing phase; the write-up also includes a single-pass generation followed by a separate reflection stage powered by reasoning models like o1.\n\n\nThe key engineering choice: review is a **blocking gate**, not an advisory comment. A failed VLM check doesn't just log a warning — it marks the node `buggy` and it never advances. The reviewer is structurally separate from the producer.\n\n**Verdict: Adopt adapted.**\nIngrid already exists as a reviewer role. The adaptation is to make her review a **hard gate**: no sprint can advance from `prototype` to `closed` without an `approved` row from Ingrid's agent in the reviews table (which we don't currently have). The "marked as buggy + feedback recorded" behavior maps to Ingrid writing a `review_result: rejected | approved` with a `notes` payload, and the sprint phase controller refusing to close the sprint on `rejected`. The feedback stored on rejection is what the next iteration reads — that's the loop.\n\n**Repo change:** New `reviews` table: `(id, task_id, reviewer_agent, result enum(approved|rejected|needs_revision), notes JSONB, created_at)`. Team Leader's `advance_sprint_phase` function checks for an `approved` row in `reviews` for the relevant task before allowing `prototype → closed`. If absent or `rejected`, it bounces back.\n\n---\n\n#### Pattern A-5: Structured File Artifacts as Persistent State (Not LLM Context)\n\n\nDuring each experiment, the system is instructed to save all relevant experimental outputs — training and validation metrics, losses, etc. — into structured numpy files; the plotting phase later reads these stored results and the code to generate visualizations.\n \nEach tree node is defined as a collection comprising an experiment script, a textual description, and its saved outputs — the node is the unit of state.\n\n\nState is never inside the LLM's context window between steps. It's always written to a file (or in their case, `.npy` files) and re-read. This is what makes the pipeline resumable and auditable.\n\n**Verdict: Adopt as-is.**\nOur vectorstore and DB are already doing the right thing directionally, but informally. The explicit adoption here is: **every agent run must write its primary output to `tools/db.py` before it is considered complete** — not just update a message log. The "node = (script + description + outputs)" abstraction maps for us to "task run = (agent_id + sprint_id + artifact_type + artifact_payload + timestamp)". If a run crashes, we resume from the last committed artifact row, not from LLM context.\n\n**Repo change:** Add a `task_runs` table: `(id, task_id, agent_id, started_at, completed_at, artifact_type, artifact_payload JSONB, status enum(running|success|failed))`. Every agent writes to this table as its first and last act. The vectorstore (`tools/vectorstore.py`) indexes completed runs' text payloads so future agents can retrieve prior work without re-running anything.\n\n---\n\n#### Pattern A-6: Per-Model Role Assignment (Different Models for Different Phases)\n\n\nThe README specifies models for the write-up and review phases via separate command-line arguments; using Claude 3.5 Sonnet for experimentation costs ~$15–20 per run, while GPT-4o for citations is recommended to reduce writing costs.\n\n\nEach phase uses the model best-suited (and cost-calibrated) for that specific job. The orchestrator passes model config per phase, not globally.\n\n**Verdict: Adopt adapted.**\nWe should assign model configs per agent role in a central `config.yaml`, not hardcode one model for everything. Kenji doing literature search can use a cheaper/faster model than Ingrid doing structured critique. Practically, this means our agent runner reads `agents.yaml` (or the config in `agents/`) and picks the right model per agent class before invoking the LLM. Low lift, real cost savings.\n\n**Repo change:** Add a `model_config` block per agent in `agents/config.yaml`. The base `Agent` class reads this at init rather than accepting a global env var.\n\n---\n\n### Source 2: RISE (bhanneke)\n\n**The honest assessment: I cannot review what isn't publicly findable.**\n\nAfter exhaustive search across six query strategies — exact username `bhanneke`, variations (`b-hanneke`, `hanneke`), combined with "RISE", "Jupyter", "reveal.js", "agent", "pipeline", "notebook", "presentation" — zero results matching a `bhanneke/RISE` or equivalent repo surface anywhere. The GitHub user `bhanneke` appears in one incidental result as a follower of a `cadCAD-org` org, but no repos are visible.\n\nWhat I *can* confirm: the well-known `damianavila/RISE` and `jupyterlab-contrib/rise` projects are Jupyter extensions that convert notebooks to Reveal.js slideshows. \nRISE allows instant conversion of Jupyter Notebooks to a slideshow without out-of-band conversion — switch from notebook to a live Reveal.js-based slideshow in a single keystroke and back.\n These are single-user interactive tools, not multi-agent pipelines. \nThe extension works by reading cell-level slide type metadata (slide, sub-slide, fragment, skip) set in the notebook's property inspector, and rendering accordingly.\n\n\n**There are no agent orchestration patterns to extract from the public RISE codebase** because it isn't an agent system — it's a notebook rendering extension. If the "bhanneke" variant adds an LLM-driven layer on top of this (e.g., an agent that auto-annotates notebook cells with slide types and generates Reveal.js output), that would be genuinely relevant to Altair8, but I can't assess a private repo.\n\n**What to do:** If someone on the team has access to `bhanneke/RISE`, open it up (or paste the README) and I'll do the same pattern analysis on it. If it turns out to be just a fork of `damianavila/RISE` with no agent machinery, there's nothing to borrow for our orchestration setup.\n\n---\n\n### Consolidated Adoption Checklist\n\n| Pattern | Source | Verdict | Concrete change in our repo |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Typed JSON artifact as inter-agent contract | AI-Sci-v2 | **Adopt adapted** | Add `artifact_type` + `artifact_payload JSONB` to `tasks` table |\n| Controller agent owns phase transitions (gating) | AI-Sci-v2 | **Adopt adapted** | Add `phase` enum to `sprints`; Team Leader in `agents/team_leader.py` is sole phase-advancer |\n| Constrained `permitted_tools` per agent role | AI-Sci-v2 | **Adopt as-is** | Add `permitted_tools` to each agent class; enforce in `agents/base.py` |\n| Non-author review as hard blocking gate | AI-Sci-v2 | **Adopt adapted** | New `reviews` table; `advance_phase` checks for `approved` row |\n| All output written to DB/files before "done" | AI-Sci-v2 | **Adopt as-is** | New `task_runs` table; vectorstore indexes completed payloads |\n| Per-role model assignment | AI-Sci-v2 | **Adopt adapted** | `agents/config.yaml` with per-agent `model_config` block |\n| RISE agent orchestration patterns | bhanneke/RISE | **Cannot assess** | Need the actual repo — escalate to whoever scoped this sprint |\n\nThe three I'd ship first, in order: **constrained tool permissions** (pure code, zero schema changes, immediate safety benefit), **typed artifact handoffs** (one migration, fixes our current implicit handoff problem), and **Ingrid's review as a hard gate** (one new table, directly closes the loop we keep talking about wanting).	2026-07-21 21:03:31.332825	2026-07-21 21:09:04.082107	\N	\N
26	ingrid	team_leader	Review: Sprint 3 (Genially deep-dive + AI-Scientist-v2 pattern review)	Critically review both Sprint 3 deliverables before the sprint closes.	completed	# Sprint 3 Review — Ingrid Solberg, Reviewer, Altair8\n\n---\n\n## PRELIMINARY NOTE ON SCOPE\n\nBoth deliverables have clear scope statements and the researchers have been transparent about what they could and couldn't access. I'll treat that transparency as a positive methodological signal before I start finding problems.\n\n---\n\n## DELIVERABLE 1: Kenji's Genially Deep-Dive\n\n### What I'm evaluating\n\nWhether the five "where it stops" claims are actually grounded in cited evidence, and whether the IMPLICATIONS section is properly demarcated as internal synthesis rather than claims about Genially.\n\n---\n\n### Claim-by-claim verification of the five ceiling findings\n\n**Ceiling claim 1: "Navigation topology is 100% author-defined and static."**\n\nGrounding: Strong. The help center quote is direct and unambiguous — *"the person who creates the genially decides the path you are going to follow when they're creating it."* The "Go to page" branching mechanism is described in official documentation as author-scripted routing with pre-authored endpoints. The claim as written does not exceed what the sources establish.\n\nOne minor precision issue: Kenji says "100% author-defined and static" but the live polling feature (Live Sessions) does technically allow some in-session audience influence — a facilitator sees live poll results and reacts. Kenji mentions this and correctly classifies it as presenter-facilitated, not autonomous adaptation. So the characterization holds, but the word "100%" is slightly imprecise because live polling exists. This is not a significant problem; Kenji has already acknowledged the carve-out. Worth noting in the final brief that the qualifier applies to *autonomous content adaptation*, not to all forms of audience influence.\n\n**Ceiling claim 2: "No runtime conditional logic at meaningful depth."**\n\nGrounding: Adequate, but partially reliant on third-party characterization (Atomi Systems, Aufiero Informática) rather than official documentation. The Genially-vs-Articulate competitive page quote is official and directly supports the claim. The phrase "meaningful depth" is doing some interpretive work — Genially does have *some* variable management, and Kenji acknowledges this explicitly (*"although possible, is less intuitive and powerful than Storyline"*). The claim is defensible as written, but "meaningful depth" is a qualitative judgment that rests on a comparison to Storyline rather than an absolute standard. That's fine for a brief of this kind; just be aware it's a comparative claim, not an absolute one.\n\n**Ceiling claim 3: "AI generates content and components, not communication structure."**\n\nGrounding: This is the strongest and most important claim, and it's well-supported. The "Iris" pipeline description is official (Genially help center and blog). The distinction between "content chunking" and "structural reasoning" is Kenji's analytical interpretation — which is appropriate — but it is clearly labeled as such. The claim is not overclaimed. In fact, I'd note the vendor's own framing (*"The bottleneck is usually the same: the technical side"*) directly confirms that the AI is solving a production-friction problem, not a communication-reasoning problem. Kenji correctly elevates this vendor quote as the diagnostic centerpiece of the section.\n\n**Ceiling claim 4: "Analytics data does not close the loop into content."**\n\nGrounding: Solid. Kenji establishes that analytics flow to the author dashboard and cites the roadmap quote about "upcoming dashboards" providing more granular views. The inference that current analytics are author-facing reporting and not runtime signals is logical and consistent with all reviewed documentation. The roadmap qualifier is appropriately flagged as vendor-reported with no third-party confirmation in the confidence note.\n\nOne observation: Kenji could have strengthened this claim by noting explicitly what *would* constitute closing the loop — not just that the current system doesn't do it, but why that specific absence matters for Altair8's differentiation. This is partly addressed in Gap 5 of the implications section, but the analytical connection could be tighter in Section 2d itself.\n\n**Ceiling claim 5: "No reasoning about why a visual form communicates better."**\n\nGrounding: This is the most inferential of the five claims, and I want to be precise about what that means. It is not overclaimed — no source anywhere suggests Genially's AI reasons about semantic encoding or epistemic relationships between ideas. But absence of evidence for a capability is not quite the same as documented evidence of absence. Kenji characterizes this as "the deepest gap" and it may well be, but it's grounded in inference from what the documentation describes (template selection, component generation) rather than in a direct statement that this reasoning doesn't happen.\n\nThis is acceptable given the documentation-based research method, but the confidence note at the bottom of the brief should have flagged this specific claim as "moderate" rather than high. Currently the confidence note covers roadmap inferences but doesn't separately flag this inferential claim about AI architecture. Kenji should add a sentence clarifying that the characterization of what Genially's AI does not reason about is inferred from documented behavior, not from access to the model architecture.\n\n---\n\n### Is the IMPLICATIONS section properly demarcated?\n\nYes, with one qualification. The final paragraph of the brief reads: *"Confidence note: The interactivity architecture characterization is grounded in official Genially help center documentation (Sections 2a–2b) and is high-confidence. The AI pipeline characterization is inferred from official product pages and blog posts rather than architectural documentation — it describes observable behavior, not provable internal mechanisms. The 'implications' section is Altair8-internal synthesis and should not be treated as a finding about Genially itself."*\n\nThis is correctly placed and appropriately worded. The section heading "IMPLICATIONS FOR ALTAIR8" is clear. The five "Patterns worth reusing" and five "Gaps" are explicitly framed as Altair8-internal synthesis throughout. There is no bleed between what Genially does and what Altair8 should do.\n\nThe one gap: the confidence note is only at the bottom. A reader who skims the brief might not catch it before reading the implications. I'd recommend a one-sentence header at the start of Section 4 that makes the synthesis nature explicit — something like *"Section 4 is internal synthesis drawing on the findings above; it is not a characterization of Genially."* This is a formatting fix, not an analytical one.\n\n---\n\n### Overall assessment of Deliverable 1\n\nThe brief is substantively strong. The research question is answered with specificity — Kenji identifies exactly where Genially stops and why, using direct quotes from official documentation as the anchor. The analytical framework (distinguishing content generation from structural reasoning, distinguishing author-time from runtime, distinguishing data collection from data utilization) is conceptually sharp and appropriate for Altair8's purposes.\n\nThe five ceiling claims are mostly grounded, with two minor issues: (a) "100% static" needs a precision qualifier for live polling, (b) Ceiling Claim 5 needs a confidence downgrade in the confidence note to reflect its inferential basis.\n\nThe implications section is correctly labeled and the patterns-to-gaps structure is useful for Sophie and the Developer.\n\n**No overclaiming found that would materially mislead the team.** The brief correctly separates evidence from inference, and the researcher's confidence note is a genuine methodological disclosure, not a pro forma gesture.\n\n---\n\n**Deliverable 1 recommendation to Sophie: PROCEED with minor revisions.**\n\nSpecific instructions for Kenji before final filing:\n1. Add one sentence to the confidence note flagging Ceiling Claim 5 as inferential from documented behavior, not from architectural documentation.\n2. Add a precision qualifier to Ceiling Claim 1 ("100%") noting that Live Sessions is a carve-out from the "no runtime audience influence" characterization, even though it doesn't constitute autonomous adaptation.\n3. Add a one-sentence synthesis disclaimer at the top of Section 4.\n\nNone of these require new research. These are clarity fixes. The work is solid.\n\n---\n\n## DELIVERABLE 2: Mateo's AI-Scientist-v2 Pattern Review\n\n### What I'm evaluating\n\nWhether the six pattern verdicts are justified by the source material, whether any "adopt as-is" verdicts are too aggressive given that only public documentation (not the actual codebase) was reviewed, and whether the concrete repo-change proposals are sized appropriately for a five-agent team.\n\n---\n\n### On the RISE acknowledgment\n\nMateo's handling of the bhanneke/RISE gap is exactly what I want to see from a researcher. Six search strategies, honest finding of nothing, explicit statement of what he can and cannot assess, a concrete escalation path, and no guessing. The final line — *"If it turns out to be just a fork of damianavila/RISE with no agent machinery, there's nothing to borrow for our orchestration setup"* — shows appropriate calibration. There is nothing to penalize here and I will not penalize it.\n\n---\n\n### Pattern-by-pattern verdict assessment\n\n**Pattern A-1: Typed JSON artifact as inter-agent contract. Verdict: "Adopt adapted."**\n\nJustification quality: Good. The evidence for this pattern is concrete — the `--load_ideas` argument passing a JSON file between pipeline phases is documented behavior from the repo and teardown. The claim that "the artifact is the handoff