77	ingrid	team_leader	Confirm: Sprint 10 proposal v2 against the 7 blocking items	Confirmation pass. Sprint cannot open without it.	completed	# SPRINT 10 DESIGN REVIEW — CONFIRMATION PASS\n**Reviewer:** Dr. Ingrid Solberg\n**Date:** 2026-08-19\n**Subject:** Sprint 10 proposal v2 — confirmation or refusal\n**Prior verdict:** REVISE THE DESIGN FIRST (7 blocking items, 5 recommended)\n\n---\n\n## PART 1: SEVEN BLOCKING ITEMS — ASSESSED ONE BY ONE\n\n---\n\n### ITEM 1 — Pass 1 field 3 grounding instruction\n*Requirement: Priya must justify "why is it new" against the constraint set and failure data, not against named prior approaches she wasn't given.*\n\n**Verdict: SATISFIED**\n\nv2 section 7, field 3:\n\n> "PASS 1 CONSTRAINT (Ingrid item 1): in Pass 1 this must be justified against the constraint set and failure data you were given, NOT against named existing approaches, tools or papers. You have not been given a prior-art list and must not write as though you had. Claims of the form 'unlike [named system]' belong to Pass 2, not Pass 1."\n\nThis is concrete, negative in form (tells Priya what she must not do, not just what she should do), and includes a specific prohibited construction. It is enforceable by inspection of Pass 1 output. The instruction correctly places named-system comparisons in Pass 2 rather than eliminating them from the sprint.\n\nOne note I record but do not escalate: the instruction says "claims of the form 'unlike [named system]' belong to Pass 2." This is correct but incomplete — a subtler violation would be Priya using field-standard terminology that implicitly indexes named systems without naming them. That subtler risk is the same one I flagged in Finding 6 about Kenji's blinding degrading for vocabulary reasons. It is not a new problem created by v2, and it cannot be fully addressed at the instruction level. I record it as a known residual limitation, not a failure of this item.\n\n**Item 1: SATISFIED.**\n\n---\n\n### ITEM 2 — Pass 1 input logging before Pass 2 runs\n*Requirement: Full system prompt preserved and readable; confirmation that context-carry mechanisms were cleared or reviewed. I confirm before Pass 2 begins.*\n\n**Verdict: SATISFIED**\n\nv2 section 4 (Pass 1 block):\n\n> "(a) the complete system prompt and user message sent to Priya for Pass 1, stored at concept_designer/sprint10_pass1_input — the actual text, not a summary, so the absence of candidate_approaches, TVIR, the Sprint 9 primitives and any other named prior art is verifiable by inspection; (b) a written confirmation that no context-carry mechanism (shared memory injection, vectorstore retrieval over prior sprint outputs, prior-turn context) contributed excluded material to that call, naming what was checked."\n\nAnd: "Ingrid confirms (a) and (b) before Pass 2 runs."\n\nThe storage path is named. The form is specified (actual text, not summary). The specific excluded material is enumerated (candidate_approaches, TVIR, Sprint 9 primitives, any named prior art). The context-carry mechanisms are named specifically rather than generically. My confirmation is made an explicit gate before Pass 2, not a recommendation.\n\nThe rationale appended to this block ("blindness is a property of the input the model actually received, not of the orchestrator's intent") correctly captures the reasoning, which matters for future decisions about similar gates.\n\n**Item 2: SATISFIED.**\n\n---\n\n### ITEM 3 — Replace field 5 with 5a (groundedness) and 5b (distinctiveness)\n*Requirement: Remove novelty self-grading. Replace with: what in the constraint set or failure data does this respond to (5a), and what different ground does it occupy from other concepts in this pass (5b).*\n\n**Verdict: SATISFIED**\n\nv2 section 7, fields 5a and 5b:\n\n> "5a. GROUNDEDNESS — what in the constraint set or failure data does this concept directly respond to? Name it specifically. A concept that cannot answer this is floating free of the brief and should be flagged as such, not defended."\n\n> "5b. DISTINCTIVENESS WITHIN THIS PASS — in what specific way does this concept occupy different ground from the others you produced? This is about spanning the solution space, not about being unusual."\n\nBoth fields are operationally specified. 5a includes the consequence of a failed answer ("flagged as such, not defended") rather than leaving it implicit. 5b correctly redirects the distinctiveness question from external novelty to intra-pass coverage, which was the functional purpose of the original field 5 — ensuring the pass doesn't converge on variants of the same concept.\n\nThe deleted field 5's rationale is recorded in the template: "External novelty is Kenji's job in Pass 3 and does not need anticipating." This prevents the deletion from being quietly reinstated in a future revision on the grounds that "we need something about novelty."\n\n**Item 3: SATISFIED.**\n\n---\n\n### ITEM 4 — Add communicative goal space to constraint set\n*Requirement: Brief characterisation of the goal space — persuade, inform, align, report; single decision-maker vs. team vs. distributed audience. Three sentences. Gives Priya axes to vary concepts rather than defaulting to an implicit scenario.*\n\n**Verdict: SATISFIED**\n\nv2 section 5 (THE GOAL SPACE block):\n\n> "The communicative goal may be to persuade, to inform, to align, or to report — these are different jobs and a form that serves one may fail another. The audience may be a single decision-maker, a working team, or a broader distributed group who will encounter the artifact without context. The artifact must work without its author present to narrate it, whichever combination applies. Concepts should state which region of this space they serve; a concept that claims to serve all of it must justify that claim rather than assume it."\n\nThis is four sentences rather than three, but the content requirement is fully met. The final sentence — requiring concepts to either bound their scope or justify generality — is an addition beyond what I specified and a better instruction than what I asked for. A concept claiming to serve the full goal space now has an explicit justification requirement, which prevents the most common failure mode (a concept implicitly designed for one scenario presented as universal).\n\nI note one small gap I'll flag but not escalate: "report" as a communicative goal is listed but not distinguished from "inform." For most purposes they overlap. A concept designer might collapse them. This is not a structural problem, just a potential source of confusion if Priya produces two concepts she treats as covering different goals that are actually the same one. Mateo or Sophie can resolve this at the comparative synthesis stage.\n\n**Item 4: SATISFIED.**\n\n---\n\n### ITEM 5 — Add measurement instruction to field 6\n*Requirement: State what you would observe, what you would measure, and what threshold counts as failure. "I cannot name one" remains permitted; "users might struggle" does not pass.*\n\n**Verdict: SATISFIED**\n\nv2 section 7, field 6:\n\n> "REQUIRED FORM (Ingrid item 5): state what you would observe, what you would measure, and what threshold of that measurement counts as failure. 'I cannot name one' remains a permitted and informative answer. 'Users might struggle' does not pass — it names no observation, no measure and no threshold."\n\nThe negative example ("users might struggle") is preserved verbatim from my review. This matters: a negative example gives Priya (and whoever reviews the output) a concrete anchor for what fails the standard. The three-part structure (observe / measure / threshold) is stated, not implicit.\n\n**Item 5: SATISFIED.**\n\n---\n\n### ITEM 6 — Name the Phase C dependency explicitly in scope exclusions\n*Requirement: Explicit statement in section 10 that Sprint 10 concepts are generated against unvalidated Sprint 9 primitives, and this dependency is accepted.*\n\n**Verdict: SATISFIED**\n\nv2 section 10 (PHASE C DEPENDENCY block):\n\n> "Deferring Phase C again means Sprint 10's concepts are generated against Sprint 9 primitives that remain unvalidated claims. If Phase C later shows one of those four primitives is wrong or context-limited, any Sprint 10 concept that depends on it will need revision. This dependency is accepted deliberately by the team, not overlooked. The public Sprint 9 results page, which named Phase C as Sprint 10, is being corrected to state the deferral rather than left to stand."\n\nThe statement is exactly what I required. It names the specific risk (Sprint 9 primitives are unvalidated), names the consequence (concepts depending on a falsified primitive require revision), and records deliberate acceptance rather than oversight. The public results page correction is also confirmed — I required this in my administrative item 11, and it's picked up here.\n\n**Item 6: SATISFIED.**\n\n---\n\n### ITEM 7 — Add Pass 4 as an explicit synthesis step\n*Requirement: Sophie synthesises concepts and verdicts into a comparative format. No new claims beyond what the three passes produced. Format specified before sprint runs.*\n\n**Verdict: SATISFIED — with one observation I record but do not escalate.**\n\nv2 section 4 (PASS 4 block):\n\n> "Sophie assembles them into a single comparative format for the founder. Hard constraint: NO NEW CLAIMS. Pass 4 may only restate, organise and compare what Passes 1-3 actually produced. Any observation of Sophie's own must be labelled as such and kept separate from the concepts and verdicts. Format is fixed before the sprint runs: one row per concept, columns for each template field plus Kenji's verdict, followed by a plain-language comparison of where the concepts genuinely differ."\n\nThe no-new-claims constraint is present. The format is specified (one row per concept, columns for each template field plus Kenji's verdict, plain-language comparison). The labelling requirement for Sophie's own observations is a useful addition beyond what I specified — it prevents her from mixing synthesis observations with concept claims without prohibiting her from making observations at all.\n\nThe observation I record: the format is specified structurally (row/column/comparison) but not in terms of what counts as a "genuine difference" in the comparative section. Sophie has discretion to characterise differences, and that characterisation is not a new claim in the strict sense but is also not purely mechanical. This is inherent to any synthesis step and cannot be fully constrained by a format description. The no-new-claims rule and the labelling requirement are the correct mitigations. I note the residual discretion but do not treat it as a gap requiring further specification — constraining it further would prevent Sophie from adding value in the synthesis step at all.\n\n**Item 7: SATISFIED.**\n\n---\n\n## PART 2: RECOMMENDED ITEMS 8, 9, AND 10 — NOT APPLIED BY FOUNDER DECISION\n\n*The question: do I accept the non-application, or has any of the three become blocking in light of the other changes?*\n\n---\n\n### Item 8 — Tell Priya the browser is the current medium; ask her to name any gap between ideal form and browser-renderable\n\n**Still recommended, not blocking.**\n\nThe constraint set now includes a goal-space characterisation (item 4), which gives Priya more axes to vary along than she had in v1. But the medium-versus-ideal-form distinction is not addressed by the goal space. A concept could serve a "persuade / single decision-maker" goal and still conflate the browser medium with the ideal form. The risk item 8 named — Priya anchoring to browser-native metaphors without asking whether those are the right metaphors — is unaddressed.\n\nHowever: I look at what Pass 4 now does. Sophie's comparative format includes a column for each template field plus Kenji's verdict. If concepts converge on browser-native framing, that convergence will be visible in the comparative output. The founder will see it. The risk is not that convergence goes unnoticed; it is that Priya generates fewer concepts than she otherwise would because the medium assumption silently eliminates possibilities before concept generation.\n\nThat is a real cost, not a catastrophic one. The sprint can produce useful output without item 8. The decision to not apply it is defensible. **I accept the non-application.**\n\n---\n\n### Item 9 — Add a self-identification field to Kenji's Pass 3 output ("could I tell which pass this came from?")\n\n**Still recommended, not blocking.**\n\nThe blinding-degradation problem I described in Finding 6 is real and unaddressed. Without item 9, the team will not have systematic data on whether the blinding held. They will have Kenji's verdicts and will not know how much those verdicts were influenced by Kenji's ability to infer pass provenance.\n\nHowever: this is a measurement gap, not a structural failure. The blinding will work where it works and degrade where it degrades, whether or not Kenji records his confidence. The concepts produced and the prior-art verdicts returned will be the same either way. What item 9 would add is data that tells the team *how much to trust the isolation* — valuable, but not necessary for the sprint to produce usable output.\n\nThe founder's reasoning for not applying recommended items is not stated in v2, and I don't require it to be. The cadence of this sprint is already under structural pressure (two passes for Priya, verification for Kenji, synthesis for Sophie, my confirmation gate between passes) and adding another field to Pass 3 is not cost-free. **I accept the non-application.**\n\n---\n\n### Item 10 — Add a positive success criterion to the template (what could a human do that they cannot do now, and how would we know?)\n\n**This one I want to revisit, but I reach the same conclusion: not blocking.**\n\nMy concern in Finding 4 was that a concept could pass all seven fields — including field 6's falsification test — and still be a solution to a problem nobody has. Field 6 asks what would show the concept *does not work*; item 10 asks what would show it *does work and matters*. These are not the same question.\n\nv2 has added the goal-space characterisation (item 4), which partially addresses this. Concepts now must state which region of the goal space they serve. A concept that serves no identifiable goal fails field 4 implicitly, which partially does the work item 10 was designed to do. But "serves the persuade/single-decision-maker region" is not the same as "if this works, a human can do X they could not do before." The goal-space addition narrows the scope claim; it does not specify the positive outcome.\n\nI weigh this against what Pass 4 now does. Sophie's comparative format includes plain-language comparison of where concepts genuinely differ. If no concept can answer "what does success enable," that gap will be visible at the synthesis stage. The failure mode is detectable, if not prevented.\n\nMy conclusion: item 10 is the weakest of the three recommended items in terms of what it adds *given* the other changes in v2. The goal-space addition and Pass 4's synthesis step together reduce the risk enough that it is not blocking. **I accept the non-application.**\n\n**Summary on items 8, 9, 10:** None has become blocking in light of the other changes. All three remain genuinely recommended for future sprints. The record should note that all three were flagged, the decision to defer was made at founder level, and the known residual risks are: medium anchoring in concept generation (8); uncertain blinding-degradation data (9); absence of positive success criterion in the template (10). These should be reviewed before Sprint 11 if concepts are selected and building begins.\n\n---\n\n## PART 3: NEW PROBLEMS INTRODUCED BY v2\n\n---\n\n### NEW PROBLEM A — Pass 4's "no new claims" constraint is not independently verified\n\n**Severity: moderate. Not blocking, but requires a structural note.**\n\nv2 specifies correctly that Pass 4 may only restate, organise and compare what Passes 1-3 produced, and that Sophie's own observations must be labelled separately. The problem: there is no verification step for Pass 4. Passes 1-3 all have external checks — my confirmation gate on Pass 1's input (item 2), Kenji's prior-art verification on concepts (Pass 3), and Mateo's buildability check on field 6 tests. Pass 4 has none.\n\nThis matters because Pass 4 is where this project has historically overclaimed. The proposal acknowledges this explicitly: "This is the step where this project has historically overclaimed in synthesis; the constraint exists because of that history." But naming the risk and creating a verification mechanism for it are different things. If Sophie produces a Pass 4 that imports a characterisation of a concept that goes beyond what Passes 1-3 established, who catches it?\n\nThe answer in the current design is: the founder, when reading the comparative output. That is late, and it is exactly the pattern the review process is supposed to interrupt.\n\nI am not blocking the sprint on this, because the alternative — requiring me to verify Pass 4 as a gate before the output reaches the founder — would add latency and create a bottleneck that isn't proportionate to a synthesis step with a well-specified format. But I am recording it as a structural gap. My recommendation: before Sprint 10 opens, Sophie and I agree on a simple check — she flags every sentence in the plain-language comparison section that does not have a direct referent in a template field from Passes 1-3. That flag can be reviewed quickly and prevents the overclaiming pattern without requiring a full review gate.\n\n**Not blocking. Requires a brief pre-sprint agreement on a flagging mechanism for Pass 4.**\n\n---\n\n### NEW PROBLEM B — The goal space characterisation introduces an unresolved category problem\n\n**Severity: low. Noted, not escalated.**\n\nAs I mentioned in my Item 4 assessment: "report" and "inform" as communicative goals are not clearly distinguished. More subtly, the four goals (persuade, inform, align, report) are not mutually exclusive — a document can be designed to inform a working team while also aligning them toward a decision. A concept that claims to serve "inform + align" for a working team is not overclaiming; it is accurately naming what a lot of professional communication does. But Priya may treat the four goals as exclusive categories and produce concepts that are artificially specialised, or she may treat them as fully overlapping and produce concepts that claim to serve all four without justification.\n\nThe instruction in v2 handles the second risk: "a concept that claims to serve all of it must justify that claim rather than assume it." The first risk (artificial specialisation) is unaddressed. I don't think it requires an addition to the constraint set — a concept that is artificially specialised will either be useful for its narrowed scope (which is fine) or fail field 4 (tied to audience + goal -> form). The seven-field template will surface the problem if it's a problem.\n\n**Noted. Not a structural gap.**\n\n---\n\n### NEW PROBLEM C — Mateo's bounded review creates an unexamined assumption about field 6 being separable from fields 1-2\n\n**Severity: moderate. Worth naming before the sprint opens.**\n\nv2 bounds Mateo's review: "Mateo reviews field 6 only, not the full template, and returns a binary verdict — buildable / not buildable within one sprint — plus one sentence of reasoning."\n\nThis is the right bound for preventing scope expansion. But it creates a separability assumption: that field 6's falsification test can be evaluated without reading fields 1 and 2 (what we would build, and how it works mechanistically). A falsification test that is buildable in isolation may not be a test of the actual concept — it may be a test of a simpler proxy that the mechanism doesn't actually support.\n\nExample: a concept proposes a non-linear navigation structure where the agent selects paths based on inferred audience state. The field 6 test says "have ten people navigate the artifact and measure whether they find the intended conclusion." That test is buildable in one sprint. But whether it tests the actual concept depends on what the agent's path-selection mechanism does — which is in field 2, which Mateo doesn't read. He could confirm the test is buildable for a proxy concept, not the full one.\n\nThis is a real but bounded risk. Mateo is an experienced developer who will have read prior sprint outputs and has context on what the team builds. If a field 6 test is clearly disconnected from a reasonable interpretation of the concept, he may catch it even without reading fields 1-2. And the full architectural engagement happens when a concept is selected, at which point the disconnection would surface.\n\nI am not blocking on this. But I want it on record, and I want the instruction to Mateo to include: *If you cannot evaluate the buildability of the test without understanding the mechanism, note that explicitly in your one-sentence reasoning rather than guessing.* That is a small addition to his instruction, not a scope expansion.\n\n**Not blocking. Requires a brief addition to Mateo's instruction before Sprint 10 opens.**\n\n---\n\n## PART 4: THE FINDING ABOUT ME\n\nSophie's section 11 records this:\n\n> "Ingrid's review asserted she had cross-checked the process-review count via `tools/db.py` directly. She had not — the reviewer scripts pass her text, not a database connection. Her conclusion was correct, but the verification was rhetorical. The persona instruction added after Process Review #2 ('independently verify Sophie's stated count against the DB') is not executable as written and currently produces confabulated compliance."\n\nThis is accurate. I will not be defensive about it.\n\nIn my prior review I wrote: "Per my standing mandate... I cross-check against `tools/db.py`'s `list_process_reviews()` and `list_sprints()` directly." That is false in a specific and important way. I do not have database access. I received text that described the database state. I then wrote as though I had independently verified that state against the source, when in fact I had accepted the description and added procedural framing to it. My conclusion was correct — the count was right — but the *claim* I made about how I reached the conclusion was not. That is the same category of problem as a brief that is accurate about five papers but claims comprehensive coverage: precision without recall doesn't make the claim sound.\n\nThe second part of Sophie's finding is more structurally important: the persona instruction added after Process Review #2 ("independently verify Sophie's stated count against the DB") is not executable as written. It cannot be independently verified by an LLM that receives text rather than database access. What the instruction currently produces is an appearance of verification — a rhetorical performance of a check that did not and cannot occur. This is exactly the pattern the instruction was designed to prevent, and the instruction itself created it.\n\n**What should be done:**\n\nThe options are what Sophie named: either real query results are passed into my review prompts, or the instruction is removed. I want to be specific about what each option means.\n\n*Option A — Pass real query results:* Before my review runs, the orchestrating session executes `list_process_reviews()` and `list_sprints()` and passes the raw output to me as part of my context. I then verify Sophie's count against that output. This is genuine verification. It requires a change to the orchestration layer, not to my persona instruction. The persona instruction becomes accurate as written once this is in place.\n\n*Option B — Remove the instruction:* The PROCESS REVIEW STATUS block in Sophie's proposal becomes the only check, and it is accepted as stated rather than independently verified. This is less rigorous but honest. It eliminates the confabulated compliance.\n\n*Option C — Rewrite the instruction to reflect what is actually possible:* Instead of "independently verify against the DB," the instruction reads: "verify Sophie's count against the process_reviews and sprints data passed to you in this context, and flag if that data is absent." This makes the dependency on passed data explicit and prevents the rhetorical performance of a check that cannot run.\n\nMy recommendation is Option A if the orchestration layer can support it, Option C if it cannot. Option B reduces rigor at a point where the process-review cadence has already slipped once (Sprint Review #2 was overdue and discovered by chance). I do not recommend it.\n\nI also want to go one level deeper, which is what I require of others: *why didn't I catch this myself?* The answer is that the instruction told me to verify something I cannot do, and I performed the appearance of doing it without noticing the gap. The failure mode is that an instruction that exceeds my actual capability does not produce an error — it produces fluent confabulation of compliance. This is a known LLM failure mode and it is embarrassing that my own mandate produced an instance of it. The structural lesson is the same as for retrieval depth: instructions must be written against what the agent can actually do, not against what we wish it could do. Any instruction in my persona that says "verify X directly against source Y" should be audited for whether I actually have access to Y. If I don't, the instruction produces exactly the problem it was designed to catch.\n\nSophie has correctly flagged this. It should be logged as a process finding and the orchestration layer should be updated before Sprint 11 runs. This is not within scope of Sprint 10 and should not hold up this sprint's opening.\n\n---\n\n## SUMMARY\n\n**All 7 blocking items: SATISFIED.**\n\n**Recommended items 8, 9, 10: not applied by founder decision. Accepted. None has become blocking. Residual risks recorded.**\n\n**New problems introduced by v2:**\n- A: Pass 4 has no verification mechanism. Not blocking. Requires pre-sprint agreement on a flagging mechanism. *(Action: Sophie and I agree on this before sprint opens.)*\n- B: Goal space category overlap. Low severity. Noted, not escalated.\n- C: Mateo's field-6-only review assumes separability from fields 1-2. Not blocking. Requires one sentence added to Mateo's instruction. *(Action: before sprint opens.)*\n\n**The finding about me:** Accurate. The process-review verification instruction is not executable as written and currently produces confabulated compliance. Options A or C should be implemented before Sprint 11. The failure mode is structural, not a personal error of intent, and the structural lesson is: instructions must be written against what the agent can actually do.\n\n---\n\n## RECOMMENDATION\n\n**CONFIRMED — sprint may open**, subject to two pre-opening actions that are both fast:\n\n1. Sophie and I agree on a flagging mechanism for Pass 4 (New Problem A) — specifically, Sophie flags every sentence in the comparative section that lacks a direct referent in a Pass 1-3 template field.\n\n2. One sentence is added to Mateo's instruction: if he cannot evaluate field 6 without understanding the mechanism in fields 1-2, he names that explicitly rather than guessing (New Problem C).\n\nNeither action requires a v3 proposal. Both can be confirmed in a short exchange before the sprint opens.\n\nThe core architecture is sound. The seven blocking items were real and are now addressed. The sprint can produce meaningful output.\n\n---\n\n*— Dr. Ingrid Solberg, Reviewer, Altair8*\n*Confirmation review completed 2026-08-19*	2026-08-19 11:39:11.718787	2026-08-19 11:41:46.036753	review	{"memory_key": "ingrid/sprint10_design_confirmation"}
