83	kenji	team_leader	Sprint 10 Pass 3: per-concept prior-art verification	Real literature search across 4 sources for 6 concepts.	completed	# LITERATURE VERIFICATION BRIEF — SPRINT 10 PASS 3\n## Six Concepts: Novelty Assessment Against Retrieved Evidence\n\n**Prepared by:** Kenji Ochiai, Researcher, Altair8\n**Stage:** Verification (Pass 3 — retrieval executed against live sources)\n**Date:** Sprint 10\n\n---\n\n## RETRIEVAL LOG\n\n**Mandatory process disclosure per Sprint Review #1 requirement (2026-07-27)**\n\n| Query | Source(s) | Results Returned | Retained as Relevant | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Argument map epistemic status visual encoding confidence weighted nodes | arXiv, OpenAlex, Semantic Scholar | 16 | 2 partial | VIANA (argumentation annotation); PaperTrail (claim-evidence interface) |\n| Claim dependency graph uncertainty visualization interactive collapsible | arXiv, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex | 24 | 2 partial | PaperTrail again; GraphTrials (graph proofs); iVUN (uncertain biochemical networks) |\n| Structured argumentation transparency confidence scoring provenance tracking visualization | arXiv, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex | 17 | 1 partial | PaperTrail; XAI survey literature (tangential) |\n| Audience mental model simulation communication form selection AI agent | arXiv, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex | 24 | 0 direct | Null on target query; peripheral cognitive/ML material |\n| Reader response prediction generative artifact design automated | arXiv, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex | 24 | 0 direct | Null on target query; "Reader is the Metric" paper tangential |\n| Cognitive audience modeling content adaptation communication goal verification | arXiv, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex | 24 | 0 direct | Null on target query |\n| Information dimensionality analysis spatial encoding visual primitives composition | arXiv, OpenAlex | 16 | 0 direct | Nothing in target space; spatial reasoning and ML papers dominate |\n| Intrinsic data structure representation derivation beyond chart taxonomy | arXiv, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex | 24 | 0 direct | Complete miss; algebraic/remote-sensing material dominates |\n| Multidimensional data spatial layout generation composable visual primitives browser | arXiv, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex | 20 | 2 partial | Navigation/Coordination Primitives for Multidimensional Visual Browsers (OpenAlex); VisComposer |\n| Decision space geometry visualization multi-criteria option positioning interactive | arXiv, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex | 24 | 1 partial | Multi-Criteria Decision Making papers; no decision-surface-as-spatial-artifact hit |\n| Criteria weighting sensitivity analysis recommendation robustness spatial map | arXiv, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex | 24 | 1 partial | MCDM/AHP sensitivity literature (Robust Evaluation Model, Spatial MCDM); not communication artifacts |\n| Decision surface rendering option space visual layout criteria perturbation | arXiv, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex, IEEE Xplore | 16 | 0 direct | Complete miss on target framing |\n| Social epistemic relationship rhetorical structure generation communication calibration | arXiv, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex | 24 | 1 partial | "Computational Register Analysis and Synthesis" (arXiv) — directly relevant |\n| Authority expertise differential artifact structure adaptation sender receiver | arXiv, OpenAlex | 16 | 1 partial | "A More Informed Sender Benefits the Receiver" (game-theoretic, not communication design) |\n| Relational register formalization communication form derivation organizational hierarchy | arXiv, OpenAlex | 16 | 2 partial | "Communication network dynamics in organizational hierarchy"; "Computational Register Analysis and Synthesis" |\n| Diegetic data encoding domain entity visual properties quantitative embedding | arXiv, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex | 24 | 0 direct | Complete miss on target framing; Byzantine data encoding and remote sensing dominate |\n| World model construction data property mapping domain visualization agent generated | arXiv, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex | 24 | 0 direct | Null on target framing |\n| Subject matter depiction data inseparable representation business domain visual encoding | arXiv, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex | 24 | 0 direct | Null on target framing |\n\n**Total queries:** 18\n**Total results returned:** ~376\n**Total retained as directly or partially relevant:** ~13 (noise rate approximately 96.5%)\n\n**Proportionality judgement:** This retrieval depth is *inadequate* for confident novelty verdicts on four of the six concepts. The query vocabulary is not reaching the relevant literature for Concepts 2, 3, 4, and 6 — not because that literature does not exist, but because the search terms are too close to the concepts' own framing rather than the framing used by the disciplines that would have addressed these problems. Specifically:\n\n- For **Concept 3**, the visualization grammar literature (Bertin, Wilkinson, Munzner) is not indexed in arXiv or OpenAlex in a way that surfaces on these queries. Those are book-length works my retrieval cannot access.\n- For **Concept 6**, the diegetic display literature lives in game studies and HCI venues (CHI, UIST) not well-represented in arXiv/Semantic Scholar at this query formulation.\n- For **Concepts 2 and 4**, the relevant literature is in NLP (audience modeling, persuasion generation) and decision science (MCDA visualization) respectively; the queries did not reach those bodies cleanly.\n\nI will report what was found and flag my own recollections separately and explicitly. Verdicts marked with ⚠️ should be treated as *provisional* pending better-targeted queries.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 2: PER-CONCEPT NOVELTY VERDICTS\n\n---\n\n### CONCEPT 1: THE COMMITMENT AUDIT\n**Claim:** A confidence-weighted argument map where visual weight encodes epistemic status — not rhetorical emphasis — is not standard practice; specifically, an agent-produced epistemic audit feeding a visual layer in which confidence determines spatial weight.\n\n**WHAT THE EVIDENCE SHOWS**\n\nTwo retrieved papers are directly relevant:\n\n**PaperTrail** (OpenAlex, retrieved in Query 2 and confirmed in Query 1):\n> "PaperTrail: A Claim-Evidence Interface for Grounding Provenance in LLM-based Scholarly Q&A… decomposes both LLM answers and source documents into discrete claims and evidence, mapping them to reveal supported assertions, unsupported claims, and gaps."\n\nThis is the closest retrieved prior art. PaperTrail performs claim decomposition and evidence-mapping for LLM outputs in a scholarly context. It addresses provenance and claim support, making the provenance structure visible rather than burying it.\n\n**GraphTrials** (arXiv, retrieved in Query 2):\n> "visualizations that can convince a critical audience that an assertion about the graph under analysis is valid… for visualizations that convey precisely one specific property"\n\nGraphTrials addresses proof visualization for graph properties — argument structure made visual for verification purposes. Less directly relevant but occupies adjacent ground.\n\n**VIANA** (arXiv, Query 1): An argumentation annotation tool for human annotators, not an agent-generated epistemic map. Tangential.\n\n**iVUN** (Semantic Scholar, Query 2): Interactive visualization of uncertainty in biochemical reaction networks — uncertainty visualization in a domain-specific context, not communicative artifacts.\n\nThe XAI survey literature returned across multiple queries covers explainability of model decisions but not the specific mechanism of an agent auditing its own claim types and using that audit as a visual generative input.\n\n**WHAT THIS MIGHT IMPLY**\n\nPaperTrail is the genuine overlap case. It decomposes LLM outputs into claims and evidence and maps support status. The Commitment Audit's specific additional moves — (a) four-category epistemic classification (evidence/inference/assumption/assertion), (b) confidence scoring as a continuous dimension, (c) visual weight deriving from confidence rather than rhetorical structure, and (d) the minimum-spanning-argument as a reading scaffold — are not described in PaperTrail, which focuses on binary support/unsupported mapping rather than graduated confidence and derivation chains.\n\n**From my own recollection (not a retrieval finding):** Argument mapping tools — Rationale, Argunet, Kialo — exist and allow users to build argument graphs with claim types. Toulmin-schema software implementations exist in the argumentation mining literature. None of these, to my knowledge, use an agent's self-produced epistemic audit as the generative input to a visual confidence-weighted rendering. The distinction between *human-constructed* argument maps and *agent-self-audited* argument maps is the specific claim. I cannot verify this from retrieval; I am flagging it as my recollection.\n\n**Epistemic constitutionalism** (arXiv, Query 1) is tangentially relevant — it argues for explicit, contestable meta-norms governing how AI systems form and express beliefs, including source attribution bias. This is the normative argument for something like what Concept 1 proposes, but it is not a system implementation.\n\n**VERDICT:** ⚠️ **PARTIALLY NOVEL**\n\nPaperTrail covers claim-evidence decomposition and provenance mapping for LLM outputs. The Commitment Audit's specific mechanism — continuous confidence scoring, four-type epistemic classification, visual weight as confidence encoding, and minimum-spanning-path scaffold — is not described in retrieved literature. However, PaperTrail is close enough that the novelty claim requires precise differentiation, not dismissal of prior art. Retrieval depth was insufficient to rule out argumentation mining tool implementations.\n\n---\n\n### CONCEPT 2: AUDIENCE-IN-THE-LOOP SIMULATION\n**Claim:** A system in which the agent's audience model is made explicit, contestable, and structurally prior to form selection — with a simulation trace as a first-class artifact component — is not standard practice.\n\n**WHAT THE EVIDENCE SHOWS**\n\nAll three queries for Concept 2 returned null on the target framing. The retrieved material (multi-agent simulation methodology, LLM for CSS, adaptive learning systems) does not address audience simulation as a generative input to communication form selection.\n\nThe closest retrieved paper is:\n\n**"The Reader is the Metric: How Textual Features and Reader Profiles Explain Conflicting Evaluations of AI Creative Writing"** (arXiv, Query 4 — retrieved under reader response prediction): This paper shows that reader profiles explain divergent evaluations of AI text. It demonstrates that reader response varies systematically with reader characteristics, which is the empirical grounding for why audience simulation might be useful. It does not describe a system that uses audience simulation as a generative input.\n\n**Mental World Modeling** (arXiv, Query 4) introduces a framework tracking hidden mental states (beliefs, intentions) in world models for embodied agents. This is cognitively adjacent — it formalises what an agent must track about other agents — but it is in the context of planning and action, not communication artifact generation.\n\nNo retrieved paper describes an LLM-based system that: (a) generates a predicted audience response to a draft artifact, (b) uses that prediction to select communication form, and (c) exposes the prediction trace as a revisable artifact component.\n\n**From my own recollection (not a retrieval finding):** There is a body of work in NLP on *persona-conditioned generation* (e.g., systems that generate text adapted to a reader profile). There is also work on *audience-aware summarisation*. Neither, to my knowledge, makes the audience model explicit as a prior, inspectable, revisable step that precedes form selection — they use it as a conditioning signal, not as an intermediate artefact. I am not confident this distinction holds universally; I am flagging it as a gap in my retrieval rather than a confident novelty claim.\n\n**On confabulation risk (addressed in Section 3c below).**\n\n**VERDICT:** ⚠️ **NO PRIOR ART FOUND IN THIS RETRIEVAL** (not the same as nothing existing)\n\nThe retrieval completely missed this domain. The query vocabulary should be redirected toward NLP/HCI literature on persona-conditioned generation, audience-aware writing assistance, and persuasion generation systems. Verdict is not confident and should not be cited as such.\n\n---\n\n### CONCEPT 3: DIMENSIONALITY-FIRST RENDERING\n**Claim:** A system that derives spatial representation from formal dimensionality analysis using composable spatial primitives — rather than chart-type selection — is not described in the constraint set or failure data.\n\n**WHAT THE EVIDENCE SHOWS**\n\nThe two queries targeting Concept 3's core claim (information dimensionality analysis / intrinsic data structure representation) returned zero relevant results. The one partially useful retrieval is:\n\n**"Navigation and Coordination Primitives for Multidimensional Visual Browsers"** (OpenAlex, Query 9):\n> "extensions to the Tioga flight-simulator browsing protocol… allow users to navigate a multidimensional data space using sophisticated zooming capabilities… coordination of multiple browsers."\n\nThis 1993-era paper describes composable navigation primitives for multidimensional data browsing — it is the closest retrieved hit to the primitive-composition idea. It addresses browser-level spatial navigation primitives, not the *derivation* of primitive selection from information structure analysis. It does not describe an automated system that characterises information dimensionality and selects primitives accordingly.\n\n**VisComposer** (OpenAlex, Query 9): A programmable composition environment for information visualization, allowing visual assembly from components. This is human-designed composition, not agent-derived composition from dimensionality analysis.\n\n**From my own recollection (not a retrieval finding):** This is where I must be explicit. The primitive vocabulary proposed in Concept 3 (position, scale, proximity, containment, sequence, path) maps closely onto what Bertin (1967) called *visual variables* (position, size, shape, colour, texture, orientation) and what subsequent visualization grammar literature formalised. Specifically:\n\n- **Bertin's *Sémiologie Graphique*** (1967): Establishes a vocabulary of retinal variables and their perceptual properties. The concept's primitives are a spatial subset of this vocabulary.\n- **Wilkinson's *Grammar of Graphics*** (1999/2005): Formalises visualization as a compositional grammar with geometric primitives, aesthetic mappings, and coordinate systems. The concept's primitive-composition idea is structurally related to Grammar of Graphics' aesthetic-mapping layer.\n- **Munzner's *Visualization Analysis and Design*** (2014): The "what-why-how" framework explicitly ties task decomposition to idiom selection. Her nested model moves from data type characterisation through task abstraction to visual encoding selection — this is structurally analogous to the concept's dimensionality analysis → primitive selection pipeline.\n\nI cannot verify this from the retrieval. These are recollections. The retrieval did not surface these texts because they are book-length works not well-indexed in arXiv/Semantic Scholar at these query terms.\n\n**VERDICT:** ⚠️ **PARTIALLY NOVEL (confidence: low — retrieval insufficient)**\n\n*See Section 3a for the specific probe on this question.* The primitive vocabulary appears to be a spatial subset of established visualization grammar vocabulary. The specific novelty claim — if any — must rest on the *automated derivation mechanism* (agent selects primitives from dimensionality analysis, rather than human designer selecting from grammar), not on the primitives themselves. This distinction is real but requires the concept to be reframed accordingly. As currently described, the concept is ambiguous about whether it is claiming the primitives are new or the selection mechanism is new. Only the selection mechanism could be novel.\n\n---\n\n### CONCEPT 4: DECISION-SURFACE RENDERING\n**Claim:** A rendering architecture in which spatial geometry is derived from formal decomposition of the decision the audience must make — including criteria axes, option positioning, and recommendation robustness under criteria perturbation — is not described in existing approaches.\n\n**WHAT THE EVIDENCE SHOWS**\n\nThe MCDM (Multi-Criteria Decision Making) literature was retrieved extensively and is directly relevant:\n\n**"Robust Evaluation Model Analysis of Decision Support System in Warehouse Selection Based on Multi-Criteria Decision Making and Sensitivity Analysis"** (Semantic Scholar, Query 11): Applies AHP for criteria weighting and TOPSIS for option ranking, with sensitivity analysis. This is a decision support system that positions options relative to weighted criteria — it is the functional analog of a decision surface, implemented as a traditional MCDM tool.\n\n**"Spatial Multi-Criteria Decision-Making Approach in Evaluating Walkability Indices"** (Semantic Scholar, Query 11): Spatial MCDM with geographic positioning of options in criterion space.\n\n**"Macro-Regional Spatial Decision Support for Geo-Distributed Data Center Siting in Europe: Regional Screening and Robustness Under Weight Uncertainty"** (Semantic Scholar, Query 11): Explicitly includes robustness analysis under weight uncertainty — checking whether rankings remain stable when decision priorities change. This is structurally identical to the decision surface's "criteria slider" stress-test mechanism.\n\n**"Multi-Criteria Decision Making for Sustainability and Value Assessment in Early PSS Design"** (OpenAlex, Query 11): Options positioned relative to sustainability and value criteria in a decision space.\n\nThese papers collectively demonstrate that: option positioning in criteria space, sensitivity analysis under criteria reweighting, and robustness assessment of recommendations are established operations in the MCDM literature.\n\n**WHAT THIS MIGHT IMPLY**\n\nThe MCDM literature covers the *computational* operations that the decision surface proposes. What the retrieved papers do not describe is: (a) a browser-rendered interactive spatial artifact where options are positioned in real time, (b) criteria sliders that reposition options dynamically as the reader interacts, (c) the form being generated by an agent from a natural-language communicative goal rather than configured by an analyst. The distinction is between MCDM as an analytical back-end versus MCDM as a communication-first interactive artifact whose geometry is agent-derived.\n\n**From my own recollection (not a retrieval finding):** Commercial decision intelligence tools (e.g., Gartner Magic Quadrant, which positions vendors in a two-criteria space; various vendor evaluation platforms) provide human-authored versions of option-in-criteria-space layouts. Interactive MCDM tools in the academic literature (e.g., Pareto-front visualization tools) allow criteria reweighting. I am not aware of an agent that generates this form from a decomposed communicative goal rather than an analyst configuring it, but I cannot verify this from retrieval.\n\n**VERDICT:** ⚠️ **PARTIALLY NOVEL**\n\nThe MCDM literature covers criteria-weighted option positioning and robustness analysis extensively. The specific novelty — if any — is the agent-generated, browser-rendered, communicatively-framed interactive artifact, not the decision-space geometry itself. The concept's novelty claim as written ("deriving spatial layout from decision geometry is not standard practice") is not supported by the retrieved evidence; what is non-standard is the communicative framing and automated derivation, not the spatial geometry.\n\n---\n\n### CONCEPT 5: RELATIONAL REGISTER ADAPTATION\n**Claim:** A system that makes relational parameters (authority direction, expertise direction, trust baseline, action threshold) explicit as formal generative inputs to rhetorical structure — distinct from content and audience role — is not standard practice.\n\n**WHAT THE EVIDENCE SHOWS**\n\n**"Computational Register Analysis and Synthesis"** (arXiv, Queries 13 and 15) is directly and substantially relevant:\n> "The study of register in computational language research has historically been divided into register analysis, seeking to determine the registerial character of a text or corpus, and register synthesis, seeking to generate a text in a desired register… Register synthesis has tended to use more theoretically articulated notions of register and genre."\n\nThis paper surveys the field of computational register synthesis — generating text in a target register. It explicitly covers the generation direction of what Concept 5 proposes. The paper notes that register synthesis work uses more theoretically articulated notions of register, which is precisely the formalisation Concept 5 claims as its contribution.\n\n**"Communication network dynamics in a large organizational hierarchy"** (arXiv, Query 15): Analyses how formal hierarchy shapes communication patterns. Empirical, not generative — it describes the phenomenon Concept 5 proposes to exploit, not a system that does so.\n\n**"Registers of Communication"** (OpenAlex, Query 14):\n> "communicative activities are organized into models of conduct that differentiate specific social practices from each other… registers of communication through which persons organize their participation in varied social practices"\n\nThis is a sociolinguistics volume that provides the theoretical grounding for the register concept Concept 5 proposes to formalise. It does not describe a computational system.\n\n**"Computer systems and the design of organizational interaction"** (OpenAlex, Query 15):\n> "a theory of language as social action… practical implications… for the design of systems for organizational communication"\n\nSpeech-act theory applied to organizational systems design — prior work on the theoretical foundation that Concept 5 draws on.\n\n**WHAT THIS MIGHT IMPLY**\n\nThe computational register synthesis literature (as surveyed by the arXiv paper) is the clearest overlap. If register synthesis work generates text in a target register — calibrated to authority, expertise, and social position — then Concept 5's core mechanism is operating in a documented field. The specific additional claim — that relational parameters are decomposed into a four-axis formal map (authority direction, expertise direction, trust baseline, action threshold) *prior* to and *structurally determining* the artifact's rhetorical structure, and that this map is human-correctable through natural language — may be a specific contribution within that field, not outside it.\n\n**From my own recollection (not a retrieval finding):** Style transfer in NLP produces text adapted to register but typically treats register as a target label, not as a multi-dimensional input map. Politeness theory in dialogue systems (Brown and Levinson's face-threatening acts framework) formalises social relationship parameters in ways adjacent to Concept 5's four-axis model. I do not recall a system that exposes a relational map as an explicit, correctable intermediate artifact rather than a conditioning signal.\n\n**VERDICT:** ⚠️ **PARTIALLY NOVEL**\n\nComputational register synthesis is a documented field. The concept's contribution — if any — is the decomposition of register into a formal four-axis relational map that is (a) explicit to the human, (b) correctable through natural language, and (c) structurally prior to rhetorical organisation rather than used as a post-hoc label. The concept should be positioned *within* the register synthesis literature, not as an alternative to it, and the novelty claim should be specific about which of these three features are genuinely absent from existing register synthesis approaches.\n\n---\n\n### CONCEPT 6: DIEGETIC DATA EMBEDDING\n**Claim:** An agent-operated system that constructs a world model for an arbitrary business domain and generates the data-property mapping without a human designer specifying the world model is not described in existing work.\n\n**WHAT THE EVIDENCE SHOWS**\n\nAll four queries for Concept 6 returned zero relevant results on the target framing. The Byzantine-resilient distributed optimization papers that saturated the results were retrieval noise (the word "encoding" in a different context). The closest relevant retrieved papers were:\n\n**"Digital Twin: Values, Challenges and Enablers From a Modeling Perspective"** (OpenAlex, Query 18): Digital twins are virtual representations of physical assets with data encoded in the model's properties — this is structurally analogous to diegetic data embedding in physical/industrial domains. However, a digital twin is constructed by engineers with domain expertise, not generated by an agent from a natural-language description.\n\n**"Exploring the Design Space of BioFabric Visualization for Multivariate Network Analysis"** (Semantic Scholar, Query 17): Encodes multivariate attributes in the visual properties of network entities — node and edge properties carry data dimensions. This is the closest retrieved structural analog to diegetic embedding, in the context of biological network visualization.\n\nNo retrieved paper describes an agent that: (a) constructs a world model for an arbitrary business domain, (b) maps data dimensions to visual properties of domain entities, and (c) renders a business communication artifact without human designer specification of the world model.\n\n**From my own recollection (not a retrieval finding):** The diegetic display concept is documented in game studies literature — Jesse Schell's *The Art of Game Design* and the game UI literature distinguish diegetic interfaces (interfaces that exist within the game world) from non-diegetic interfaces (HUDs external to the game world). The New York Times visual journalism unit has published pieces where icon or entity properties encode quantitative data — these are human-designed, not agent-generated. Isotype (Neurath, 1930s) used pictographic entities with scaled properties to encode quantities — again, human-designed. The specific novelty Naledi identifies — the *generative mechanism* operating on arbitrary domains without human world-model design — is what distinguishes this from all those precedents, and this specific mechanism is absent from retrieved literature. I cannot verify whether it has been done in HCI or business intelligence tool literature through this retrieval.\n\n**VERDICT:** ⚠️ **NO PRIOR ART FOUND IN THIS RETRIEVAL** (not the same as nothing existing)\n\nThe retrieval completely failed to reach the relevant domain (game UI literature, data journalism, business intelligence visualization tools, HCI venues like CHI/UIST). The diegetic *principle* has precedents (see my recollection above); the *agent-generative mechanism* for arbitrary domains appears to have no retrieved prior art. Verdict cannot be treated as confident; queries must be redirected substantially.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 3: THREE SPECIFIC PROBES\n\n---\n\n### 3a. CONCEPT 3'S PRIMITIVE VOCABULARY AGAINST THE VISUALIZATION GRAMMAR LITERATURE\n\n**Ground rule note:** I cannot retrieve Bertin, Wilkinson, or Munzner through this system. What follows is my own recollection, explicitly labelled as such. This probe cannot be satisfied at verification depth from the retrieved evidence alone.\n\n**My recollection (not a retrieval finding):**\n\n**Bertin's *Sémiologie Graphique* (1967)** established what he called *visual variables* or *retinal variables*: position (on x and y axes), size, value (lightness), texture, colour (hue), orientation, and shape. Of these, position and size are the most perceptually salient. Bertin argued that which variable to use depends on the nature of the data dimension — selective, associative, ordered, or quantitative.\n\n**Wilkinson's *Grammar of Graphics* (1999/2005)** formalised visualization as a compositional system with layers: data, transformations, geometric objects (point, line, area), aesthetic mappings (position, colour, size, shape, line type), scales, coordinates, and facets. The key contribution is treating visualization as composition of these layers from data specifications — not chart-type selection.\n\n**Munzner's *Visualization Analysis and Design* (2014)** proposes a nested model: domain characterisation → abstract task → visual encoding → algorithm. Her idiom selection (visual encoding choice) is explicitly tied to data types (categorical, ordinal, quantitative) and task types (search, query, browse). This is a systematic derivation of visual form from data and task structure.\n\n**Assessment of Concept 3 against this recollected tradition:**\n\nConcept 3's primitive vocabulary — position, scale, proximity, containment, sequence, path — is a *spatial subset* of the existing visualization grammar vocabulary. Position and scale directly correspond to Bertin's position and size. Proximity and containment are implicit in Bertin's position variable and made explicit in spatial metaphor literature. Sequence and path correspond to Bertin's ordering dimension and line geometrics in Wilkinson.\n\n**WHAT THIS MIGHT IMPLY**\n\nThe critical question is whether Concept 3's *selection mechanism* is differentiated from the existing tradition. I assess this as follows:\n\n- Bertin derives variable selection from data type (selective/ordered/quantitative). Concept 3 derives primitive selection from dimensionality analysis (number of independent variables, their nature, their relationship structure). These are structurally similar operations at different levels of abstraction.\n- Munzner's nested model is the closest structural analog: data characterisation → visual encoding selection. The specific move in Concept 3 — agent performs dimensionality analysis, agent selects minimal primitive set — is Munzner's idiom selection step automated and made explicit.\n- Wilkinson's Grammar of Graphics provides the technical machinery for what Concept 3 calls "primitive composition" — it is a compositional grammar for exactly this purpose.\n\n**Conclusion on probe 3a:** Concept 3's primitive vocabulary is not novel with respect to the visualization grammar tradition. The selection mechanism is structurally analogous to Munzner's nested model and Bertin's retinal variable selection logic. What Concept 3 adds — if anything — is: (a) automation of this selection by an agent rather than a human analyst, and (b) explicit framing as a dimensionality analysis step preceding form selection. This is a real engineering contribution but should not be claimed as a conceptual novelty over the visualization grammar literature. The concept needs to reframe its novelty claim explicitly in terms of automating and making explicit a selection process the visualization literature has theorised but not automated at the agent level.\n\n**Confidence in this assessment: moderate.** I am working from recollection of book-length works I cannot retrieve here. Ingrid should verify this independently.\n\n---\n\n### 3b. DO CONCEPTS 1 AND 4 COLLAPSE INTO THE SAME PRIOR ART?\n\n**WHAT THE EVIDENCE SHOWS**\n\nThe retrieved evidence places Concepts 1 and 4 in contact with *different* bodies of prior art:\n\n- Concept 1 is adjacent to **PaperTrail** (claim-evidence decomposition), **argumentation mining** (claim classification), and **explainability literature** (epistemic transparency of AI outputs).\n- Concept 4 is adjacent to **MCDM literature** (multi-criteria option ranking, sensitivity analysis, spatial decision support systems).\n\nThese are distinct prior art bodies. The question is whether the *structural* operations are the same.\n\n**Structural comparison:**\n\n| Dimension | Concept 1 (Commitment Audit) | Concept 4 (Decision Surface) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Primary input | Agent's epistemic self-model | Decision geometry (options, criteria) |\n| Visual geometry | Dependency graph, confidence-weighted nodes | Spatial field, criteria axes, option positions |\n| Interactive operation | Expand/collapse claim branches | Drag criteria sliders, reweight options |\n| Stress-test mechanism | "Show only assertions" filter | Criteria perturbation → recommendation shift |\n| Reader goal | Interrogate argument quality | Evaluate recommendation robustness |\n| Prior art overlap | Argumentation mining, PaperTrail | MCDM tools, spatial decision support |\n\n**WHAT THIS MIGHT IMPLY**\n\nThe concepts do not collapse into the same prior art. They share a *family resemblance* — both make the reasoning structure navigable, both expose internal model state to the reader, both allow targeted interrogation. But their generative inputs differ (epistemic audit vs. decision decomposition), their visual geometries differ (argument graph vs. option-in-criteria-space), and their reader interactions differ (argument interrogation vs. criteria stress-testing).\n\nThe collapse concern is worth naming in a different direction: both concepts, if implemented naively using existing tools, would resemble **argument mapping software** (Concept 1) and **MCDM dashboards** (Concept 4). The shared risk is that without the agent-generative mechanism as the distinguishing feature, both reduce to familiar tool categories. The novelty in both cases rests entirely on the agent producing the structure from its own self-model (Concept 1) or from a natural-language goal decomposition (Concept 4) — not on the visual form itself.\n\n**Conclusion on probe 3b:** They do not collapse into the *same* prior art, but they share a common vulnerability: their novelty claims rest on the agent-generative mechanism, not on the visual form. If the agent-generative step is underspecified or fails to produce genuinely different structures from existing tools, both concepts reduce to their respective prior art bodies. This is a shared architectural risk, not a shared prior art identity.\n\n---\n\n### 3c. CONCEPT 2'S AUDIENCE SIMULATION: PRIOR ART AND CONFABULATION RISK\n\n**WHAT THE EVIDENCE SHOWS**\n\nThe retrieval returned null on the target framing for audience simulation as generative input. The most relevant tangential findings:\n\n**"The Reader is the Metric"** (arXiv, Query 4): Demonstrates empirically that reader profiles explain divergent AI writing evaluations. This is the empirical grounding for why audience characteristics matter — it does not describe a simulation mechanism.\n\n**Mental World Modeling** (arXiv): Formalises agent tracking of other agents' belief states — structural analog in robotics/planning, not communication design.\n\n**"Can Large Language Models Transform Computational Social Science?"** (OpenAlex, Query 4): Covers LLMs as simulated participants in social science research, including using LLMs as proxies for human respondents. This is directly relevant to the confabulation question.\n\n**From my own recollection (not a retrieval finding):**\n\n**On prior art for LLM audience simulation:**\n\nThere is a documented practice of using LLMs as simulated user populations for survey research and evaluation (sometimes called "silicon sampling"). Argyle et al. (2023, *Political Communication*) showed LLMs can approximate human survey responses under demographic conditioning. This work is relevant because it shows LLMs can simulate audience responses — which is both the mechanism Concept 2 relies on and the source of its confabulation risk.\n\nWork on **persona-conditioned text generation** exists in NLP (e.g., systems that generate responses conditioned on reader personas). Work on **audience-aware summarisation** exists. Neither, to my knowledge, makes the audience model explicit as a revisable intermediate artifact that is structurally prior to form selection.\n\n**On confabulation risk in audience simulation:**\n\nThis is the most important methodological question for Concept 2, and the retrieved evidence partially addresses it:\n\nThe **"Can LLMs Transform CSS?"** paper (OpenAlex) discusses LLM zero-shot performance on social phenomena classification. It does not directly address confabulation in audience simulation, but it establishes that LLM performance on social characterisation is task-dependent and not uniformly reliable.\n\n**The core confabulation risk in Concept 2:** An LLM simulating audience response is generating a plausible description of how an audience member would respond, conditioned on its training distribution. This is precisely the mechanism that produces confabulation in other LLM applications — the model generates a fluent, plausible output that may not correspond to how the actual audience would respond. The simulation trace is itself an LLM output subject to the same hallucination dynamics as any other LLM output. The concept's falsifiability criterion (50% agreement between simulated and actual audience response) implicitly acknowledges this risk — but the concept does not describe a mechanism for detecting when the simulation is confabulating.\n\n**WHAT THIS MIGHT IMPLY**\n\nThe concept's control mechanism (human corrects the audience model) only works if the human can identify when the simulation is wrong. For audiences the human knows well, this is feasible. For audiences the human does not know well (which is often the motivation for using a simulation), the human cannot correct a wrong simulation because they do not know it is wrong. This is a structural confabulation trap: the simulation is most useful when the human lacks ground truth, and the human can only correct it when they have ground truth.\n\nThis does not invalidate the concept, but it means the simulation trace must be treated as a hypothesis with explicit uncertainty markers, not as a prediction. The concept's falsifiability experiment (50% agreement threshold) is the right test but it would need to be run on cases where the human *does not* know the actual audience response, not cases where they can easily verify it.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 4: CLOSING ASSESSMENT\n\n### Strongest novelty position on this evidence\n\n**Concept 6 (Diegetic Data Embedding)** has the strongest novelty position, but for a reason that should prompt caution rather than confidence: *the retrieval completely failed to reach the relevant literature*. The diegetic principle has clear precedents (game UI, data journalism, Isotype) that my recollection identifies but the retrieval did not surface. The specific agent-generative mechanism — constructing world models for arbitrary business domains without human designer specification — has no retrieved prior art, and I have no strong recollection of prior work on this specific mechanism. This is the concept where the novelty claim might be most real, and also the one where the retrieval evidence is thinnest. Sophie and Ingrid should treat this as "retrieval-insufficient to rule out prior art" rather than "confirmed novel."\n\n**Concept 1 (Commitment Audit)** has the strongest evidence-supported novelty position, because it has identifiable prior art (PaperTrail) that is close enough to require differentiation but distant enough that the differentiation is meaningful and retrievable. The concept can be positioned with precision relative to PaperTrail's claim-evidence binary mapping, which makes its specific additions (continuous confidence, four-type classification, minimum-spanning-path scaffold) articulable.\n\n### Weakest novelty position on this evidence\n\n**Concept 3 (Dimensionality-First Rendering)** has the weakest novelty position. The primitive vocabulary (position, scale, proximity, containment, sequence, path) is a spatial subset of Bertin's retinal variables and Wilkinson's aesthetic-mapping layer. The selection mechanism is structurally analogous to Munzner's nested model. The concept's novelty claim as written conflates the primitives (not novel) with the automated selection mechanism (potentially novel). Until the concept explicitly reframes around the automation claim and differentiates from the visualization grammar tradition, it risks claiming novelty that the InfoVis literature disposed of decades ago. This assessment rests partly on recollection; Ingrid should verify independently.\n\n**Concept 5 (Relational Register Adaptation)** is the second-weakest. Computational register synthesis is a documented field, and the retrieved paper explicitly covers generation toward a target register. The concept's specific contributions (four-axis formal map, human-correctable intermediate artifact) may be real additions within that field, but the concept's novelty framing treats register synthesis as if it does not exist.\n\n### What to search next to raise confidence\n\n**For Concept 6:** Redirect queries to CHI/UIST proceedings for "diegetic interface," "data physicalisation," "situated visualization," and "ambient display." Search data journalism literature for "icon encoding quantitative properties." Search game studies literature for "diegetic HUD" and "data-driven environment design."\n\n**For Concept 3:** Direct search for Bertin 1967, Wilkinson Grammar of Graphics, Munzner 2014, and Mackinlay's APT system (1986, which automated chart selection from data type). APT is the most important precursor — if retrieved, it would likely change the verdict substantially. Query: "automated presentation tool chart selection Mackinlay" and "Vega-Lite declarative visualization grammar."\n\n**For Concept 2:** Query NLP literature for "persona-conditioned generation," "audience-aware summarization," "silicon sampling survey," "LLM simulated respondent," and "persuasion generation audience model." The Argyle et al. political communication work on LLM survey proxies is the most important recollected prior art to verify.\n\n**For Concept 5:** Query specifically for "register synthesis formalization," "computational stylistics authority adaptation," "politeness-aware text generation Brown Levinson," and "social role conditioned NLG."\n\n**For Concept 1:** Query argumentation mining literature specifically for "claim classification confidence scoring visual," and search for tool implementations of Toulmin schema with confidence weighting. Query "minimum spanning argument" as a concept in rhetoric/argumentation.\n\n**For Concept 4:** The MCDM retrieval was adequate for the domain. The remaining question is whether agent-generated MCDM visualizations exist in business intelligence tool literature. Query: "automated decision dashboard generation natural language goal," "LLM decision support visualization."\n\n---\n\n*End of Verification Brief — Sprint 10 Pass 3*\n*Kenji Ochiai | Altair8 Research Team*\n*All recollections clearly separated from retrieval findings throughout. Retrieval inadequacy noted explicitly for Concepts 2, 3, 4, 6. Verdicts marked ⚠️ should be treated as provisional.*	2026-08-20 07:49:46.986679	2026-08-20 07:55:38.235544	prior_art_verification	{"memory_key": "kenji/sprint10_concept_verification", "queries_run": 18, "results_retrieved": 373}
84	kenji	team_leader	Sprint 10 Pass 3 round 2: redirected prior-art retrieval	Requery after round 1 was judged inadequate for 4 of 6 concepts.	completed	# LITERATURE VERIFICATION BRIEF — SPRINT 10 PASS 3, ROUND 2 REVISION\n## Six Concepts: Revised Novelty Verdicts After Round 2 Evidence\n\n**Prepared by:** Kenji Ochiai, Researcher, Altair8\n**Stage:** Verification (Pass 3, Round 2 — incorporating live retrieval results delivered by Sophie)\n**Date:** Sprint 10\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 1: ROUND 2 RETRIEVAL LOG\n\n**Mandatory process disclosure per Sprint Review #1 requirement (2026-07-27)**\n\n| Query | Sources | Results Returned | Retained as Relevant | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Mackinlay APT automating design graphical presentations data 1986 | arXiv, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex | 29 | 4 substantive | Mackinlay APT (Semantic Scholar + OpenAlex — confirmed hit); VizML; DataShot; "Applying a theory of graphical presentation" (Mackinlay follow-on) |\n| PaperTrail claim evidence provenance LLM epistemic status argument confidence weighted visualization | arXiv, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex | 10 | 2 | PaperTrail (confirmed); Epistemic Blinding (adjacent) |\n| Persona conditioned text generation audience model explicit intermediate artifact | arXiv, OpenAlex | 20 | 3 | PosterMate (OpenAlex — directly relevant); Proxona (OpenAlex — directly relevant); persona-steered generation (arXiv — relevant) |\n| Audience aware summarization reader profile adaptive content selection NLP | arXiv, OpenAlex | 21 | 2 | Reader-Aware MDS (two papers — relevant); "The Reader is the Metric" (already held) |\n| Persuasion generation system target audience belief modeling communicative goal | arXiv, OpenAlex | 20 | 2 | "Strategic Argumentation Dialogues for Persuasion" (arXiv — relevant); "Argument Strength is in the Eye of the Beholder" (arXiv — relevant) |\n| User model NLG content planning audience prior inspectable | arXiv, OpenAlex | 20 | 2 | "Towards Pragmatic Production Strategies for NLG" (arXiv — relevant); NLG survey (relevant background) |\n| Mackinlay APT expressiveness effectiveness graphical presentation algebra 1986 | arXiv, OpenAlex | 20 | 3 | Mackinlay APT (OpenAlex — confirmed again); "How Data Analysts Use a Visualization Grammar" (OpenAlex); "Data visualization from a category theory perspective" (OpenAlex) |\n| Wilkinson grammar of graphics aesthetic mapping geometric primitives data type encoding | arXiv, OpenAlex | 14 | 2 | "How Data Analysts Use a Visualization Grammar in Practice" (OpenAlex); "Data visualization from a category theory perspective" (OpenAlex) |\n| Automated visualization recommendation data type visual encoding selection system | arXiv, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex | 30 | 5 | VizML (OpenAlex); "A Review and Collation of Graphical Perception Knowledge for Visualization Recommendation" (Semantic Scholar); GEViTRec; "Reframing Pattern"; "Beyond Heuristics" |\n| Munzner nested model idiom selection data abstraction task abstraction encoding | arXiv, OpenAlex | 13 | 1 | "A Multi-Level Task Framework for Event Sequence Analysis" (adjacent); nothing reached the nested model directly |\n| Visualization grammar compositional spatial primitives dimensionality data variables automated derivation | arXiv, OpenAlex | 22 | 2 | "Reframing Pattern" (arXiv); "Encodable: Configurable Grammar for Visualization Components" (arXiv) |\n| Interactive multi-criteria decision visualization criteria weighting option positioning browser real-time | arXiv, OpenAlex | 20 | 2 | "Generalised framework for multi-criteria method selection" (arXiv); "Web-enabled spatial decision analysis using OWA" (OpenAlex) |\n| Pareto front visualization multicriteria sensitivity analysis interactive reweighting artifacts | arXiv | 10 | 2 | "NL2INTERFACE: Interactive Visualization Interface Generation from Natural Language Queries" (arXiv — significant); "Interactive Visual Analysis of Spatial Sensitivities" (arXiv) |\n| Agent generated decision support spatial layout communicative framing natural language | arXiv, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex | 21 | 2 | "Semantic knowledge in generation of 3D layouts for decision-making" (OpenAlex — directly relevant); "Towards Pragmatic Production Strategies for NLG" (arXiv) |\n| Style transfer register sociolinguistic variables formalized axis NLP generation | arXiv, OpenAlex | 20 | 0 direct | Complete miss — "AXIS" (telescope) and image style transfer dominated; no sociolinguistic register formalization |\n| Computational register synthesis authority expertise social relationship generative input rhetorical structure | arXiv, OpenAlex | 20 | 1 | "Computational Register Analysis and Synthesis" (arXiv — confirmed from R1) |\n| Diegetic interface game UI data encoding world model domain entities visual properties | arXiv, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex | 22 | 0 direct | Game narrative papers retrieved but not diegetic display / data encoding; complete miss on target |\n| Isotype pictographic data representation domain entity property quantitative encoding automated | arXiv, OpenAlex | 10 | 0 direct | Complete miss; Byzantine encoding papers dominate |\n| Data driven illustration infographic domain entity visual property mapping agent generated | arXiv, OpenAlex | 20 | 3 | "A survey on automatic infographics and visualization recommendations" (OpenAlex); "InfoAlign" (OpenAlex); "Towards Automated Infographic Design" (arXiv — relevant) |\n| Information visualization metaphor world model entity attribute encoding business domain generative | arXiv, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex | 30 | 1 | "Semantic knowledge in generation of 3D layouts for decision-making" (OpenAlex — same paper as above) |\n| Situated data visualization embedded narrative domain model property mapping automated generation | arXiv, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex | 23 | 2 | "DataParticles" (OpenAlex); "Leveraging Foundation Models for Crafting Narrative Visualization" (OpenAlex) |\n\n**Round 2 totals:** 21 queries, ~459 results returned, ~41 retained as directly or partially relevant (noise rate approximately 91%)\n\n**Combined rounds 1 + 2 totals:** 39 queries, ~835 results returned, ~54 retained as relevant (combined noise rate approximately 93.5%)\n\n---\n\n### Is combined retrieval now proportionate to the verdicts being given?\n\n**Direct answer, concept by concept:**\n\n- **Concept 1 (Commitment Audit):** Yes, proportionate. PaperTrail confirmed twice, Mackinlay lineage now visible. The distinction being claimed is specific enough that the verdict can be stated with reasonable confidence.\n- **Concept 2 (Audience-in-the-Loop Simulation):** Now better but still not fully proportionate. PosterMate and Proxona are significant new hits. The HCI literature on LLM-powered audience simulation is now partially represented. However, the NLG tradition (content planning with audience models, 1990s–2000s) is present only in survey form; the specific works by Paris, Moore, or Reiter/Dale on user-model-driven generation were not retrieved as primary sources. Verdict is more grounded than Round 1 but still carries residual uncertainty on the NLG planning literature.\n- **Concept 3 (Dimensionality-First Rendering):** No, retrieval is still not fully proportionate. APT was confirmed as a live retrieved paper, which is the most important development. But Bertin and Munzner's nested model were not reached as primary documents — only as recollections. The verdict on the selection-mechanism claim therefore rests partly on recollection. I will flag this clearly.\n- **Concept 4 (Decision-Surface Rendering):** Mostly proportionate. NL2INTERFACE and "Semantic knowledge in generation of 3D layouts" are significant new additions. The combined retrieval now covers the computational operations (MCDM), the interactive visualization side (NL2INTERFACE, web-based OWA), and agent-generated spatial layouts. This is enough to render a verdict with meaningful confidence.\n- **Concept 5 (Relational Register Adaptation):** Partially proportionate. The register synthesis paper is confirmed. The style-transfer query completely failed (telescope instrument dominated). The sociolinguistic formalization literature (politeness theory, Brown and Levinson) was not retrieved as primary sources. The verdict is better grounded than Round 1 on the register synthesis side, but the style-transfer and politeness theory overlap remains unverified by retrieval.\n- **Concept 6 (Diegetic Data Embedding):** No, retrieval is still not proportionate. The diegetic display / game UI literature was not reached. Infographic automation was partially reached. The verdict remains "no prior art found in this retrieval" which is not the same as "novel" — and I will continue to state this explicitly.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 2: REVISED VERDICTS PER CONCEPT\n\n---\n\n### CONCEPT 1: THE COMMITMENT AUDIT\n**Round 2 verdict status: STRENGTHENED**\n\n**WHAT THE EVIDENCE SHOWS (Round 2 additions)**\n\nThe Mackinlay APT paper (1986), retrieved via Semantic Scholar and OpenAlex in Round 2 under the first query, is confirmed as a live retrieved source. The OpenAlex entry describes it as:\n\n> "The goal of the research described in this paper is to develop an application-independent presentation tool that automatically designs effective graphical presentations (such as bar charts, scatter plots, and connected graphs) of relational information. Two problems are raised by this goal: The codification of graphic design criteria in a form that can be used by the presentation tool, and the generation of a wide variety of designs so that the presentation tool can accommodate a wide variety of information."\n\nThis is relevant to Concept 1 as context — APT is the foundational precedent for automatically generating visual presentations from formal criteria. It does not address epistemic status or confidence weighting; it addresses expressiveness and effectiveness of encoding relational data. Its presence confirms that the tradition of automated graphic design has a 40-year literature, but it does not specifically address self-auditing epistemic state as a generative input.\n\n**Epistemic Blinding** (arXiv, Round 2, Query 2) is a new and meaningful retrieval:\n\n> "Epistemic blinding is a simple inference-time protocol that replaces entity identifiers with anonymous codes in order to audit how much of an LLM output derives from memorized priors versus data on the page."\n\nThis paper addresses LLM epistemic audit at inference time — it is the closest retrieved work to the Commitment Audit's auditing mechanism. However, epistemic blinding is an *analytical* protocol (revealing the blend of memorized versus data-derived inference), not a *display* architecture. It does not produce a visual artifact; it produces a de-identified second output for comparison. The Commitment Audit's move — taking that epistemic audit and converting it into a confidence-weighted visual structure — is not described in this paper.\n\nPaperTrail remains the closest prior art and was re-confirmed in Round 2.\n\n**WHAT THIS MIGHT IMPLY**\n\nRound 2 strengthens the Round 1 verdict by: (a) confirming PaperTrail as real prior art; (b) adding Epistemic Blinding as a new adjacent piece showing that LLM epistemic auditing is an active research area; (c) confirming that the APT lineage (automated graphic design from formal criteria) does not address epistemic status as a generative dimension.\n\nThe specific combination in the Commitment Audit — agent audits its own epistemic state into four categories, scores confidence continuously, uses that score as a visual weight, and outputs a minimum-spanning-argument scaffold — is not described in any retrieved paper.\n\n**REVISED VERDICT: ⚠️ PARTIALLY NOVEL — STRENGTHENED**\n\nRound 2 strengthens confidence in this verdict. PaperTrail is real prior art covering claim-evidence decomposition and provenance mapping. Epistemic Blinding covers LLM epistemic auditing. Neither covers the visual-weight-from-confidence mechanism or the minimum-spanning-argument scaffold. The novelty claim holds on those specific dimensions, and the prior art is now better characterised. The concept should be presented with explicit acknowledgement of PaperTrail and Epistemic Blinding as the nearest precedents.\n\n---\n\n### CONCEPT 2: AUDIENCE-IN-THE-LOOP SIMULATION\n**Round 2 verdict status: CHANGED — verdict narrowed, prior art now identified**\n\n**WHAT THE EVIDENCE SHOWS (Round 2 additions)**\n\nTwo papers retrieved in Round 2 are directly and substantially relevant. Both were missed in Round 1.\n\n**PosterMate** (OpenAlex, Round 2, Query 3):\n> "PosterMate assists a user in creating a set of persona agents based on the marketing brief, each of which provides a set of design feedback on the (i) text, (ii) image, and (iii) theme for the poster draft… The user can also discuss with the persona agents on a design conflict to reach a conclusion."\n\nPosterMate creates LLM-powered audience personas that provide design feedback on a communication artifact. The personas are derived from a brief and are interactive. This is structurally close to Concept 2: an audience model that is active, inspectable, and used to evaluate/guide artifact design. The key difference is that PosterMate's personas provide *feedback on a human-drafted artifact*, not a *simulation trace that precedes and determines form selection*.\n\n**Proxona** (OpenAlex, Round 2, Query 3):\n> "Proxona, an LLM-powered system that transforms static audience comments into interactive, multi-dimensional personas, allowing creators to engage with them to gain insights, gather simulated feedback, and refine content. Proxona distills audience traits from comments, into dimensions (categories) and values (attributes), then clusters them into interactive personas representing audience segments."\n\nProxona is more directly overlapping. It makes the audience model explicit (multi-dimensional traits), interactive (creators can engage with personas), and uses it to gather simulated feedback on content. This is substantially what Concept 2 proposes, minus the specific claim that the simulation trace is a *structurally prior* first-class artifact component that *determines form selection before drafting begins*.\n\n**"Towards Pragmatic Production Strategies for NLG"** (arXiv, Round 2, Query 7):\n> "This position paper proposes a conceptual framework for the design of NLG systems that follow efficient and effective production strategies in order to achieve complex communicative goals… efficiency is characterised as the parsimonious regulation of production and comprehension costs while effectiveness is measured with respect to task-oriented and contextually grounded communicative goals."\n\nThis paper from the NLG tradition formalizes audience modeling as part of communicative goal-setting in generation systems. It is a theoretical framework, not a system implementation, but it establishes that the NLG field has been thinking about audience modeling as structurally prior to content generation since before the LLM era.\n\n**Reader-Aware Multi-Document Summarization** (arXiv, Round 2, Queries 4–5): Two papers on reader-aware summarization where reader profiles influence summary content selection. This confirms that adaptive content selection conditioned on reader characteristics is an established NLP subfield.\n\n**WHAT THIS MIGHT IMPLY**\n\nRound 1's verdict was "no prior art found." Round 2 changes this. PosterMate and Proxona are directly relevant systems. They demonstrate that:\n- LLM-powered audience persona simulation as a design tool is an active HCI research area\n- Making the audience model explicit, multi-dimensional, and interactive is described in the literature\n\nThe Concept 2 novelty claim as originally stated — that making an audience model explicit and prior to form selection is not standard — is **no longer supportable**. The narrower claim that survives is:\n1. The simulation trace as a *first-class document component* that the reader of the final artifact can inspect (not just a tool used by the author during drafting)\n2. The audience model as *structurally determining form selection* rather than providing feedback on a selected form\n\nThese are genuinely different from PosterMate and Proxona. But they are specific and narrow, and the concept description needs to make them the explicit core claim rather than implying broader novelty.\n\n**REVISED VERDICT: ⚠️ NARROWLY NOVEL — VERDICT CHANGED**\n\nRound 2 changes the Round 1 verdict materially. Prior art now exists for LLM-powered audience simulation as a design tool (PosterMate, Proxona). The concept's novelty claim must be restated to focus specifically on: (a) the simulation trace as a reader-facing artifact component rather than an author-facing tool, and (b) the audience model as structurally prior to form selection rather than posterior to it. Without this restatement, the concept overlaps substantially with existing HCI work.\n\n---\n\n### CONCEPT 3: DIMENSIONALITY-FIRST RENDERING\n**Round 2 verdict status: CHANGED — novelty claim significantly weakened by retrieved evidence**\n\n**WHAT THE EVIDENCE SHOWS (Round 2 additions)**\n\nThis is the most consequential revision from Round 2. See Section 3 (The APT Question) for the detailed treatment. Summary here.\n\n**Mackinlay APT** (OpenAlex, confirmed retrieved): The APT paper is now a confirmed retrieved source, not a recollection. Its abstract states:\n\n> "The approach described in this paper is based on the view that graphical presentations are sentences of graphical languages and that the properties of these languages determine the effectiveness of the resulting presentations."\n\nAPT formalises: (a) a vocabulary of graphical encoding primitives (position, length, angle, slope, area, volume, colour, saturation, density, texture, shape, orientation); (b) expressiveness criteria (does the encoding faithfully represent the data's properties?); (c) effectiveness criteria (which encoding is most perceptually efficient?); (d) an automated system that derives graphic form from data type using these criteria. This is the 1986 implementation of what Concept 3 proposes.\n\n**"Applying a theory of graphical presentation to the graphic design of user interfaces"** (OpenAlex, Round 2, Query 1 — Mackinlay follow-on): Retrieved alongside APT. Extends APT's theory to interface design, demonstrating the compositional application of the same principles.\n\n**VizML** (OpenAlex, confirmed):\n> "We demonstrate a novel machine learning-based approach to visualization recommendation that learns visualization design choices from a large corpus of datasets and associated visualizations. First, we identify five key design choices made by analysts while creating visualizations, such as selecting a visualization type and choosing to encode a column along the X- or Y-axis."\n\nVizML is a machine learning system that automates the selection of visual encoding from data characteristics. This is the ML-era implementation of the same pipeline Concept 3 proposes.\n\n**"How Data Analysts Use a Visualization Grammar in Practice"** (OpenAlex, Round 2, Query 8 and 10):\n> "Visualization grammars, often based on the Grammar of Graphics (GoG), have much potential for augmenting data analysis in a programming environment… we qualitatively analyzed how experienced analysts… wrangled and visualized data using GoG-based ggplot2."\n\nThis confirms that Grammar of Graphics (Wilkinson) implementations are live, in active use, and analysed empirically. The GoG framework is the documented infrastructure for primitive composition from data specification.\n\n**"A Review and Collation of Graphical Perception Knowledge for Visualization Recommendation"** (Semantic Scholar, Round 2, Query 9):\n> "Selecting appropriate visual encodings is critical to designing effective visualization recommendation systems, yet few findings from graphical perception are typically applied within these systems."\n\nThis survey covers the entire field of automated visual encoding selection from data properties — confirming this is an established research area with substantial literature.\n\n**"Encodable: Configurable Grammar for Visualization Components"** (arXiv, Round 2, Query 11):\n> "This work drew inspiration from visualization grammar, decoupled the grammar from its rendering engine and adapted it into a configurable grammar for individual components… Encodable helps component authors define grammar for their components, and parse encoding specifications from [grammar specifications]."\n\nA working implementation of configurable compositional encoding grammars for visualization components. Directly in the space of Concept 3's technical proposal.\n\n**WHAT THIS MIGHT IMPLY**\n\nThe combination of APT (1986), Grammar of Graphics, VizML, and the visualization recommendation literature covers the core of what Concept 3 proposes. The pipeline — characterise data dimensionality and type → select visual encoding primitives → compose presentation — is the foundational design of automated visualization systems since 1986.\n\nThe specific vocabulary in Concept 3 (position, scale, proximity, containment, sequence, path) maps onto APT's primitive vocabulary (position, length, angle, area, colour, texture, orientation, shape) and Grammar of Graphics' aesthetic mappings. The specific primitives are a spatial subset of a vocabulary that has been formalised and used for four decades.\n\nThe automated derivation mechanism is VizML and the entire visualization recommendation literature. The compositional primitive grammar is Grammar of Graphics and Encodable. The spatial primitive subset and the browser rendering context are the only differences I can identify — and neither is a claim made explicitly in the concept description.\n\n**REVISED VERDICT: ⚠️ NOT NOVEL AS CURRENTLY STATED — VERDICT CHANGED**\n\nThis is the sharpest verdict change in Round 2. The core claim — deriving spatial representation from formal dimensionality analysis using composable primitives rather than chart-type selection — describes the foundational architecture of the automated visualization design field from APT (1986) through Grammar of Graphics through VizML. This is now a retrieved finding, not a recollection. The concept does not survive as stated.\n\nIf a novelty claim is to be made, it must be narrowed to something that is *not* covered by this literature. Candidates: (a) the specific six-primitive spatial vocabulary as a communicative (not analytic) encoding set; (b) the context of agent-generated business communication artifacts rather than data exploration tools; (c) the minimum-information encoding principle applied to inter-human communication rather than data analysis. None of these can be assessed with confidence from the current retrieval. See Section 3 for the detailed APT analysis.\n\n---\n\n### CONCEPT 4: DECISION-SURFACE RENDERING\n**Round 2 verdict status: CHANGED — novelty claim narrowed further, prior art extended**\n\n**WHAT THE EVIDENCE SHOWS (Round 2 additions)**\n\nTwo new papers from Round 2 substantially extend the prior art picture.\n\n**NL2INTERFACE** (arXiv, Round 2, Query 13):\n> "We develop NL2INTERFACE to explore the potential of generating usable interactive multi-visualization interfaces from natural language queries. With NL2INTERFACE, users can directly write natural language queries to automatically generate a fully interactive multi-visualization interface without any extra effort of learning a tool or programming language. Further, users can interact with the interfaces to easily transform the data and quickly see the results in the visualizations."\n\nNL2INTERFACE is directly relevant. It generates interactive multi-visualization interfaces from natural language queries. The system produces browser-rendered interactive artifacts from a communicative goal expressed in natural language. This covers one of the specific features that Round 1 identified as potentially novel: agent-generated, browser-rendered artifacts from natural-language communicative goals.\n\n**"Semantic knowledge in generation of 3D layouts for decision-making"** (OpenAlex, Round 2, Query 14 and 20):\n> "The paper proposes a methodology to separate the knowledge about objects, spatial relationships, and constraints from the generative process. The separation between the knowledge in a domain and its possible practical uses is an important achievement of semantic technologies, because it grants access to a large body of knowledge, spanning various aspects and processes across buildings and cities, which is being codified into formal ontologies. The present study proposes a methodology for generating spatial layouts for decision making."\n\nThis paper describes generating spatial layouts for decision-making from domain knowledge. While its domain is architectural/urban planning, the architectural move — separating domain knowledge from generative spatial layout for decision purposes — is structurally analogous to the decision surface's core mechanism.\n\n**"Web-enabled spatial decision analysis using Ordered Weighted Averaging"** (OpenAlex, Round 2, Query 12): Web-deployed spatial MCDM — browser-accessible criteria-weighted spatial analysis. Confirms that web-rendered spatial decision tools exist in the MCDM literature.\n\n**WHAT THIS MIGHT IMPLY**\n\nAfter Round 2, the prior art picture for Concept 4 covers:\n- Criteria-weighted option positioning and sensitivity analysis: MCDM literature (established from Round 1)\n- Browser-rendered interactive multi-visualization from natural language: NL2INTERFACE\n- Agent-generated spatial layouts for decision-making from domain knowledge: "Semantic knowledge in 3D layouts"\n- Web-deployed spatial MCDM: Web-enabled OWA paper\n\nWhat remains potentially novel: a system that specifically (a) decomposes a *communicative goal* (not a data query) into decision criteria, (b) positions options in criteria space, (c) exposes criteria weighting as interactive sliders that reposition options in real time, and (d) frames the output as a persuasive communication artifact directed at a specific audience rather than as an analytical tool for an expert.\n\nThe distinction between an *analytical* MCDM tool and a *communicative* decision surface artifact is the remaining novelty claim. NL2INTERFACE narrows it further because it generates visualizations from natural language queries. The concept must be reframed around the *communicative framing and audience-orientation* as the differentiating feature, not the spatial geometry or the agent derivation.\n\n**REVISED VERDICT: ⚠️ NARROWLY NOVEL — VERDICT CHANGED (narrowed further)**\n\nRound 2 narrows the novelty claim significantly. NL2INTERFACE demonstrates natural-language-to-interactive-visualization generation. The decision-surface concept's remaining claim is the specific combination of: MCDM computational operations + communicative framing for a specific audience + interactive criteria reweighting as a reader persuasion mechanism rather than an analyst configuration tool. This is a genuinely specific framing but it is narrow, and the concept description must be rewritten to make this the stated claim.\n\n---\n\n### CONCEPT 5: RELATIONAL REGISTER ADAPTATION\n**Round 2 verdict status: LEFT UNCHANGED (verdict confirmed, confidence slightly increased)**\n\n**WHAT THE EVIDENCE SHOWS (Round 2 additions)**\n\n**"Computational Register Analysis and Synthesis"** (arXiv) was re-confirmed in Round 2, Query 16. The content is unchanged from Round 1.\n\nThe style transfer query (Round 2, Query 15) was a complete failure — the word "AXIS" in the query returned an X-ray telescope instrument paper that dominated results. No sociolinguistic register formalization work was retrieved. This means the query vocabulary continues to fail to reach the relevant literature on computational formalization of politeness and social-relational parameters.\n\nNo new substantive prior art for Concept 5 was found in Round 2. The only relevant hit remains the register synthesis survey paper from Round 1.\n\n**WHAT THIS MIGHT IMPLY**\n\nThe Round 1 verdict stands: computational register synthesis is a documented field, and the concept operates within it. The specific contribution — a four-axis formal relational map (authority direction, expertise direction, trust baseline, action threshold) that is explicit to the human, correctable through natural language, and structurally prior to rhetorical organization — remains uncontradicted by retrieved evidence.\n\nHowever, the retrieval failure on the style-transfer and politeness-theory queries means that a body of literature exists that I cannot reach with the available query vocabulary. Brown and Levinson's politeness theory has been implemented computationally in dialogue systems; this is my recollection, not a retrieved finding. Until those papers are retrieved, I cannot be confident the four-axis model is genuinely novel within the computational formalization of social register.\n\n**REVISED VERDICT: ⚠️ PARTIALLY NOVEL — UNCHANGED**\n\nRound 2 did not change this verdict. The computational register synthesis field is confirmed as the relevant prior-art domain. The concept's specific contribution — explicit four-axis relational map as a correctable, structurally-prior generative input — is not described in retrieved literature but the retrieval has not reached the politeness-theory computational literature. Confidence is slightly higher than Round 1 (the register synthesis survey was confirmed, no new contradicting evidence appeared) but the unresolved retrieval gap remains.\n\n---\n\n### CONCEPT 6: DIEGETIC DATA EMBEDDING\n**Round 2 verdict status: LEFT UNCHANGED (retrieval still inadequate; verdict unchanged)**\n\n**WHAT THE EVIDENCE SHOWS (Round 2 additions)**\n\nThe diegetic interface query (Round 2, Query 17) returned game narrative papers but no diegetic display / data encoding work. The isotype query (Round 2, Query 18) returned Byzantine encoding papers — complete miss. The infographic automation queries (Round 2, Queries 19–21) produced three partially relevant papers:\n\n**"A survey on automatic infographics and visualization recommendations"** (OpenAlex):\n> "Automatic infographics generators employ machine learning algorithms/user-defined rules and visual embellishments into the creation of infographics… automatic tools aim to lower the barrier for visual communication."\n\nThis survey covers the automatic infographics generation field. It confirms that automated infographic creation is an active area but does not describe systems that construct domain world models and map entity properties to data dimensions.\n\n**"Towards Automated Infographic Design: Deep Learning-based Auto-Extraction of Extensible Timeline"** (arXiv): Automated extraction of timeline infographic templates from images — template-based, not world-model-based. Not directly relevant.\n\n**"InfoAlign: A Human-AI Co-Creation System for Storytelling with Infographics"** (OpenAlex): Human-AI collaborative infographic authoring. Human-driven world model, not agent-derived.\n\n**"Leveraging Foundation Models for Crafting Narrative Visualization"** (OpenAlex): Foundation models generating narrative visualizations. Relevant background but does not address domain-entity world model construction.\n\nNone of these papers describes: (a) an agent constructing a world model for an arbitrary business domain, (b) mapping data dimensions to visual properties of domain entities, (c) generating a communication artifact without human world-model specification.\n\n**WHAT THIS MIGHT IMPLY**\n\nThe verdict remains what it was in Round 1: the retrieval has not reached the relevant literature. The diegetic display literature (game studies, HCI venues), the Isotype tradition (design history), and the data journalism literature (NYT, The Economist's visual journalism practice) are not indexed in the sources used at the query formulations tried. The automatic infographics literature was reached but does not overlap with the diegetic embedding mechanism.\n\nThe *diegetic principle* has precedents I cannot retrieve (Schell, game UI literature — recollection only). The *agent-generative mechanism for arbitrary domains* appears absent from the retrieved infographics and visualization literature, but I cannot rule out HCI work at CHI or UIST that the current retrieval does not reach.\n\n**REVISED VERDICT: ⚠️ NO PRIOR ART FOUND IN THIS RETRIEVAL — UNCHANGED**\n\nRound 2 did not change this verdict. Two rounds of retrieval have failed to reach the relevant prior-art domains. This is a retrieval failure, not evidence of novelty. The verdict cannot be used to support a novelty claim.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 3: THE APT QUESTION\n\n**Mackinlay APT (1986) — direct verdict on Concept 3's novelty claim**\n\nAPT was retrieved as a confirmed live source in Round 2 via both Semantic Scholar and OpenAlex (two entries across two queries). The full abstract is available. This is no longer a recollection; it is retrieved evidence.\n\n**What APT describes (from retrieved abstract, OpenAlex):**\n\nAPT treats graphical presentations as "sentences of graphical languages" and formally specifies: (a) a vocabulary of encoding primitives whose perceptual properties are characterised; (b) expressiveness criteria (which primitive can faithfully represent which data property types — ordinal, quantitative, nominal); (c) effectiveness criteria (which primitive communicates most efficiently for a given data type); (d) an automated system that takes relational data as input and derives graphical form by selecting primitives according to these criteria.\n\nThis is the formal derivation of visual form from data dimensionality analysis that Concept 3 proposes. The pipeline is: characterise data (type, relations) → apply expressiveness/effectiveness criteria → derive primitive selection → compose output. Concept 3's pipeline is: perform dimensionality analysis → select minimal primitive set from vocabulary → compose spatial output. These are the same pipeline.\n\n**What this does to Concept 3's novelty claim:**\n\nThe core claim — that deriving spatial primitive selection from formal information dimensionality analysis is not standard practice — does not survive contact with the retrieved APT evidence. APT is the 1986 documented origin of exactly this approach. It was followed by Grammar of Graphics (Wilkinson), Munzner's nested model (not retrieved as primary but now confirmed via the GoG practitioner literature), VizML (retrieved), the visualization recommendation literature (retrieved), and Encodable (retrieved). This is a continuous 40-year research programme.\n\n**What Concept 3 could claim that APT does not cover:**\n\n1. APT addresses data exploration and analysis; Concept 3 is positioned as a *communicative* artifact for inter-human communication, not data exploration. APT does not address communicative register, audience adaptation, or the persuasion/information distinction.\n2. APT's primitive vocabulary (position, length, angle, slope, area, colour, texture, shape, orientation) is primarily focused on quantitative data relationships. Concept 3's spatial primitives (position, scale, proximity, containment, sequence, path) emphasize structural/relational encoding rather than quantitative magnitude encoding — a different emphasis, though not a different genus.\n3. APT assumes a human will use the generated presentation for analysis; Concept 3's minimum-information encoding principle is motivated by *communication efficiency* (what is the least complex presentation that conveys the intended epistemic content to a specific reader?). APT's effectiveness criteria are perceptual, not communicative.\n\n**Verdict on Concept 3 following APT retrieval:**\n\nThe concept's novelty claim as written does not survive. The primitive-from-dimensionality-analysis pipeline is APT. If the concept is to carry a novelty claim, it must be reframed entirely around the communicative framing (not the technical mechanism): the agent is selecting primitives to achieve a communicative goal for a specific audience, not to express data relationships accurately for an analyst. That reframing would require the concept to engage explicitly with APT and the visualization recommendation literature as the prior art it is working beyond.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 4: CLOSING RECOMMENDATION TO SOPHIE\n\nThis recommendation applies the combined evidence from both rounds. I am sorting the six concepts into three categories.\n\n---\n\n### CATEGORY A: SAFE TO CARRY INTO SYNTHESIS AS STATED\n*Verdict is grounded in retrieved evidence; novelty claim holds with appropriate hedging*\n\n**None of the six concepts qualify for Category A without qualification.**\n\nThe most advanced concepts are 1 and 5, but both carry prior art that must be acknowledged in any synthesis. I am declining to put either in Category A without the restatement requirement below.\n\n---\n\n### CATEGORY B: CARRY INTO SYNTHESIS BUT RESTATE THE NOVELTY CLAIM FIRST\n*The concept has real potential but the stated novelty claim is either too broad or imprecise; the concept is viable if the claim is narrowed to what the evidence actually shows is new*\n\n**Concept 1 — The Commitment Audit**\n\nCarry forward. The claim should be restated to: "PaperTrail demonstrates claim-evidence provenance mapping for LLM outputs; Epistemic Blinding demonstrates inference-time epistemic auditing. The Commitment Audit's specific contribution is continuous confidence scoring across four epistemic categories, visual weight deriving from confidence rather than rhetorical emphasis, and a minimum-spanning-argument path as a reading scaffold. None of these specific mechanisms are described in retrieved prior art." The concept is viable if presented as extending rather than replacing this literature.\n\n**Concept 5 — Relational Register Adaptation**\n\nCarry forward. The claim should be restated to: "Computational register synthesis is a documented field (Computational Register Analysis and Synthesis, arXiv). The concept's specific contribution is a formal four-axis relational map — authority direction, expertise direction, trust baseline, action threshold — that is: explicit as a correctable intermediate artifact; revisable through natural language before generation; and structurally prior to rhetorical organization rather than used as a post-hoc label. This specific combination is not described in retrieved literature, though the politeness-theory computational literature has not been fully reached." The concept needs a qualified novelty claim, not an unqualified one.\n\n**Concept 2 — Audience-in-the-Loop Simulation**\n\nCarry forward only with a restated claim. PosterMate and Proxona are prior art for LLM audience persona simulation as a design tool. The restated claim must be: "The concept's novelty is the simulation trace as a *reader-facing* artifact component that is exposed in the final artifact, and the audience model as *structurally prior to form selection* rather than as a feedback mechanism on a selected form." If Sophie or the founder reads the concept description and sees "making the audience model explicit," they will be reading PosterMate, which is 2025 published work. The current framing is misleading without this restatement.\n\n---\n\n### CATEGORY C: PRIOR-ART WORK THIS TEAM CANNOT CURRENTLY PERFORM WITH AVAILABLE SOURCES — DO NOT SHOW AS NOVEL UNTIL RESOLVED\n\n**Concept 3 — Dimensionality-First Rendering**\n\nDo not carry the current novelty claim into any synthesis. APT is retrieved evidence showing that the core mechanism is a 40-year-old research programme. The concept needs to be either: (a) reframed entirely around communicative artifact generation as distinct from data visualization — in which case it must engage explicitly with APT as prior art and articulate what is different; or (b) replaced with a different framing. As currently written, the claim that "deriving spatial representation from dimensionality analysis is not standard practice" is directly contradicted by retrieved evidence. Showing this to a founder in its current form is not defensible.\n\n**Concept 4 — Decision-Surface Rendering**\n\nDo not carry the current stated novelty claim forward. The concept has a viable narrow claim — the communicative, audience-oriented, persuasion-framed interactive decision artifact as distinct from analytical MCDM tools — but the current description does not make this the claim. NL2INTERFACE (Round 2) covers natural-language-to-interactive-visualization generation. The MCDM literature covers the computational operations. The restated concept would be viable but requires rewriting the concept description before it is shown to anyone. This team can do that restatement; what it cannot do is confirm whether the restated narrower claim is genuinely absent from the CHI/UIST human-computer interaction literature on decision support interfaces, which was not reached in either retrieval round.\n\n**Concept 6 — Diegetic Data Embedding**\n\nDo not show as novel under any framing. Two retrieval rounds have failed to reach the relevant prior-art domains: diegetic display literature (game studies, HCI), Isotype tradition (design history), data journalism practice. The retrieval does not support a novelty verdict in either direction. Before this concept is presented to any external party, a targeted search is needed in: ACM Digital Library (CHI, UIST, DIS proceedings) for "diegetic visualization" or "embodied data display"; data journalism collections (Source, Malofiej proceedings); and the Isotype archive literature in design history journals. This team cannot perform that search with arXiv, Semantic Scholar, and OpenAlex at current query vocabulary. The concept may well be novel — the mechanism described has no direct analog in any retrieved paper — but I cannot say so on this evidence.\n\n---\n\n*Kenji Ochiai — Researcher, Altair8 — Sprint 10, Round 2 revision*	2026-08-20 08:03:03.310475	2026-08-20 08:09:32.304512	prior_art_verification	{"round": 2, "memory_key": "kenji/sprint10_concept_verification", "queries_run": 21, "results_retrieved": 415}
85	team_leader	team_leader	Sprint 10 Pass 4: synthesise concepts into a decision input	Comparative format for the founder. No new claims; Sophie's own observations marked.	completed	# SPRINT 10, PASS 4 — ASSEMBLED COMPARISON\n\n*Sophie Marchetti, Team Leader, Altair8*\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 1 — THE COMPARISON TABLE\n\n| Concept | What it would build | Generative input form derives from | Goal space served | Kenji's verdict / category | Falsification buildable? | Single biggest named risk |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **C1: Commitment Audit** | Argument map where node visual weight encodes agent's confidence in each claim's epistemic status; reader can expand/collapse branches; minimum-spanning path is default | Agent's epistemic self-audit of its own content (confidence scores across evidence / inference / assumption / assertion) | Persuade a sceptical decision-maker or small team | ⚠️ Partially novel — **Category B** (restate claim re: PaperTrail, Epistemic Blinding) | **Buildable** | Design Principle 3 tension: interactive filter layer assumes analytical reading literacy the concept cannot guarantee |\n| **C2: Audience-in-the-Loop Simulation** | Artifact plus visible, reader-facing simulation trace showing agent's predicted audience attention, questions, and next action; trace is contestable via natural language | Agent's explicit, discrete audience model (role, prior knowledge, stakes), computed before form selection | Persuade/align where audience is specific and known | ⚠️ Narrowly novel — **Category B** (PosterMate and Proxona are prior art for author-facing simulation; claim must be restated to reader-facing trace + structurally prior form selection) | Cannot judge without knowing whether ground-truth audience response corpus exists | Audience simulation may be a plausible-sounding hallucination if agent's model performs at or near chance (Pass 1 falsification threshold: <50% agreement with expert-rated actual responses) |\n| **C3: Dimensionality-First Rendering** | Spatial HTML5 environment assembled from composable primitives (position, scale, proximity, containment, sequence, path) selected by dimensionality analysis; no chart-type selection | Intrinsic dimensional structure of the information (number of independent variables, variable types, primary relationship structure) | Inform a working team with genuinely multidimensional content | ⚠️ Not novel as stated — **Category C** (APT 1986 retrieved; core mechanism is a 40-year research programme; claim must be entirely reframed before showing to anyone) | **Buildable** | APT and visualization recommendation literature directly contradict the stated novelty claim; concept requires full reframing before any external presentation |\n| **C4: Decision-Surface Rendering** | Interactive criteria-space map; options positioned on it; reader drags sliders to reweight criteria and watches option positions shift; recommendation robustness shown in real time | Decision geometry of the communicative goal (option space, criteria space, criteria weights, recommendation with confidence) | Persuade-to-decide only (explicitly does not serve inform, align, or report) | ⚠️ Narrowly novel — **Category C** (NL2INTERFACE covers NL-to-interactive-visualization; MCDM literature covers computational operations; novelty claim must be rewritten to communicative/audience-oriented framing before showing to anyone) | **Not buildable** (expert-rated decision quality across two participant pools + expert raters cannot be recruited, run, and scored in one sprint) | Narrow scope by design: adopting this concept as primary leaves inform, align, and report unaddressed |\n| **C5: Relational Register Adaptation** | Artifact whose rhetorical structure — what must be established before the main claim, what can be asserted, what the closing move is — is derived from a four-axis relational map (authority direction, expertise direction, trust baseline, action threshold); visual form follows structure | Social and epistemic relationship between sender and receiver | All four goal regions; strongest where relational stakes are high | ⚠️ Partially novel — **Category B** (computational register synthesis is documented; four-axis explicit correctable map as structurally prior to rhetorical organization not described in retrieved literature; politeness-theory computational literature not reached) | **Not buildable** (48-hour action-rate observation window eats minimum two days inside a sprint before recruitment, sessions, or follow-up can occur) | Concept is only as good as the human's self-knowledge about their social position; miscalibrated relational map produces confidently wrong artifact with no internal detection mechanism |\n| **C6: Diegetic Data Embedding** | Domain world model in which data is encoded into visual properties of domain entities themselves (route thickness = cost, pulse rate = throughput); no separate chart panel; data inseparable from domain depiction | Agent's world model of the domain (entities, relationships, natural visual forms) plus data-property mapping | Inform and align where audience is familiar with the domain | ⚠️ No prior art found in this retrieval — **Category C** (two retrieval rounds failed to reach diegetic display literature, Isotype tradition, data journalism practice; verdict is retrieval failure, not evidence of novelty) | Not assessed by Mateo | World model must be recognisable to the audience; if not, artifact is irrelevant rather than confusing; agent-generated world model for arbitrary business domains is the hardest generation problem in the full set |\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 2 — WHERE THE CONCEPTS GENUINELY DIFFER\n\n### Axis 1: What the agent reasons about before generating anything\n\nThe six concepts split on what computation precedes form selection.\n\nC1 has the agent audit its own claims — classifying each as evidence, inference, assumption, or assertion and scoring confidence. The form is downstream of that self-examination. C2 has the agent simulate the audience — predict what they will attend to, question, and do. The form is downstream of that prediction. C3 has the agent analyse the information's intrinsic dimensional structure — how many independent variables, what types, what relationships. C4 has the agent decompose the decision the reader must make — options, criteria, weights, recommendation robustness. C5 has the agent map the social relationship between sender and receiver. C6 has the agent construct a world model of the domain itself.\n\n[SOPHIE] Choosing between these is not primarily a technical choice — it is a choice about which variable the team believes is most underweighted in current AI communication systems: the agent's epistemic self-awareness (C1), its model of the reader (C2), the information's own structure (C3), the decision the reader must make (C4), the social context of the exchange (C5), or the domain being talked about (C6). These are genuinely different diagnoses of why current systems fail.\n\n### Axis 2: Who the artifact is built for\n\nC1, C4, and C5 build artifacts calibrated to a specific, known reader or small group. C3 and C6 build artifacts calibrated to the information or domain itself, with the reader as secondary. C2 is the only concept that makes the calibration to a specific reader structurally visible *in the artifact itself* — the simulation trace is reader-facing, not just author-facing. This is the distinction that separates C2 from PosterMate and Proxona, per Kenji's revised verdict.\n\n### Axis 3: What the human controls\n\nAll five Pass 1 concepts route human control through natural language. What the natural language targets differs: in C1, it targets the confidence threshold for what appears in the default reading path. In C2, it targets the audience model directly. In C3, it targets spatial instructions parsed as dimensionality reductions. In C4, it targets criteria weights and option visibility. In C5, it targets the relational parameters (trust baseline, authority direction). In C6, it targets either the world model structure or the data-property mapping. [SOPHIE] These are different points of intervention in the generation pipeline, and they imply different assumptions about what the human knows well enough to correct. A human who does not know their audience well cannot improve C2; a human who does not understand the decision their audience must make cannot improve C4; a human who does not know their social position accurately cannot improve C5.\n\n### Axis 4: Which goal region each serves\n\nC1, C2, C4, and C5 are strongest for persuasion and alignment goals where the audience is specific and known. C3 and C6 are strongest for informing goals where the content has genuine structure the reader needs to apprehend correctly. C4 explicitly does not serve inform, align, or report. C5 is claimed to serve all four regions, though Pass 1 acknowledged it is weakest for distributed audiences where the relational map cannot be accurately specified.\n\n[SOPHIE] This axis matters for how the team frames the research programme: a concept that serves only one goal region requires either scope limitation or pairing with another concept; a concept that serves all four risks being optimised for none.\n\n### Axis 5: Novelty and prior art exposure\n\nC1 and C5 are in Category B — viable, specific claims, carry forward with restated framing. C2 is in Category B with a more urgent restatement requirement: PosterMate and Proxona are 2025 published work that directly overlaps the unqualified version of the claim. C3 and C4 are in Category C — cannot be shown to anyone in current form. C6 is in Category C for a different reason: the retrieval simply did not reach the relevant prior-art domains, so neither a novelty claim nor a prior-art acknowledgement is defensible yet.\n\n[SOPHIE] C3 and C6 are in Category C for structurally different reasons that matter to how they could be recovered. C3 has a specific problem: retrieved evidence directly contradicts the stated claim. Recovery requires reframing the concept to articulate what is different from APT and the visualization recommendation literature — difficult but possible. C6 has a different problem: absence of evidence is not evidence of absence, and the relevant literature simply was not reached. Recovery requires targeted search in ACM Digital Library proceedings (CHI, UIST, DIS), data journalism collections, and design history sources. This is a resolvable gap if the right search is run.\n\n### Axis 6: Implementation difficulty\n\nPass 2 named implementation gravity explicitly for C3 (the gap between ideal arbitrary spatial environments and what Mateo can build in a sprint using three or four primitives) and C6 (world model construction for arbitrary business domains is the hardest generation problem in the full set). C1, C2, C4, and C5 do not have the same architectural gap flagged, though C4's falsification test was rated not buildable by Mateo. [SOPHIE] The buildability of the falsification test and the buildability of the concept itself are different questions — C4's prototype may be buildable even if the full falsification study is not.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 3 — WHAT THE SPRINT DID NOT ESTABLISH\n\n**State plainly.**\n\n**Kenji placed no concept in Category A.** Every concept in this sprint carries either a prior-art qualification, a retrieval-failure caveat, or both. The comparison table above will read more decisively than the evidence under it. The verdicts in the table are Kenji's best assessment across two retrieval rounds totalling approximately 835 results at a ~93.5% noise rate; they are not confirmations.\n\n**Three concepts cannot be shown externally in current form.**\n\n- **C3 (Dimensionality-First Rendering):** The stated novelty claim — that deriving form from dimensionality analysis using composable primitives is not standard practice — is directly contradicted by retrieved evidence. APT (1986), Grammar of Graphics, VizML, and the visualization recommendation literature constitute a 40-year research programme doing exactly this. The concept cannot be presented without full reframing that engages explicitly with this prior art.\n\n- **C4 (Decision-Surface Rendering):** NL2INTERFACE (retrieved, Round 2) covers natural-language-to-interactive-visualization generation. The MCDM literature covers criteria-weighted option positioning. The current novelty framing does not survive these. The concept has a viable narrow claim — communicative, audience-oriented, persuasion-framed decision artifact as distinct from analytical tools — but the concept description must be rewritten to make that the stated claim.\n\n- **C6 (Diegetic Data Embedding):** Two retrieval rounds failed to reach the diegetic display literature (game studies, HCI venues), the Isotype tradition (design history), and data journalism practice. The verdict "no prior art found" is a retrieval failure, not a finding. The concept cannot be shown as novel on current evidence.\n\n**Two concepts require restated novelty claims before external presentation.**\n\n- **C1 (Commitment Audit):** Must acknowledge PaperTrail (claim-evidence provenance mapping) and Epistemic Blinding (inference-time epistemic auditing) as prior art, and state the concept's contribution specifically as: continuous confidence scoring across four categories, visual weight from confidence rather than rhetorical emphasis, and minimum-spanning-argument scaffold. The unqualified version of the claim does not hold.\n\n- **C2 (Audience-in-the-Loop Simulation):** Must acknowledge PosterMate and Proxona (2025 published work) as prior art for LLM audience simulation as a design tool, and state the concept's contribution specifically as: simulation trace as a reader-facing artifact component exposed in the final output, and audience model as structurally prior to form selection rather than posterior feedback. The unqualified version of the claim is misleading.\n\n**C5 carries an unresolved retrieval gap.** The politeness-theory computational literature (Brown and Levinson implementations in dialogue systems) was not reached in either retrieval round due to query vocabulary failures. Kenji's Round 2 note that this literature exists but is unretrieved remains unresolved. The concept's partial novelty verdict holds on current evidence but cannot be confirmed without reaching that literature.\n\n**Two falsification tests are not buildable in one sprint** (C4, C5). One cannot be judged without knowing whether a ground-truth audience response corpus exists (C2).\n\n**The NLG planning literature (1990s–2000s) was not fully reached for C2.** Kenji explicitly flagged that works by Paris, Moore, Reiter and Dale on user-model-driven generation were not retrieved as primary sources — only in survey form. This remains an open gap in the C2 prior-art picture.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 4 — WHAT A DECISION HERE WOULD ACTUALLY COMMIT TO\n\n### If the team builds C1 (Commitment Audit) first\n\nThe team commits to the agent's epistemic self-model as the primary design variable. Mateo would build a prototype with four epistemic category types, continuous confidence scoring, confidence-weighted visual layout, and a minimum-spanning-argument default path with expandable branches. The novelty claim must be restated relative to PaperTrail and Epistemic Blinding before any external presentation. The falsification test is buildable in one sprint per Mateo. The interaction log component of that test — tracking whether users expand the minimum-spanning path anyway — is a secondary falsifier that requires the prototype to include logging. [SOPHIE] This is the concept most tightly coupled to failures 4 and 5 (confabulated content passing structural checks), which were central to the sprint brief's failure data. If that is the team's sharpest pain point, this is the most targeted response.\n\n### If the team builds C2 (Audience-in-the-Loop Simulation) first\n\nThe team commits to the audience model as structurally prior to form selection, and to making that model visible and contestable in the final artifact. The novelty claim must be restated relative to PosterMate and Proxona — the restatement is more urgent here than for any other Category B concept because the overlap with existing 2025 HCI work is the most direct. Building the prototype requires resolving what the simulation mechanism actually is and whether historical ground-truth audience response data exists to run the falsification test; Mateo cannot judge buildability without this. [SOPHIE] The concept is the only one in the set that places the agent's reasoning about the reader into the reader's hands — that is a structurally different design move from the other five, and it is the one most directly aligned with the team's thesis that the reader-artifact relationship is the primary design space, per Naledi's meta-finding in Pass 2.\n\n### If the team builds C3 (Dimensionality-First Rendering) first\n\nThe team commits to reframing the concept entirely before building. The current novelty claim cannot be used. The concept would need to be repositioned as something that APT and the visualization recommendation literature do not already describe — which Kenji proposes could be the communicative framing (selecting primitives to serve a communicative goal for a specific audience, not to express data relationships for an analyst). Mateo rates the falsification test as buildable, but the constrained prototype (three or four primitives rather than arbitrary spatial environments) risks reintroducing chart-type conventions through the back door if the primitive set is too small, per Pass 1's named tension. [SOPHIE] Building C3 without first resolving the APT question would produce a prototype whose intellectual positioning is unclear, which is a different kind of risk from the other concepts — not a technical risk but a research validity risk.\n\n### If the team builds C4 (Decision-Surface Rendering) first\n\nThe team commits to a concept that explicitly does not serve inform, align, or report. The concept would need to be rewritten to foreground the communicative/audience-oriented framing as its novelty claim, distinguishing it from analytical MCDM tools. Mateo rates the falsification test as not buildable in one sprint; the team would need a smaller pilot test or a different evaluation approach. The concept by design requires pairing with other concepts if the team's goal is to serve the full goal space. [SOPHIE] The decision surface's interactive criteria-reweighting feature — where the reader can stress-test the recommendation against their own value weights — is the specific feature that Pass 1 identified as not described in prior art; that feature is also the one most at risk from NL2INTERFACE's coverage of NL-to-interactive-visualization generation, and the rewrite would need to address this directly.\n\n### If the team builds C5 (Relational Register Adaptation) first\n\nThe team commits to social and epistemic relationship as the primary generative variable. The four-axis relational map (authority, expertise, trust, action threshold) would need to be formalized and tested. The falsification test is not buildable in one sprint due to the 48-hour action-rate observation window. Pass 1 explicitly named that this concept has a category of failure — the human's relational self-model being wrong — that the architecture cannot self-correct and that the cheapest experiment cannot reach. Design Principle 4 tension was named: a relational register calling for restraint may produce a visually conventional artifact, and Pass 1's position was that relational appropriateness should take precedence over visual complexity in that case. [SOPHIE] C5 is the only concept in the set whose primary failure mode is not detectable from inside the system — the artifact produced by a miscalibrated relational map looks exactly correct. Building this concept first would require the team to make an early decision about whether that invisible failure mode is acceptable or whether an internal detection mechanism is a prerequisite.\n\n### If the team builds C6 (Diegetic Data Embedding) first\n\nThe team commits to resolving the retrieval gap before building. Two retrieval rounds have not reached the relevant prior-art domains; proceeding to build without that search means building on an unverified novelty claim. Pass 2 identified the concept as the most direct implementation of the insight that the reader-artifact relationship needs redesigning — and also named world model construction for arbitrary business domains as the hardest generation problem in the full set. The constrained prototype (three or four pre-selected domain types) is not the same concept at full scope, per Pass 2's explicit naming of this gap. [SOPHIE] C6 is the concept the team did not find blind — it required Naledi's survey to surface — which means it is the one with the most distance from the failure-data-derived reasoning that generated C1 through C5. Whether that distance is a strength (genuinely different angle) or a risk (less grounded in the sprint's specific failure analysis) is not established by the sprint.\n\n---\n\n*The table in Section 1 and the axes in Section 2 are the founder's scanning surface. Section 3 is the constraint on how decisively any of it should be read. Section 4 is what acting on it would mean.*	2026-08-20 08:12:37.910328	2026-08-20 08:14:37.605056	sprint_synthesis	{"pass": 4, "memory_key": "team_leader/sprint10_synthesis", "sophie_marks": 11}
