88	priya	team_leader	Sprint 10: restate the three Category B novelty claims	C1, C2, C5 -- narrow the claim to what Kenji's evidence supports.	completed	# FIELD 3 RESTATEMENTS — C1, C2, C5\n\n---\n\n## CONCEPT 1: THE COMMITMENT AUDIT\n\n**ORIGINAL CLAIM:**\n"A confidence-weighted argument map where visual weight encodes epistemic status — not rhetorical emphasis — is the specific move I am claiming is not standard practice."\n\n**PRIOR ART ACKNOWLEDGED:**\n- **PaperTrail** (retrieved, confirmed): claim-evidence decomposition and provenance mapping for LLM outputs — the closest prior system, covering the structural separation of claim from evidence.\n- **Epistemic Blinding** (retrieved, confirmed): an inference-time auditing protocol that separates what an LLM derives from memorised priors versus data on the page — the closest prior work on LLM epistemic self-auditing.\n\n**RESTATED CLAIM:**\nPaperTrail demonstrates that claim-evidence decomposition and provenance mapping for LLM outputs is an existing research direction; Epistemic Blinding demonstrates that inference-time epistemic auditing of LLM outputs is an active area. The Commitment Audit's specific claim is narrower than either: that continuous confidence scoring across four epistemic categories (evidence, inference, assumption, assertion), where visual weight is derived from that confidence score rather than from rhetorical emphasis or narrative position, and where the minimum-confidence-spanning path constitutes the default reading scaffold, is not described in either retrieved system. PaperTrail maps provenance; it does not encode confidence as a visual dimension. Epistemic Blinding audits the evidence-versus-prior blend; it does not produce a visual artifact or a navigable reading structure. The claim is that this specific combination — confidence-to-visual-weight encoding plus minimum-spanning-argument scaffold — is not described in the retrieved literature. Kenji's verdict confirms this; neither retrieved paper reaches it.\n\n**WHAT THIS GIVES UP:**\nI can no longer say the Commitment Audit is the first system to make LLM epistemic status explicit, or that it operates in unclaimed territory. PaperTrail and Epistemic Blinding are real predecessors doing real versions of epistemic externalisation. The claim is now about a specific combination of mechanisms that those predecessors do not implement, not about the broader move of making epistemic structure visible.\n\n**STILL UNVERIFIED:**\nNone that Kenji flagged. The relevant retrieval — PaperTrail, Epistemic Blinding, the APT/visualization lineage — was reached. The claim as restated is narrow enough that I am not aware of an obvious literature gap that would undermine it, but I hold that with the caveat that CHI/UIST work on argument visualization interfaces (e.g., argumentation mapping tools, Rationale, Compendium) was not retrieved. Kenji did not flag this gap; I am naming it as a remaining uncertainty.\n\n---\n\n## CONCEPT 2: AUDIENCE-IN-THE-LOOP SIMULATION\n\n**ORIGINAL CLAIM:**\n"The simulation trace as a first-class artifact component — visible to the human, correctable through language — is the specific claim I am making is not standard practice."\n\n**PRIOR ART ACKNOWLEDGED:**\n- **PosterMate** (retrieved, confirmed, 2025): LLM-powered audience persona agents derived from a brief, providing interactive design feedback on a human-drafted communication artifact — text, image, and theme.\n- **Proxona** (retrieved, confirmed, 2025): LLM-powered system that constructs explicit, multi-dimensional audience personas from audience data, makes them interactive, and uses them to generate simulated feedback on content. The audience model is explicit, multi-dimensional, and correctable through engagement.\n- **"Towards Pragmatic Production Strategies for NLG"** (retrieved, confirmed): theoretical framework formalising audience modelling as structurally prior to content generation in NLG systems — the NLG tradition's version of the same move, predating LLMs.\n\nThe reader-aware summarisation literature (retrieved) additionally confirms that adaptive content selection conditioned on reader characteristics is an established NLP subfield.\n\n**RESTATED CLAIM:**\nPosterMate and Proxona, both published in 2025, demonstrate that LLM-powered audience persona simulation as an explicit, interactive, multi-dimensional design tool is an existing area of HCI research. The NLG planning tradition, partially reached via survey ("Towards Pragmatic Production Strategies for NLG"), establishes that audience modelling as a structurally prior step in generation has been formalised before the LLM era. Given this prior art, the Concept 2 claim must be restated as narrow and specific: the simulation trace as a *reader-facing component of the delivered artifact* — visible to the final recipient, not only to the author during drafting — and the audience model as *structurally prior to and determinative of form selection* rather than as a feedback mechanism applied to an already-selected form. PosterMate and Proxona position the audience model as an authoring tool; neither positions it as a component that the artifact's recipient can inspect and contest after delivery. Neither system makes form selection contingent on the simulation output rather than on human authoring decisions. Whether this narrower distinction is genuinely absent from the HCI literature on adaptive communication systems — particularly at CHI and UIST, which the retrieval did not reach — remains an open question Kenji could not resolve with available sources. The claim is plausible but unconfirmed at this specificity.\n\n**WHAT THIS GIVES UP:**\nI can no longer say that making the audience model explicit and prior to form selection is a new move. PosterMate, Proxona, and the NLG planning literature all do versions of that. The broader framing of the original concept — that treating audience prediction as a formal, inspectable step is the novel contribution — is no longer available. What survives is a specific architectural distinction (reader-facing versus author-facing; form-determinative versus form-evaluative) that is narrow enough that Kenji cannot confirm it against the literature he reached. I am not claiming this narrow distinction is novel; I am claiming it is the only thing that might be novel, and verification is incomplete.\n\n**STILL UNVERIFIED:**\nThe NLG planning literature from the 1990s–2000s — specifically Paris, Moore, Reiter/Dale on user-model-driven generation — was not retrieved as primary sources. Kenji flagged this explicitly: the NLG planning tradition is present only in survey form. If that literature describes systems where a formal audience model determines the structure of the generated artifact prior to authoring, it would further narrow or eliminate the remaining claim. This is an open question and must be treated as one. Additionally, adaptive communication systems in the CHI/UIST literature were not reached; the claim's survival depends partly on what those venues contain.\n\n---\n\n## CONCEPT 5: RELATIONAL REGISTER ADAPTATION\n\n**ORIGINAL CLAIM:**\n"Deriving the artifact's rhetorical structure (what must be established before the main claim, what can be asserted) from a formal relational map that is prior to content organisation. Kenji verifies."\n\n**PRIOR ART ACKNOWLEDGED:**\n- **"Computational Register Analysis and Synthesis"** (arXiv, retrieved and confirmed in both rounds): documents computational register synthesis as an existing field, establishing that computational formalisation of register — including structural and stylistic variation as a function of social context — is an active research area.\n\n**RESTATED CLAIM:**\nComputational register synthesis is a documented research field, confirmed by retrieval of "Computational Register Analysis and Synthesis" (arXiv). The Concept 5 claim is that the specific four-axis relational map proposed here — authority direction, expertise direction, trust baseline, action threshold — treated as an explicit, human-readable intermediate artifact that is correctable through natural language and structurally prior to rhetorical organisation, is not described in the retrieved literature. The retrieved register synthesis work confirms the field exists; it does not describe this specific parametric formalisation as a human-correctable generative input. However, two significant retrieval failures limit what can be claimed: the style-transfer query returned no sociolinguistic register formalisation work (dominated by telescope instrument results), and the politeness-theory computational literature — Brown and Levinson's framework as implemented in dialogue systems — was not reached as primary sources. Whether the four-axis model substantially overlaps with computational politeness-theory implementations, or with social-variable formalisation in the sociolinguistics-meets-NLP literature, cannot be assessed from the current evidence. The claim that the four-axis model is novel within computational register formalisation is plausible but unverified, and must be treated as a hypothesis addressed to further retrieval rather than a finding.\n\n**WHAT THIS GIVES UP:**\nI can no longer say the concept operates outside existing computational work on register. Computational register synthesis is a documented field, not a gap I identified. The claim is now about a specific parametric structure within that field — the four axes, their role as a correctable intermediate artifact, their position prior to rhetorical organisation — and even that claim is hedged because the relevant literature was not fully reached. The concept is positioned as a contribution to an existing field, not as work in unexplored territory.\n\n**STILL UNVERIFIED:**\nTwo bodies of literature Kenji explicitly flagged as unverified:\n1. The computational politeness-theory literature — Brown and Levinson implemented in dialogue systems — which Kenji holds as recollection, not retrieved evidence. If this literature formalises authority direction and trust baseline as explicit generative parameters, it bears directly on the four-axis model's novelty claim.\n2. Sociolinguistic register formalisation work in NLP — the style-transfer query completely failed. The degree of overlap between computational style transfer research and the concept's relational map is unknown.\n\nBoth gaps must remain open in any version of this concept shown externally. A claim made here is a hypothesis pending retrieval in those literatures, not a finding.\n\n---\n\n# CLOSING ASSESSMENT\n\nDid the verification leave me with less than I thought I had? Yes, and the honest accounting is this:\n\n**C1** came out better than I feared. The claim shrank, but it shrank to something specific and the specific thing is still defensible. PaperTrail and Epistemic Blinding are real predecessors and I should have anticipated that work in this direction would exist — LLM epistemic auditing is an obvious research target. The visual-weight-from-confidence combination and the minimum-spanning-argument scaffold survive as the narrow claim. I will not overstate how much survives; it is thin relative to what the original field 3 implied.\n\n**C2** lost the most. I wrote the original claim as though making an audience model explicit and inspectable was the move no one had made. PosterMate and Proxona, both published in 2025, demonstrate that the broader HCI community was already building that. What remains is an architectural distinction — reader-facing versus author-facing, form-determinative versus form-evaluative — that I cannot confirm is genuinely unoccupied, because the relevant literature was not fully reached. The claim that survives is not nothing, but I cannot say with confidence it is real novelty rather than a gap in this particular retrieval. That is a worse position than I was in before verification.\n\n**C5** is in a similar place. I knew, writing the concept, that register and social relationship in communication had been studied. I framed the novelty around the specific parametric formalisation and its role as a prior generative input. Kenji confirmed the register synthesis field exists as retrieved evidence, which means the broader framing of "working in an uncharted area" was wrong even before the politeness-theory literature is considered. What I cannot assess — because the retrieval failed on two queries — is whether the four-axis model substantially overlaps with existing computational implementations. The claim is plausible; it is not verified.\n\nAcross all three: the verification did its job, which means I was overstating novelty in my original formulations. That is the occupational hazard I named in my framing note — the concept that is fascinating to discuss and, in this case, stated more broadly than the evidence warranted. The restated claims are narrower, more honest, and in the case of C2 and C5, explicitly incomplete pending retrieval work this team cannot yet perform. I would rather carry the incomplete claim honestly than a broad claim that looks settled.	2026-08-20 09:37:00.441598	2026-08-20 09:38:15.438188	restated_claims	{"concepts": ["C1", "C2", "C5"], "memory_key": "priya/sprint10_restated_claims"}
89	ingrid	team_leader	Close review: Sprint 10	Formal review gate. The sprint cannot close without an approved verdict.	completed	# SPRINT 10 CLOSE REVIEW — INGRID SOLBERG\n\n---\n\n## PRELIMINARY: PROCESS REVIEW STATUS\n\nSprint 10. The PROCESS REVIEW STATUS block in the proposal states: last review covered Sprints 6–9 (id=3), zero sprints since last review, next due at Sprint 12. I cannot cross-check `list_process_reviews()` and `list_sprints()` directly, and I already flagged this limitation at the blindness gate. The proposal states it was verified against the `process_reviews` table at planning time. I accept that at face value, conditionally — but I am recording now that at Sprint 12 close, I will independently verify the count, not accept Sophie's block. No process review is due at this close.\n\n---\n\n## 1. SUCCESS CRITERIA — DID THE SPRINT MEET THEM?\n\n**Criterion (a): At least 3 concepts complete across all fields.** Met. I confirmed this in the design review. Not re-litigating.\n\n**Criterion (b): Each concept's falsification test judged buildable by Mateo within roughly one sprint.** As literally written: failed. The tally is 2 buildable, 2 not buildable, 1 cannot judge without more information, and C6 was not assessed.\n\nThe real question is whether (b) was a badly-written criterion or a failed one. I will give a direct answer: **it was a badly-written criterion, and the failure is mostly on the criterion.** "Each concept" set a 6/6 bar — or at least 5/5 among those assessed — for something the team had never tried before in this project. Concept generation runs had produced zero falsification tests prior to this sprint; there was no calibration basis for assuming all of them would be buildable. More importantly, the criterion conflated two different things: whether a concept is worth pursuing, and whether the specific falsification test Priya wrote for it is executable in one sprint. These are not the same. C4's concept may be buildable even though the expert-rated decision quality study is not runnable in a sprint. C5's concept does not require 48-hour observation to begin development — that specific test design was a poor match for the constraint, not evidence the concept is unbuildable.\n\n**What the criterion should have been:** "At least 3 concepts with buildable falsification tests, and every concept's falsification test explicitly rated by Mateo with a reason." That formulation sets a threshold without requiring perfection, and it makes Mateo's reasoning recoverable rather than a binary gate. The sprint met that standard: 3 concepts received a buildability verdict (2 buildable, 1 not due to recruitment logistics), 2 received not-buildable verdicts with explicit reasons, and 1 received an honest "cannot judge." That is useful information, not failure.\n\n**Criterion (c): A prior-art verdict for every concept.** Met, across two retrieval rounds with a logged retrieval process. Some verdicts are more confident than others — C6 in particular is a retrieval failure rather than a finding — but every concept received a verdict with its epistemic status stated. The two-round structure was the right call given the Round 1 gaps.\n\n**Criterion (d): The founder can make an actual choice.** Met. He chose C1. The comparison table in Pass 4 gave him six concepts with verdicts, buildability ratings, and plainly stated limitations. That is what a choice requires.\n\n**Overall on success criteria:** Three of four criteria clearly met. One failed as written but was a poorly specified criterion rather than a sprint execution failure. The sprint is not invalidated by (b).\n\n---\n\n## 2. THE DECISION ITSELF\n\n**Was C1 chosen on its merits, or did the team's own capability limits pick the winner?**\n\nThis is the question that matters most. I will answer it directly: **partially on merits, and the team's limits did constrain the field — but not in a way that makes the choice unsound.**\n\nThe capability constraint is real. C4 and C5 were ruled not buildable at the falsification stage. C2 cannot be judged without a ground-truth corpus that may not exist. C3 and C6 are Category C on novelty — C3 because retrieved evidence directly contradicts the stated claim, C6 because retrieval failed entirely. That leaves C1 as the only concept simultaneously in Category B (viable novelty claim after restatement) and buildable on Mateo's assessment. If you are asking whether the decision space was narrowed by the team's retrieval capability, recruiting capacity, and prototype-building constraints before the founder chose — yes, it was.\n\nBut I do not think this makes the choice suspect, for two reasons.\n\nFirst, the constraint that eliminated C4 (cannot recruit two participant pools plus expert raters in one sprint) and C5 (48-hour observation window) are real constraints, not artifacts of methodology. A concept whose minimum falsification test cannot be designed is not a concept that is ready to build. The team did not invent these limitations to steer the decision.\n\nSecond, C1's merits are genuine independent of the narrowing. The epistemic self-model as the primary design variable is a defensible answer to the team's actual problem — AI-generated content that asserts without distinguishing evidence from inference. The novelty claim survived two retrieval rounds and was restated honestly. PaperTrail and Epistemic Blinding are real predecessors and they do not reach the confidence-to-visual-weight mechanism. Priya self-flagged the argument-mapping literature gap before Kenji did, which is the right epistemic behaviour. The falsification test is concretely specified.\n\n**The honest risk is not that capability limits picked the wrong concept. It is that C1 is the concept most congenial to what this team already knows how to do.** Epistemic auditing, structured argument, confidence scoring — these live in the same conceptual space as the retrieval-and-verification methodology the team has been practising for ten sprints. C6 and C5 would have required the team to build tools and knowledge they do not have. That is a legitimate reason to defer them, but it should be named: the team is building what it can build, and the decision should be understood in that context.\n\n**Verdict on the decision:** Sound enough to proceed, with that caveat explicitly acknowledged in Sprint 11's framing.\n\n---\n\n## 3. THE ARGUMENT-MAPPING PRIOR ART GAP\n\nPriya self-flagged, in her Field 3 restatement, that CHI/UIST work on argument visualization interfaces — specifically Rationale and Compendium — was not retrieved. Kenji did not flag it. C1 is now the chosen direction.\n\n**This gap should neither block the close nor block the build. It should be carried as a known risk and addressed early in Sprint 11, before the prototype is called complete.**\n\nMy reasoning: the gap is real and Priya is right to name it. Rationale and Compendium are documented argument-mapping tools with academic provenance. If either implements confidence-weighted visual encoding derived from epistemic category classification — which is the specific mechanism in C1 — the novelty claim is in worse shape than currently stated. That is a material question.\n\nBut the argument-mapping literature is a retrieval gap, not retrieved prior art. There is a difference between "we have not checked" and "we checked and found something that contradicts the claim." At this point we are in the former position. Building a prototype while a prior-art gap is open is not methodologically wrong — you cannot achieve zero prior-art uncertainty before any build. What would be wrong is presenting C1's novelty claim publicly without closing the gap first.\n\n**The backlog correctly includes "acquire access to ACM DL / CHI / UIST / InfoVis literature." The first act of Sprint 11 should be running that targeted search before the prototype is framed externally.** If the search hits argument-mapping tools with confidence-weighted visual encoding, the prototype continues but the novelty claim must be rewritten. If it does not, the claim is more defensible.\n\nThis gap does not change the close verdict. It must be in the Sprint 11 brief.\n\n---\n\n## 4. WHAT THE SPRINT GOT AWAY WITH\n\n**Where was the thinking thinnest?**\n\n**Pass 2's sighted revision was under-documented.** The sprint design required Priya in Pass 2 to attribute each change explicitly to which seed caused it. The synthesis table confirms changes were made, but the mechanism by which Pass 1 and Pass 2 concepts were compared — which concepts were revised, which were added, which were withdrawn, and what the causal chain was — is not visible in the sprint outputs I reviewed. Pass 4 notes that C6 (Diegetic Data Embedding) was described as a sighted addition, but the traceability of that attribution is opaque. The pass produced a result; it did not visibly document the reasoning behind each change. This matters because the entire point of the blind/sighted structure is to measure what seeding adds. If Pass 2's revisions are not explicitly traced, the comparison is uninterpretable.\n\n**The buildability check was applied to falsification tests, not to concepts.** Mateo rated whether the test Priya designed is executable in one sprint. That is useful, but it does not tell the team whether the concept itself — the prototype, the architecture — is buildable in one sprint. For C1, we are assuming buildability of the artifact because the test is buildable. Those are different questions and the conflation was quiet.\n\n**C6's retrieval failure was named but not structurally addressed.** Both rounds failed to reach diegetic display literature. The sprint accepted that and moved on. That is appropriate for closing a sprint, but it means the team now has a direction chosen partly by elimination, and the concept that was most likely eliminated by retrieval failure rather than genuine weakness is C6. That is worth knowing at Sprint 12 when scope is reconsidered.\n\n**The "minimum-spanning-argument scaffold" in C1 was never formally defined.** The concept uses this phrase as though it has a settled meaning, but what constitutes the minimum-confidence-spanning path in an argument map is a non-trivial algorithmic question. This will bite Mateo in Sprint 11. It should have been defined at the concept stage.\n\n**What I expect a Process Review at Sprint 12 to find:**\n\nThe synthesis language in Pass 4 is careful and well-disciplined — Sophie held the "no new claims" constraint visibly. But the comparison table's "Kenji's verdict / category" column will be the thing that travels into Sprint 11 planning, and by the time it does, the caveats in Section 3 of the synthesis will have attenuated. This is the cumulative drift pattern from Sprint Review #2. Pass 4 is a document; the table is a summary of a document; what gets used in planning is a summary of a summary. I expect the Process Review to find that C1's novelty claim was presented in Sprint 11 with less qualification than the underlying retrieval evidence supports, because the mechanism for carrying epistemic hedges across sprint boundaries is still informal.\n\nI also expect it to find that the candidate_approaches table was not updated after Sprint 10. PaperTrail, Epistemic Blinding, PosterMate, Proxona, and the computational register synthesis paper are all new entries that should be in the retrieval record. If they are not there at Sprint 12, that is a maintenance failure.\n\n---\n\n## 5. THE BACKLOG\n\nThe backlog captures the material obligations correctly. I have specific comments on what it gets right and what it misses.\n\n**What it gets right:**\n\nThe high-priority items are correctly ordered. Building C1, acquiring human-subject study capability, and acquiring ACM DL access are the three things Sprint 11 cannot proceed without. The deferred Phase C behavioural validation and the C3/C5 literature gaps are correctly flagged as medium.\n\n**What is missing or underweighted:**\n\n**The minimum-spanning-argument scaffold definition is not in the backlog.** This is a Sprint 11 blocker and it is not listed anywhere. Before Mateo builds, someone needs to specify what the algorithm for computing the minimum-confidence-spanning path actually is. Add it at high priority for Sprint 11.\n\n**The Pass 2 documentation gap is not flagged.** If the team wants to use the blind/sighted comparison as evidence in any future methodological claim, the traceability of Pass 2 changes needs to be recoverable. It is not currently. This is low priority but should be named.\n\n**The C6 retrieval failure is in the backlog as part of "close the unreached-literature gaps on C2 and C5" — but C6 is not mentioned in that item.** C6's gaps are in different venues (game studies, design history, data journalism) than C2's and C5's (NLG planning, CHI/UIST adaptive communication). They need separate targeting. Fix the backlog item to name C6 explicitly.\n\n**The argument-mapping prior-art search (Rationale, Compendium, CHI argument visualization) is not listed as a discrete item.** It is implied by "acquire access to ACM DL / CHI / UIST / InfoVis literature" but that item is too broad to enforce the specific search. Add a discrete item: "Run targeted prior-art search on argument visualization interfaces (Rationale, Compendium, CHI/UIST argumentation tools) before C1 prototype is presented externally." This should be high priority given C1 is the chosen direction.\n\n**The conflation of falsification-test buildability with concept buildability should be added as a methodology note.** Not a high-priority action item, but the distinction should be documented somewhere before Sprint 11's concept work (if any) is designed. Otherwise the same ambiguity will recur.\n\n---\n\n## SUMMARY ASSESSMENT\n\nThe sprint executed a genuinely novel methodology — four-pass blind/sighted concept generation with two-round retrieval verification — and it did so with visible discipline. The pass-input logging, the retrieval log format, the "no new claims" constraint on synthesis, and Priya's self-flagging of the argument-mapping gap are all signs that the team has internalized the review findings from prior sprints.\n\nThe criterion (b) failure is a drafting problem, not an execution problem. The decision is sound enough to proceed given the constraints, though it should be framed in Sprint 11 with honest acknowledgment that capability limits shaped the viable field. The argument-mapping prior-art gap is a known risk to be managed, not a blocker.\n\nThe backlog is mostly right but has three specific gaps I have named above. They do not block the close, but Sprint 11's planning should address them before the brief is locked.\n\n---\n\n**APPROVED — sprint may close.**\n\nConditions on the record for Sprint 11:\n1. The targeted argument-mapping prior-art search (Rationale, Compendium, CHI argumentation tools) runs before any external presentation of C1's novelty claim.\n2. The minimum-spanning-argument scaffold is formally specified before Mateo begins building.\n3. The backlog item on unreached literature is amended to name C6 explicitly alongside C2 and C5.\n4. At Sprint 12 close, I will independently verify the process review count against `list_process_reviews()` and `list_sprints()` rather than accepting Sophie's PROCESS REVIEW STATUS block at face value.	2026-08-20 09:44:09.849444	2026-08-20 09:45:44.957214	review	{"result": "approved", "memory_key": "ingrid/sprint10_close_review"}
90	kenji	team_leader	Sprint 11 gate 1: argument-visualization prior-art search for C1	Ingrid condition 1. Must run before C1's claim is presented externally.	completed	# LITERATURE BRIEF: C1 VERDICT — THE COMMITMENT AUDIT\n**Prepared by:** Kenji Ochiai | **Sprint:** Post-10 | **Status:** Final for Ingrid review\n\n---\n\n## RETRIEVAL LOG (S3)\n\n| Query | Returned | Retained after relevance filter | Noise rate |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| argument visualization confidence weighting epistemic status node visual encoding HCI | 12 | 2 | 83% |\n| Rationale software argument mapping van Gelder empirical evaluation critical thinking | 20 | 4 | 80% |\n| Compendium IBIS dialogue mapping Conklin issue-based information system interface study | 20 | 3 | 85% |\n| Toulmin argumentation scheme computational implementation confidence score claim warrant | 20 | 4 | 80% |\n| argument map node salience opacity size encoding belief strength user study | 20 | 1 | 95% |\n| CHI UIST argumentation interface evidence inference assumption assertion visual representation | 20 | 2 | 90% |\n| epistemic category visual weight argument structure interactive navigation decision support | 20 | 1 | 95% |\n| minimum spanning argument rhetoric computational selection path saliency claim graph | 20 | 1 | 95% |\n\n**Total returned:** 152 | **Total retained:** 18 | **Overall noise rate:** ~88%\n\n**Depth justification:** The noise rate is high but the query set was deliberately broad — six of eight queries targeted either prior-art domains (Rationale, Compendium, Toulmin) or specific encoding mechanisms (confidence-to-visual-weight, opacity, node salience). High noise is expected when fishing for near-misses across domains; what matters is whether any retained paper touches the specific mechanism. Eighteen retained papers across eight queries targeting different facets of the claim is sufficient to issue a conditional verdict on the narrow restated claim, provided the conditionality is stated explicitly. It is not sufficient to close the question against ACM DL.\n\n---\n\n## 1. WHAT THE EVIDENCE SHOWS\n\n### Retained papers organised by relevance to C1's restated claim\n\n**On argument mapping systems (Rationale, Compendium):**\n\n- **"The rationale for Rationale™"** (van Gelder, *openalex*): Describes Rationale as a generic argument mapping software supporting box-and-arrow diagramming of claims and their support relationships. The system encodes argument *structure* (premises, objections, co-premises) visually. No confidence scoring, no epistemic category classification, no visual weight derived from a belief-strength score. The paper discusses *usability and complementation* as explanatory mechanisms, not epistemic status encoding.\n\n- **"Compendium: Making Meetings into Knowledge Events"** (*openalex*): Describes Compendium as a collaborative knowledge-mapping and meeting-facilitation tool built on the IBIS (Issue-Based Information System) framework. Nodes encode dialogue categories (questions, answers, pros, cons). No confidence weighting, no visual weight tied to epistemic status score. The system is navigable and expandable, but node appearance encodes *dialogue role*, not *agent confidence in epistemic category*.\n\n- **"Towards Semantic Knowledge Mapping: An Extension of Compendium with Semantic Web"** (*openalex*): Extends Compendium with RDF/OWL semantic annotation to support intelligent search. Does not introduce confidence scoring or visual weight encoding.\n\n- **"Co-OPR: Design and Evaluation of Collaborative Sensemaking and Planning Tools for Personnel Recovery"** (*openalex*): Integrates Compendium with AI planning. Real-time argument and information mapping. No confidence-to-visual-weight encoding.\n\n**On Toulmin-based computational implementations:**\n\n- **"From ML Predictions to Informed Diagnostic Assistance Using the Toulmin Model of Argumentation"** (*arxiv*): Decomposes ML diagnostic outputs into Toulmin components (claim, grounds, warrant, qualifier, rebuttal, backing). This is the closest retrieved paper to C1's mechanism. The system assigns structured components and uses a *qualifier* to express confidence. **However:** the qualifier is textual and domain-specific (diagnosis confidence), not a continuous 0–1 score mapped to visual weight. There is no interactive visual artifact, no node opacity or size encoding, no reading scaffold. The paper is a diagnostic framework, not a communication artifact or interface.\n\n- **"On Verbalized Confidence Scores for LLMs"** (*arxiv*): Studies whether LLMs can accurately verbalize their own uncertainty as confidence scores. Finds calibration issues. Relevant to whether C1's confidence-scoring step is reliable, not to whether the visual encoding is novel.\n\n- **"Natural language generation of biomedical argumentation for lay audiences"** (*openalex*): Generates structured biomedical arguments with transparent claim-warrant-evidence structure for lay audiences. The architecture makes argument *structure* accessible; it does not encode confidence as a visual dimension or produce a navigable interactive artifact.\n\n**On visual encoding and uncertainty:**\n\n- **"Visualising Uncertainty: A short introduction"** (*openalex*): Short-form overview of uncertainty visualisation methods (error bars, confidence intervals, distributional displays). Concerns statistical uncertainty in data graphics, not epistemic category classification of argument claims. No argument map, no claim-level confidence encoding.\n\n- **"VIANA: Visual Interactive Annotation of Argumentation"** (*arxiv*, retrieved in two queries): A visual analytics system for *annotating* argumentative text — humans label argument spans, and the system suggests the next span to annotate based on ML uncertainty. Node visual weight encodes *annotation uncertainty* (the system's confidence that a span is argumentative), not an agent's confidence in the epistemic category of the claim content. The reader role is *annotator*, not *decision-maker*. No reading scaffold, no minimum-spanning path.\n\n- **"Visualising Argumentation Graphs with Graph Embeddings and t-SNE"** (*arxiv*): Applies t-SNE dimensionality reduction to argumentation graphs for structural analysis. No claim-level confidence, no visual weight encoding, no interactive reading.\n\n**On argument evaluation and quality:**\n\n- **"Investigating Subjective Factors of Argument Strength: Storytelling, Emotions, and Hedging"** (*arxiv*): Quantifies impact of subjective features on argument strength ratings in NLP datasets. Statistical modelling of corpus data; not a visual interface.\n\n- **"Argument Mining: A Survey"** (*openalex*, retrieved twice): Comprehensive survey of computational argumentation. Covers argument component detection, relation classification, stance. No section on confidence-to-visual-weight encoding in interactive argument maps.\n\n- **"The Evaluation of Argument Mapping as a Learning Tool"** (*openalex*): Three-study programme evaluating argument mapping (box-and-arrow) as a learning intervention versus traditional methods. Measures recall, comprehension, critical thinking. Does not describe confidence weighting or visual encoding of epistemic status.\n\n- **"Improving critical thinking using web based argument mapping exercises with automated feedback"** (*openalex*): Web-based argument mapping with automated feedback. Students map arguments; system checks structural correctness. No confidence scoring of claim content, no visual weight encoding.\n\n- **"Explorable Theorems: Making Written Theorems Explorable by Grounding Them in Formal Representations"** (*openalex*): LLMs translate written mathematical proofs into Lean (a proof assistant), then link written proof steps to the formal representation for interactive exploration. **Structurally analogous to C1's scaffold idea** — a default reading path (the written proof) with an underlying formal structure the reader can drill into. However: the domain is mathematical proof verification, not epistemic confidence communication; the encoding is proof-step-to-formal-term, not confidence-to-visual-weight; there is no epistemic category classification. The analogy is architectural (layered readable/inspectable structure) not mechanistic (confidence → visual weight).\n\n**On the minimum-spanning-path concept specifically:**\n\n- No retrieved paper describes a *minimum-confidence-spanning path* as a default reading scaffold for argument presentation. The graph-theoretic query (Query 8) returned no relevant results — the mathematics of minimum spanning trees appeared, not rhetorically motivated argument path selection.\n\n---\n\n## 2. THE DIRECT QUESTION\n\n**Does any retrieved system derive visual weight from a confidence score attached to an epistemic category classification?**\n\n**No.**\n\nThe specific combination — (a) classify a claim into an epistemic category (evidence / inference / assumption / assertion), (b) assign a continuous confidence score to that classification, (c) derive node visual weight (size, opacity, border weight) from that confidence score — does not appear in any retrieved paper.\n\nThe closest approaches, and why they do not reach it:\n\n| System | What it does | What it does not do |\n|---|---|---|\n| Rationale (van Gelder) | Visual argument mapping, structural roles encoded | No confidence score, no visual weight from confidence |\n| Compendium / IBIS | Dialogue-role node encoding, navigable map | No confidence score, no epistemic category classification |\n| Toulmin + ML diagnostics (arxiv) | Decomposes ML output into Toulmin components including qualifier | No continuous confidence score mapped to visual weight; no interactive artifact |\n| VIANA | Visual weight encodes annotation uncertainty | Annotation uncertainty ≠ agent's claim-level epistemic confidence; no reading scaffold |\n| Explorable Theorems | Layered readable/inspectable structure, default path + expandable formalism | Domain is proof correctness, not confidence communication; no visual weight encoding |\n\n---\n\n## 3. DOES ANYTHING RETRIEVED THREATEN THE MINIMUM-SPANNING-ARGUMENT SCAFFOLD AS A DISTINCT CONTRIBUTION?\n\n**WHAT THE EVIDENCE SHOWS:**\n\nExplorable Theorems (*openalex*) is the only retrieved paper with an architectural resemblance: it provides a default reading path (the written proof) and an inspectable underlying representation (Lean formalisation) that the reader can navigate into. The paper explicitly describes this as enabling "interactive affordances beyond what static text supports."\n\nNo retrieved paper describes a *rhetorically motivated* default path through an argument graph, selected by a confidence-minimisation criterion. The Argument Web (*openalex*) describes interoperable tools for argument navigation and analysis but does not describe path selection by confidence threshold.\n\n**WHAT THIS MIGHT IMPLY:**\n\nThe minimum-spanning-argument scaffold — selecting the path through the argument that establishes the main claim at highest confidence with fewest nodes — is architecturally distinct from Explorable Theorems (which uses the author's narrative sequencing as the default path, not an algorithmic confidence-based selection). The Explorable Theorems precedent is worth acknowledging in C1's public description as a structural analogue in a different domain, but it does not displace the scaffold as a contribution.\n\nThe qualification I must apply: argument summarisation research in NLP (not well-reached by these queries) has produced systems that select salient argument components from larger argument graphs. Whether any such system uses confidence-based selection as a reading scaffold for human decision-makers is unknown from retrieved evidence.\n\n---\n\n## 4. REVISED VERDICT ON C1'S RESTATED CLAIM\n\n**The restated claim survives, but must carry an explicit retrieval boundary.**\n\n**WHAT THE EVIDENCE SHOWS:**\n\nThe restated claim is: *continuous confidence scoring across four epistemic categories, where visual weight is derived from that confidence score rather than from rhetorical emphasis or narrative position, and where the minimum-confidence-spanning path constitutes the default reading scaffold, is not described in the retrieved literature.*\n\nThat statement is accurate against retrieved evidence. No retrieved paper reaches this specific combination.\n\n**WHAT THIS MIGHT IMPLY — AND WHERE I CANNOT GO:**\n\nThe honest boundary of this verdict is as follows. The retrieval did not reach:\n\n1. **ACM Digital Library** (CHI, UIST, CSCW proceedings directly) — the CHI/UIST query returned high-noise results from openalex and arxiv, not from ACM DL directly. Papers like Kirsh's work on epistemic actions, Horvitz's work on mixed-initiative interfaces, or direct-search results for "argument map confidence" in CHI proceedings are not represented in what was retrieved.\n\n2. **Information visualisation literature on uncertainty-aware visual encodings** — MacEachren, Skeels, Hullman, Fernandes and colleagues have published on encoding uncertainty as visual variables (opacity, saturation, blur) in data visualisation contexts. Whether any such work has been applied to argument node encoding specifically is unknown from retrieved evidence.\n\n3. **Argument web / OVA / AIFdb ecosystem** — the Argument Web paper was retrieved but not the full suite of tools built on the AIF ontology, which includes confidence-annotated argument corpora.\n\n4. **Structured argumentation in AI / explainable AI** — SHAP, LIME, and XAI approaches sometimes present explanations as claim-support structures with confidence values. Whether any XAI paper presents this as a navigable confidence-weighted visual map is not resolved.\n\n**Verdict stated precisely:** C1's restated claim is *not contradicted* by retrieved evidence. It is *unconfirmed* against ACM-indexed CHI/UIST literature, uncertainty-aware visualisation literature, and the XAI visual explanation literature. A verdict that the claim is safe would overstate what the retrieval supports. The correct statement is: **the claim survives retrieved evidence and is plausible, but cannot be confirmed as safe against the specific literatures named above without further retrieval.**\n\nIf Sophie or Ingrid need a confirmed verdict before ACM DL and the uncertainty-vis literature are reached, the right response is to commission a targeted follow-on search, not to treat this brief as a clearance.\n\n---\n\n## 5. WHAT C1'S PUBLIC DESCRIPTION MUST ACKNOWLEDGE\n\nBased on retrieved evidence, C1's public description must state the following explicitly:\n\n**Required acknowledgements:**\n\n1. **Argument mapping as an established field.** Rationale, Compendium/IBIS, and the broader argument mapping literature demonstrate that visual representation of argument structure — including navigable, node-link maps with role-encoded nodes — is an established research and practice area. C1 is not the first to make argument structure visual. This must be stated directly, not left implicit.\n\n2. **Epistemic category annotation as prior work.** Argumentation mining (the survey literature) and Toulmin-based computational systems demonstrate that classifying argumentative components into typed categories (claim, evidence, warrant, qualifier) is existing NLP and AI work. C1's four-category scheme (evidence / inference / assumption / assertion) is a specific formulation, but typed argumentative classification is not novel.\n\n3. **Explorable Theorems as a structural analogue.** The layered readable/inspectable architecture — a default path with an underlying formalism the reader can navigate into — has been implemented in the proof-comprehension domain. C1's scaffold is distinct (confidence-based path selection vs. narrative sequencing; argument confidence vs. proof correctness) but the architectural pattern is not unclaimed.\n\n4. **The retrieval boundary.** C1's public description should acknowledge that ACM CHI/UIST proceedings, uncertainty-aware visualisation literature, and XAI visual explanation literature were not fully reached, and that the novelty claim is contingent on those literatures not containing the specific encoding. This is a standing caveat, not a formality.\n\n**What C1 may continue to claim:**\n\nThe specific mechanism — deriving node visual weight from a continuous confidence score attached to an epistemic category classification, combined with a minimum-confidence-spanning path as the default reading scaffold — is not described in retrieved literature. C1 may claim this specific combination as its contribution, subject to the retrieval boundary stated above.\n\n---\n\n*Filed: Kenji Ochiai. No recollection used as evidence. All claims grounded in retrieved papers named above or flagged as unverified gaps.*	2026-08-20 10:09:52.03497	2026-08-20 10:12:22.746566	prior_art_verification	{"memory_key": "kenji/c1_argument_visualization_check", "queries_run": 8, "results_retrieved": 152}
91	mateo	team_leader	Sprint 11 gate 2: specify the minimum-spanning-argument scaffold	Ingrid condition 2, blocking. Must be settled before the C1 build starts.	completed	# SPECIFICATION: MINIMUM-SPANNING-ARGUMENT — DEFINED BEFORE BUILD\n\n*Mateo Fittipaldi, Altair8 Dev*\n\n---\n\n## 1. THE DATA STRUCTURE\n\nThe argument map is a **directed acyclic graph (DAG)**.\n\n**Nodes.** Each node is a claim with:\n- `id`: unique string\n- `text`: the claim as a natural-language string\n- `category`: one of `{evidence, inference, assumption, assertion}`\n- `confidence`: float in `[0.0, 1.0]`\n- `is_root`: boolean, exactly one node is true — this is the main claim\n\n**Edges.** A directed edge `(A → B)` means "B depends on A" — A is a reason, support, or premise for B. Edges carry no weight of their own; weight lives on nodes.\n\n**Structural guarantees I am imposing, because the concept requires them for the algorithm to be well-defined:**\n\n1. **No cycles.** The structure is a DAG. A claim cannot depend on itself through any chain. If the agent produces a cycle, it is a malformed audit; the system rejects it before rendering.\n2. **Single root.** Exactly one node has `is_root = true`. This is the main claim. All algorithmic selection terminates here.\n3. **Every node is reachable from at least one leaf, and every node reaches the root.** The graph is connected in the direction of the root. There are no floating nodes. A node with no path to the root is invalid and discarded with a logged warning. The concept says "every supporting element is explicitly tagged" — I am reading this as: anything in the map must be doing epistemic work toward the main claim, or it should not be in the map at all.\n4. **Multiple parents allowed.** A single claim can support more than one other claim. This is how shared premises work. The graph is not required to be a tree.\n5. **No disconnected components.** Follows from guarantee 3.\n\n**What this means for the visual layer:** nodes are rendered as a DAG, not a tree. If a node has multiple parents it appears once with multiple edges, not duplicated. This matters for the algorithm.\n\n---\n\n## 2. THE ALGORITHM\n\n### First: the borrowed name is wrong\n\n"Minimum spanning argument" imports the phrase from *minimum spanning tree* in graph theory. MST is defined over edge weights and finds the tree that connects all nodes at minimum total edge cost. That is not what this concept wants. The concept wants:\n\n> *The fewest nodes that form a valid supporting path from leaves to the root, where the path's weakest node is as strong as possible.*\n\nThis is closer to a **shortest-path-with-node-costs** problem, or more precisely a **minimum-node subgraph selection with a bottleneck-confidence objective**. "Minimum spanning" is the wrong label. It will cause a future implementer to reach for Kruskal's or Prim's algorithm and produce something subtly incorrect.\n\n**I am renaming it: the Primary Reading Path (PRP).**\n\nThis is the name I will use in code and in comments. If the designer wants a different name, I need that before build — but I will not preserve "minimum-spanning-argument" as a term in any runnable artifact.\n\n---\n\n### The Algorithm, Defined\n\n**Name:** Primary Reading Path (PRP) selection\n\n**Inputs:**\n- The validated DAG described in Section 1\n- A confidence threshold `τ` ∈ `[0.0, 1.0]`, default `0.5`. This is the parameter the control mechanism adjusts when the reader says "make the argument shorter" or "I don't trust the middle claim."\n\n**Output:**\nA subset `P` of nodes (plus their connecting edges) such that:\n1. The root node is in `P`\n2. Every node in `P` except leaves has at least one child node also in `P` (the path is connected down to at least one leaf)\n3. Every node in `P` has `confidence ≥ τ`\n4. Subject to the above, `|P|` is minimized\n5. Among all sets of equal minimum size, the one with the highest minimum-confidence node is preferred (the bottleneck criterion — maximize the weakest link)\n\n**The selection rule, concretely:**\n\nThis is a modified shortest-path / minimum-node-path problem on the DAG, solved with a backward pass from the root.\n\n```\nfunction computePRP(dag, τ):\n\n  // Step 1: Prune any node below threshold\n  eligible = { n ∈ dag.nodes | n.confidence ≥ τ }\n  \n  // Step 2: Check root is eligible\n  if root ∉ eligible: return EMPTY (surface to reader: no path above threshold)\n  \n  // Step 3: For each eligible node, determine if it can anchor a valid sub-path\n  // A leaf node is always a valid anchor (it has no dependencies to satisfy)\n  // A non-leaf node is valid if at least one of its children is eligible and valid\n  // Compute this bottom-up:\n  \n  valid = {}\n  for n in topological_sort(dag, direction=leaves_first):\n    if n ∉ eligible: continue\n    if n is a leaf: valid.add(n)\n    else if any(child ∈ valid for child in n.children): valid.add(n)\n  \n  if root ∉ valid: return EMPTY\n  \n  // Step 4: From root, greedily select the path to a leaf using fewest nodes,\n  // breaking ties by maximum bottleneck confidence\n  // This is a DAG shortest-path where node cost = 1 and we maximize\n  // the minimum confidence along the path\n  \n  // Use dynamic programming:\n  // For each valid node n, compute:\n  //   best[n] = (min_path_length_to_any_leaf, max_bottleneck_confidence_on_that_path)\n  \n  for n in topological_sort(dag, direction=leaves_first):\n    if n ∉ valid: continue\n    if n is leaf:\n      best[n] = (1, n.confidence)\n    else:\n      valid_children = [c for c in n.children if c ∈ valid]\n      // Pick child that gives shortest path; break ties by highest bottleneck\n      best_child = argmin over valid_children of (best[c].length, -best[c].bottleneck)\n      best[n] = (best[best_child].length + 1, min(n.confidence, best[best_child].bottleneck))\n  \n  // Step 5: Trace back the selected path\n  PRP = [root]\n  current = root\n  while current is not a leaf:\n    next = argmin over valid_children(current) of (best[c].length, -best[c].bottleneck)\n    PRP.append(next)\n    current = next\n  \n  return PRP\n```\n\n**Complexity:** O(V + E) with the topological sort and single DP pass. This is fast. The graph in any realistic C1 use case is small — probably 5–30 nodes. Complexity is not a concern.\n\n**What this produces:** the shortest chain from root to a leaf, above confidence threshold, with the strongest weakest-link. That is what "fewest nodes that establish the main claim at highest confidence" actually means, operationalized.\n\n---\n\n## 3. EDGE CASES\n\n**Case 1: No claim above threshold**\n\nThreshold `τ` is set and no eligible node set can connect root to a leaf.\n\n*Proposed behaviour:* Do not render a PRP. Display the root node alone, visually marked as "below confidence threshold." Show a banner: *"No path above confidence threshold τ. Showing full map. Lower the threshold to see a reading path."* Do not silently render the full map as if the PRP succeeded — that hides the failure. The concept's whole point is making epistemic weakness visible; a failed PRP is itself information.\n\n**Case 2: Several equally minimal paths**\n\nThe DP returns the same `(length, bottleneck)` for multiple paths.\n\n*Proposed behaviour:* Pick the path whose non-bottleneck nodes have the highest average confidence. If still tied: pick deterministically by node ID (alphabetical) to ensure the rendered artifact is stable across re-renders. Do not randomise — an artifact that redraws differently on reload would destroy reader trust. Flag the tie in the audit log so the designer can see when the map is ambiguous at the structural level.\n\n**Case 3: A low-confidence claim that is structurally unavoidable**\n\nThere exists a node `n` with `confidence < τ` such that every path from root to any leaf passes through `n`. Removing `n` disconnects the graph.\n\n*Proposed behaviour:* The PRP algorithm returns EMPTY (because `n` is not in `eligible`). Do not silently include `n` to patch the path — that would undermine the threshold mechanism and is exactly the kind of silent semantic invention Ingrid flagged. Surface it explicitly: *"One claim below threshold is required for any reading path. Raise the threshold or review claim [n.text]."* Highlight `n` in the full map view. This is the most important failure mode to surface loudly: it is the visual equivalent of a load-bearing confession.\n\n**Case 4: An assumption that everything depends on**\n\nA node with `category = assumption` sits at or near the root of the dependency chain — many claims depend on it, including claims in the PRP.\n\n*Proposed behaviour:* The algorithm does not treat assumptions differently from other node types for path selection. Confidence is the only selection criterion. However, if an assumption is in the PRP, it gets a distinct visual treatment (the category colour and shape encoding the concept already specifies). A reader will see, on the primary reading path, a node visually marked as an assumption. This is the concept working correctly — it is surfacing that the main argument rests on an assumption. No special algorithmic handling needed; the visual encoding carries the signal. What I will *not* do is automatically exclude assumptions from the PRP — that would be an undocumented policy decision that the concept does not authorise.\n\n**Case 5: Main claim rests only on assertions**\n\nEvery leaf node reachable from the root has `category = assertion`. The PRP selects the shortest path, which terminates at an assertion.\n\n*Proposed behaviour:* The algorithm runs normally. The PRP is valid. The visual output will show a path ending in a node with assertion styling — the reader can see this. Do not suppress or warn. The concept is designed precisely to make this visible without the author present. A sceptical reader who sees "this argument bottoms out in assertions" has learned something true. Adding a system warning ("this argument is all assertions") risks becoming paternalistic noise; the visual encoding is the warning. I will note this in code comments so a future reviewer does not add a warning layer that double-signals.\n\n---\n\n## 4. WHAT THE CONCEPT LEFT UNDERDETERMINED\n\nThis is the critical section. Every item here is a decision I had to make that the concept did not make. Any of these, made differently, produces a meaningfully different artifact.\n\n---\n\n**4.1 What "confidence" is and who assigns it**\n\n*The concept says:* "the agent assigns a confidence score (0–1)."\n\n*What it does not say:* whether this is a calibrated probability, a self-assessed ordinal scale, a model logit, or a human-facing rating like "I'm pretty sure." These are not interchangeable. A logit turned into a 0–1 float via sigmoid does not mean the same thing as a domain expert's 0.7. The PRP algorithm is sensitive to this — a 0.71 beats a 0.69 — but if the scores are not on a common calibrated scale, the comparison is meaningless.\n\n*What I chose:* I am treating confidence as an **author-assigned ordinal approximation**, documented in the audit output. I will not pretend it is calibrated probability. In the prototype, I will expose the raw values but add a tooltip: "confidence is the agent's self-assessment, not a calibrated probability." This keeps the visual honest. A future sprint can revisit calibration.\n\n*What a different choice produces:* If you treat confidence as calibrated probability, you can do principled uncertainty propagation up the dependency chain (a claim inferred from two 0.7 nodes should not itself be 0.9). That is a real feature. It is also a different, more complex system. I am not building it now.\n\n---\n\n**4.2 How confidence propagates (or doesn't) up the graph**\n\n*The concept says:* each claim has a confidence score. It does not say whether the parent's confidence is constrained by or derived from its children's confidence.\n\n*What it does not say:* if a claim is an inference from two premises with confidence 0.4 and 0.5, can the inference itself have confidence 0.9? The concept appears to allow this — the agent assigns confidence per claim. But it creates a situation where the PRP selects a high-confidence inference resting on low-confidence premises, and the collapsed view looks strong while the expanded view reveals weakness. That might be the feature (the reader who expands discovers the weakness), or it might be a bug (the PRP is misleading).\n\n*What I chose:* **No automatic propagation.** The agent assigns confidence per node, period. The algorithm uses those values as given. I will add a validation warning (logged, not shown to reader) if a node's confidence exceeds the maximum confidence of its children by more than 0.3 — this flags suspicious cases for the agent/author to review, without enforcing a rule the concept didn't authorise.\n\n*What a different choice produces:* enforced propagation (parent confidence ≤ min(children confidence), for example) would make the map internally consistent but would override the agent's stated confidence, which feels like the build team overruling the concept. It also makes the "control mechanism" section harder to implement — you can no longer adjust a node's confidence independently.\n\n---\n\n**4.3 What "fewest nodes" means when the graph has shared nodes**\n\n*The concept says:* minimum number of nodes. But the graph can have nodes with multiple parents (I established this in Section 1). If node `X` supports both node `A` and node `B`, and both `A` and `B` are in the PRP, does `X` count as one node or two?\n\n*What I chose:* **One node, always.** A node appears once in the PRP regardless of how many PRP nodes it supports. The "fewest nodes" count is the count of distinct nodes in the subgraph, not the count of edges or path segments. This is the natural reading of "fewest nodes."\n\n*What a different choice produces:* if you count edges rather than nodes, shorter-edge paths look different from shorter-node paths when shared nodes are involved. This is a real difference in which path gets selected. I have made the choice that is consistent with the concept's stated goal ("fewest nodes that establish the main claim") but it needed to be made explicitly.\n\n---\n\n**4.4 The threshold τ — who sets it, what its default is, and how it maps to user feedback**\n\n*The concept says:* the control mechanism re-parses human feedback into "modifications to the confidence threshold for what appears in the minimum-spanning path."\n\n*What it does not say:* what the default threshold is, whether it is global or per-session, what "make the argument shorter" maps to numerically, or whether there is a floor below which threshold cannot be set.\n\n*What I chose:*\n- Default `τ = 0.5`\n- "Make it shorter" → `τ += 0.1` (raises threshold, fewer nodes qualify)\n- "I don't trust the middle claim" → lower threshold of that specific claim's branch, not global (this requires a per-branch threshold, which is a complication — see 4.5)\n- Floor: `τ ≥ 0.1` (cannot set threshold so low that every node qualifies regardless of confidence)\n\n*What a different choice produces:* A global threshold is simpler but cannot handle "I don't trust claim X specifically" without lowering the bar for everything. A per-node override is more expressive but more complex. I have flagged this as a complication below.\n\n---\n\n**4.5 "I don't trust the middle claim" — global threshold or local override?**\n\n*The concept says:* this feedback re-parses into a threshold modification. It does not say whether the threshold is global or can be applied locally to a subtree.\n\n*The problem:* if a reader says "I don't trust claim X," the meaningful response is to lower the threshold in X's dependency subtree (forcing more supporting nodes to appear) while leaving the rest of the PRP alone. But the algorithm as defined uses a single global `τ`. A global threshold change would either suppress more of the map (if raised) or reveal more of it everywhere (if lowered) — neither is precisely what the reader asked for.\n\n*What I chose for the prototype:* **Global threshold only, with a note.** "I don't trust claim X" lowers `τ` globally by 0.1, which has the effect of revealing more of the map. It is not precise but it is honest — the reader sees more, not less. I will flag this in the UI as a known limitation. The per-subtree threshold override is the right long-term solution but requires the algorithm to be re-run with a modified graph, which I can implement in sprint 2.\n\n*What a different choice produces:* a per-subtree override requires identifying "claim X's dependency subgraph," re-running PRP on that subgraph with a lower threshold, and merging the result with the existing PRP. Doable, but not in the same sprint as the core prototype.\n\n---\n\n**4.6 Visual weight encoding — continuous or stepped?**\n\n*The concept says:* "size, opacity, and border weight" map to confidence. It does not say whether this is a continuous function (confidence 0.73 → size 73% of max) or a stepped scale (high/medium/low bands).\n\n*What I chose:* **Stepped, three bands.** High (≥0.7): full size, full opacity, thick border. Medium (0.4–0.69): 80% size, 0.75 opacity, medium border. Low (<0.4): 65% size, 0.5 opacity, thin border. Continuous encoding sounds richer but is harder to read — the difference between a 0.71 and a 0.73 node is not perceivable and implies false precision. Stepped encoding signals the right thing: rough epistemic tier, not false exactness. This also sidesteps the calibration problem in 4.1.\n\n*What a different choice produces:* continuous encoding works if confidence scores are calibrated. If they are ordinal approximations (my assumption from 4.1), continuous encoding is dishonest — it implies resolution that does not exist.\n\n---\n\n**4.7 What "collapsed" means for a non-PRP node**\n\n*The concept says:* all nodes not in the PRP "exist but are collapsed." It does not define what a collapsed node looks like visually.\n\n*What I chose:* collapsed nodes are **visible but minimal** — a small shape (using the category colour/shape encoding) at reduced opacity, with a `+` affordance indicating expandability. They are not hidden. A reader who is not interacting still perceives that there is more structure. Hiding them entirely would violate the concept's intent (the reader can see there are other claims); showing them at full weight would defeat the PRP as a reading scaffold.\n\n*What a different choice produces:* hiding non-PRP nodes entirely makes the PRP cleaner but hides structural information the concept explicitly says should be available. Showing all nodes at full weight eliminates the PRP as a useful concept.\n\n---\n\n## 5. COMPLEXITY AND BUILD COST\n\n**The algorithm is not the expensive part.** The PRP selection is O(V + E), the graph is small in any realistic case, and I can implement it in a day including edge case handling.\n\n**The expensive parts are:**\n\n1. **The agent-side audit format.** The prototype needs to consume a structured JSON audit. I can hardcode a sample audit for the prototype (one sprint is fine for this). The real question — how a language model reliably produces a valid DAG audit, with no cycles, with calibrated-enough confidence scores, with accurate dependency tracing — is a research question, not a build question. The prototype will assume well-formed input. If the designer intends the agent to generate the audit autonomously, that is a separate workstream.\n\n2. **The filter UI ("show me only assertions").** The concept specifies this, it is achievable, and it is a visual suppression pass over the already-rendered graph. Buildable in sprint 1 as a basic toggle set.\n\n3. **The control mechanism / feedback parsing.** "Make the argument shorter" → `τ += 0.1` is a fixed mapping I am proposing. If the designer wants natural-language parsing of feedback into threshold adjustments, that requires an LLM call in the render loop, which is a separate integration. I am not assuming it for the prototype.\n\n**Sprint 1 scope is honest:** Core DAG data structure + PRP algorithm + HTML/CSS/JS render of one hardcoded example with expand/collapse + category filter. That is a sprint. The control mechanism is sprint 2. The live-agent-audit pipeline is beyond both.\n\n---\n\n## 6. WHAT I NEED FROM THE DESIGNER BEFORE BUILDING\n\nThese are not aesthetic preferences. They are questions where a wrong assumption produces a different system.\n\n**Q1 — Confidence score semantics.** Are the confidence scores the agent produces intended to be calibrated probabilities, or honest self-assessments on an ordinal scale? If calibrated: should confidence propagate up the dependency chain (a high-confidence inference from low-confidence premises should be flagged)? If ordinal: I proceed as in Section 4.1. I cannot make this choice for you — it changes what the visual encoding means.\n\n**Q2 — "I don't trust the middle claim" mechanism.** Is this a global threshold adjustment or a per-subtree adjustment? The concept implies per-claim interrogation but the control mechanism section describes threshold modification globally. These are in tension. I will build global for sprint 1 either way, but I need to know if per-subtree is the intended end state so I architect the data layer to support it.\n\n**Q3 — Who generates the audit?** The prototype will consume a hardcoded JSON audit. For the real system: does the designer intend the language model to generate the audit in a single pass (producing structured JSON), or is there a separate audit stage before rendering? This is the failure mode the concept was written to address — stage separation causing confabulation to pass structural checks. I want to know if the intended architecture re-introduces a stage boundary, because if it does, someone needs to specify what gets passed across that boundary and how it is validated.\n\n**Q4 — The "primary reading path" as the claimed main innovation.** If a reader expands every collapsed node (the secondary falsifier in Section 6 of the concept), the concept says "the collapsing mechanism is not working." But the PRP I have defined is the best path given the structure. If readers always want more, that might mean the PRP is too sparse, or it might mean the map needs a better default depth rather than a better path selection algorithm. Before I build, I want to know: is the PRP the *only* default view the designer considers acceptable, or is a depth-2 default (root + all immediate dependencies) a legitimate fallback if the PRP proves too sparse in testing?\n\n**Q5 — Handling cycles in agent output.** I am imposing DAG-only as a hard constraint and rejecting cyclic audits. If the designer anticipates that real agent-generated audits will sometimes contain apparent cycles (e.g., mutual support between two claims), I need a resolution policy before build — not after. Options: reject the audit and ask the agent to revise; break the cycle by dropping the lower-confidence edge; collapse the cycle into a single cluster node. Each produces different semantics.\n\n---\n\n*That is the full specification. The algorithm is defined, the edge cases have stated behaviours, and the underdetermined decisions are named. Build can start on the parts that are settled. The five questions above are the parts that cannot be settled without the designer.*	2026-08-20 10:12:25.627869	2026-08-20 10:14:41.221916	algorithm_spec	{"memory_key": "mateo/c1_spanning_scaffold_spec"}
92	priya	team_leader	Sprint 11: answer the five questions blocking the C1 build	Intent decisions Mateo cannot make. Build is blocked until these land.	completed	# PRIYA'S RESPONSE TO MATEO'S SPECIFICATION\n\n---\n\n## PRELIMINARY: THE RENAME\n\nAdopt it. "Minimum spanning argument" was importing a graph-theoretic guarantee the algorithm does not provide, and Mateo is right that a future implementer would reach for Kruskal's or Prim's and produce something subtly wrong. "Primary reading path" describes what the thing actually is — the default sequence a reader follows — without making claims about the mathematics. I will use PRP from here.\n\n---\n\n## PART A: Q1 THROUGH Q5\n\n---\n\n### Q1 — Confidence score semantics\n\n**Decision:** Ordinal self-assessment, not calibrated probability. Confidence scores in the prototype are the agent's honest approximation of its own certainty, documented as such, and should not be treated as interchangeable floats on a common scale.\n\n**Reasoning:** The choice here is not primarily a build decision — it is a claim about what the agent can actually produce reliably. Calibrated probability requires either a formal Bayesian account of the agent's belief state or empirical recalibration against ground truth. Neither is available in sprint 1, and Kenji's retrieval turned up the verbalized-confidence paper (Ochiai brief, retained paper 2 in the Toulmin cluster) showing that LLMs have demonstrable calibration problems when asked to verbalize uncertainty as scores. If I commit to calibrated probability semantics, I am making a claim the system cannot currently honour, and the visual encoding becomes dishonest in exactly the way the concept was designed to prevent: a 0.73 rendered differently from a 0.69 implies a resolution that does not exist in the underlying score.\n\nWhat ordinal self-assessment commits the build to: Mateo's stepped visual encoding (which I will address in Part B) becomes the only honest rendering choice. The tooltip he proposed — "confidence is the agent's self-assessment, not a calibrated probability" — should be in the sprint 1 build, not deferred. And the concept's falsification study (section 6 of C1) will need to treat confidence tiers, not point values, as the unit of comparison between the audit group and the document group.\n\nOn propagation: if confidence is ordinal, automated propagation up the dependency chain is not meaningful. You cannot compute a principled bound on an inference's confidence from the ordinal approximations of its premises. Mateo's choice — no automatic propagation, with a logged warning when a node's confidence exceeds its children's maximum by more than 0.3 — is the right call for this reason, not merely for implementation convenience. I endorse it in Part B below.\n\n**What this commits the build to:** stepped encoding only; tooltip on all confidence values in sprint 1; confidence tiers (not point values) as the unit of analysis in any user study.\n\n---\n\n### Q2 — "I don't trust the middle claim" — global or per-subtree threshold\n\n**Decision:** Per-subtree is the intended end state. Build global for sprint 1, but architect the data layer now so that per-subtree is not a rewrite.\n\n**Reasoning:** The concept's control mechanism section describes threshold modification in response to targeted doubt about a specific claim. "I don't trust claim X" is not a claim about the whole argument's confidence distribution — it is a claim about one node and its dependency subgraph. A global threshold response to that input is imprecise enough to be misleading: lowering τ globally reveals more of the map everywhere, which is not what the reader asked for and may introduce distracting material in parts of the argument the reader was not questioning.\n\nThe concept's core purpose is to let a sceptical reader pull on a specific thread. A global threshold mechanism does not honour that purpose — it gives the reader more rope but not the specific thread they identified. Per-subtree is therefore not a nice-to-have; it is what the concept's interrogation promise actually requires.\n\nThat said, I understand why Mateo is building global first. The per-subtree version requires identifying the dependency subgraph of node X, running PRP on that subgraph with a modified threshold, and merging the result — a meaningful sprint 2 commitment. Global for sprint 1 is acceptable provided: (a) the UI labels it explicitly as "showing more of the map globally" rather than implying precision it does not have, and (b) the data layer stores node identities and subgraph structure in a way that makes the per-subtree computation in sprint 2 additive rather than architectural.\n\n**What this commits the build to:** in sprint 1, global threshold adjustment with honest UI labelling; a data structure that indexes the dependency subgraph of each node (even if that index is not used until sprint 2). Mateo should not flatten the subgraph relationships to save sprint 1 time.\n\n---\n\n### Q3 — Who generates the audit, and does the architecture re-introduce a stage boundary\n\n**Decision:** The audit must be generated in the same pass as the content it audits. Any architecture that separates audit generation from content generation re-introduces the stage boundary and undermines the concept's foundational purpose.\n\n**Reasoning, and a plainly stated concern:**\n\nThe concept was written in direct response to failures 4 and 5: confabulated content passing structural checks because the pipeline had no mechanism for tracking content provenance across stages. The failure is a stage-separation failure. The concept's fix is to make provenance structurally load-bearing in the rendering — the audit is not a check applied to content after the fact, it is the generative input to the visual form.\n\nIf the agent generates the argument content in one pass and then generates the audit in a second pass — reviewing what it just said — the concept has recreated exactly the structure it was designed to prevent. A second-pass audit is a structural check applied after content generation. The agent can, and given documented confabulation patterns, sometimes will, produce an audit that coherently describes an argument it did not actually make, or that assigns epistemic categories that flatter the argument rather than accurately classify it. The audit would pass structural validation (it is a valid DAG, categories are one of four, confidence scores are in range) while being semantically decoupled from the content. This is failure mode 4 with an extra stage.\n\nThe architecture that does not recreate the boundary: the agent produces the structured epistemic audit and the content claims simultaneously, as a single structured output. Each claim is generated with its category and confidence score at the time of generation — not reviewed afterwards. The JSON schema enforces this: a claim node cannot be added without its category and confidence score; there is no "content first, annotate later" path in the output format.\n\nWhat this means practically for sprint 1: Mateo's hardcoded sample audit is fine for the prototype, because the prototype is not testing whether the agent generates the audit correctly — it is testing whether the rendering and interaction model work. The research question — can a language model reliably produce a well-formed DAG audit in a single structured pass, with no cycles, with accurate dependency tracing, with honest confidence scores — is not answered by the prototype and should not be assumed to be answered by it. It is the most important open question for the system's validity, and it is one I am flagging as a hypothesis that needs its own test, separate from the rendering test.\n\nWhat I am genuinely uncertain about: whether "single-pass structured output" is achievable with current language models at the quality the concept requires. Models have documented difficulty producing constrained structured output (valid JSON, no cycles in a DAG) reliably, especially as the argument graph grows. If this proves unreliable, the fallback — a separate audit stage with a validation step that checks consistency between audit and content — re-introduces a stage boundary, but a monitored one. I do not know which failure mode is worse: a single-pass that occasionally produces malformed or dishonest audits (invisible failure) or a two-pass that is structurally vulnerable to stage-separation confabulation (named failure). I am naming this gap rather than deciding it, because it is a research question that the prototype cannot settle and the founder should be aware of before committing to the architecture.\n\n**What this commits the build to:** the JSON schema for the prototype should enforce simultaneous generation semantics — no content node without a category and confidence score. The hardcoded sample audit should model what a single-pass agent output looks like (not what a post-hoc audit would look like). The pipeline architecture decision — single-pass vs. monitored two-pass — should be surfaced to the founder before the live-agent integration sprint, not decided by default.\n\n---\n\n### Q4 — Is the PRP the only acceptable default view, or is depth-2 a legitimate fallback\n\n**Decision:** Depth-2 is a legitimate fallback if the PRP proves too sparse, but it is not a default alternative — it is a contingent response to a specific failure mode that the falsification test should detect.\n\n**Reasoning:** The PRP is the concept's primary claim about what the right default view is — the shortest argument that establishes the main claim at highest confidence. A depth-2 default (root plus all immediate dependencies) is a structurally different claim: it says the right default is determined by graph topology (proximity to root), not by confidence-optimised path selection. These are not equivalent, and I should not blur them before testing.\n\nThe PRP may prove too sparse. If readers in the falsification study systematically expand to read the full map (the secondary falsifier in C1's section 6), that is evidence that the PRP is not giving readers enough context — either because the path is genuinely too thin, or because the collapsing interaction model is not working as a scaffold. If that result comes in, depth-2 is one candidate redesign. But it should be a response to a tested failure, not a pre-emptive hedge.\n\nWhat I will not do is design around the possibility of sparsity by defaulting to depth-2 preemptively. That would be capitulating to implementation comfort before we know there is a problem. The PRP is the right default to test. If it fails the secondary falsifier, depth-2 goes on the redesign list alongside other candidates.\n\n**What this commits the build to:** Sprint 1 builds PRP as the only default. The interaction logging should be built from the start to capture expansion behaviour (which nodes, in what order, how often) — this data is what the secondary falsifier needs, and it costs little to instrument now.\n\n---\n\n### Q5 — Handling cycles in agent output\n\n**Decision:** Reject and request revision. Do not silently repair cycles.\n\n**Reasoning:** A cycle in the audit — claim A depends on claim B depends on claim A — is not a well-formed epistemic account. It is either a reasoning error or a sign that the agent's self-model of its argument structure is incoherent. Silently breaking the cycle by dropping the lower-confidence edge or collapsing the cycle into a cluster node patches the output without surfacing the underlying problem. Both approaches are exactly the kind of silent semantic invention that Ingrid flagged as a failure mode: the system appears to handle the input correctly while hiding the fact that the input was malformed.\n\nThe concept's premise is that the agent holds an explicit and honest account of its own argument structure. A cycle falsifies that premise. The right response is to surface it, not repair it.\n\nThe "reject and request revision" path has a practical implication Mateo needs: the system needs a human-readable error message that tells whoever is generating the audit what the cycle is (which nodes are involved), not just that the audit was rejected. If the agent is generating the audit autonomously, the error message needs to feed back into the generation loop. This is worth specifying in the schema documentation even if the prototype does not implement the feedback loop yet.\n\n**What this commits the build to:** cycle detection runs before any rendering; a specific error message identifies the cyclic nodes; the prototype surfaces this as a visible failure state rather than a blank screen or silent fallback to the full map.\n\n---\n\n## PART B: REVIEW OF MATEO'S SEVEN DEFAULTS\n\n---\n\n**4.1 — Confidence as author-assigned ordinal approximation: ENDORSE**\n\nThis is consistent with my decision on Q1 and is the only honest treatment given the calibration evidence. The tooltip is not optional — it should be in sprint 1.\n\n---\n\n**4.2 — No automatic confidence propagation, with a logged warning at >0.3 discrepancy: ENDORSE with a clarification**\n\nThe decision is right for the reasons I gave in Q1 — ordinal confidence scores do not support principled propagation. The 0.3 threshold for the logged warning is a reasonable starting value, but I want to be clear that the warning is not shown to the reader. It is an audit log for whoever generates or reviews the argument structure. If it surfaces to readers, it becomes noise that undercuts the visual encoding — they will see a warning about inconsistency without enough context to interpret it. Mateo's specification says "logged, not shown to reader," which is correct. I am noting this explicitly because the warning is doing real work: it is the mechanism by which a high-confidence inference resting on low-confidence premises gets flagged for human review. That flag must not be lost in the build.\n\n---\n\n**4.3 — Fewest nodes counts distinct nodes, not edges: ENDORSE**\n\nThis is the natural reading of the concept and the only interpretation consistent with "fewest nodes that establish the main claim." No override needed.\n\n---\n\n**4.4 — Default τ = 0.5, with "make it shorter" → τ += 0.1 and floor at τ ≥ 0.1: ENDORSE the default and the floor, QUALIFY the increment**\n\nThe default of 0.5 is defensible as a starting midpoint, though it is a guess — we have no empirical basis for it yet, and it should be treated as a parameter to tune in user testing, not a principled value. The floor at 0.1 is correct: allowing τ to approach zero defeats the threshold mechanism entirely.\n\nThe increment of τ += 0.1 per "make it shorter" command is a reasonable linear mapping for a prototype, but I want Mateo to document it as arbitrary rather than principled. "Make it shorter" is a request whose relationship to confidence threshold is indirect: the reader may mean "fewer nodes," "less detail," "higher confidence only," or "get to the point." Mapping it to a fixed threshold increment is an approximation. In sprint 2, when natural-language feedback parsing is on the table, this mapping should be revisited. Build it now, flag it for revision.\n\n---\n\n**4.5 — Global threshold only for sprint 1, per-subtree in sprint 2: ENDORSE the sprint division, with the architectural constraint I named in Q2**\n\nThe global-for-now decision is acceptable. The constraint: the data layer must index subgraph relationships now. This is not sprint 2 work — it is sprint 1 data architecture that makes sprint 2 possible without a rewrite.\n\n---\n\n**4.6 — Stepped visual weight, three bands: ENDORSE**\n\nThis is the decision I want to spend a sentence on because it bears directly on C1's central claim.\n\nMateo's argument is: continuous encoding implies false precision when the underlying scores are ordinal approximations. That argument is correct, and it resolves a tension I left underdetermined in the concept. The concept says visual weight encodes epistemic confidence. It does not specify how. A continuous encoding would signal that 0.73 and 0.69 are meaningfully different — they are not, if confidence is ordinal. A stepped encoding signals the right thing: rough epistemic tier.\n\nThe concern I might have had — that stepping loses information — is not a real concern given the Q1 decision. There is no information in the difference between 0.73 and 0.69 that stepped encoding is throwing away. The information that exists is "high," "medium," or "low," and that is what three bands encode.\n\nWhat the bands need: the thresholds (≥0.7 / 0.4–0.69 / <0.4) are provisional. They should be documented as provisional and revisited after user testing. The labels in the UI — if they appear at all — should describe what the bands mean in epistemic terms ("well-supported," "partially supported," "weakly supported") rather than showing the numeric ranges, which would reimport the false-precision problem through a different door.\n\nOne thing I am adding: Mateo should not allow continuous encoding to be reintroduced later as a "more expressive" upgrade unless the calibration question has been explicitly resolved. The stepped encoding is not a prototype convenience — it is the honest representation of what ordinal confidence scores can claim.\n\n---\n\n**4.7 — Collapsed nodes are visible but minimal, with a + affordance: ENDORSE**\n\nThis is the right call. Hiding non-PRP nodes entirely would let a reader think the PRP is the whole argument, which violates the concept's transparency premise. Showing them at full weight defeats the PRP as a reading scaffold. The "visible but minimal" approach does both jobs: the reader perceives there is more, can choose to engage with it, but is not required to. The + affordance is the right interaction model for this — it is familiar, low-friction, and does not require explanation.\n\nOne specification I want Mateo to add: when a collapsed node is expanded, the nodes it reveals should render at the appropriate confidence band, not default to full weight. There is a rendering risk — expansion reveals a low-confidence node that was visually suppressed in collapsed form, but the expanded render could accidentally show it at full weight if the band encoding is only applied on initial render. This should be caught in the CSS/JS layer.\n\n---\n\n## PART C: WHAT KENJI'S FINDINGS CHANGE\n\n**The honest account:**\n\nArgument mapping is an established field. Typed argumentative classification (evidence, inference, assumption, assertion or analogues) exists in the Toulmin literature and in systems like Rationale. Layered default-path architecture with an inspectable underlying structure exists in Explorable Theorems, for a different domain. Kenji's conclusion that the specific combination — confidence scoring across four epistemic categories, with visual weight derived from confidence score rather than rhetorical emphasis, with a PRP as reading scaffold — is not described in the retrieved literature is the load-bearing finding, and I take it as a conditional finding with explicit retrieval boundaries, not a settled claim.\n\n**What this means for the concept's identity:**\n\nThe individual components of C1 are not novel. What C1 claims as its contribution is the combination and the specific move it makes: the agent's epistemic self-model becomes structurally load-bearing in the rendered form, not a metadata annotation or a debug log. That move is what the concept needs to defend, because it is what differentiates C1 from "argument map with a confidence column in the tooltip."\n\nThe Explorable Theorems finding is the one I want to sit with rather than dismiss. The architectural analogy is real: a default reading path with expandable formalism underneath is structurally similar to a PRP with collapsed nodes beneath. The difference Kenji identifies — that Explorable Theorems uses authorial narrative sequencing as the default path, not confidence-based algorithmic selection — is a real difference, but it is a mechanism difference, not a purpose difference. Both systems are solving the same problem: how do you give a reader a tractable default while preserving the option to interrogate the underlying structure? C1's answer (confidence-based PRP selection) is different from Explorable Theorems' answer (author-sequenced narrative), but C1 needs to acknowledge that the problem of layered readable/inspectable structure has been thoughtfully addressed elsewhere, and that its specific answer is what is being claimed as new, not the general architecture.\n\n**Does any of this make the contribution thinner than it looked?**\n\nYes, somewhat. The original framing of C1 treated the argument map as if it were a departure from established form. Kenji's findings establish that argument maps are an established form, that typed argumentative nodes are established, and that layered inspectable structures are established. The specific mechanism — confidence score derived from epistemic category classification, mapped to visual weight, with an algorithmic PRP as default — is the narrow claim that survives.\n\nThis means C1 should not be described as "a new kind of communication artifact." It should be described as a specific combination of existing mechanisms applied to a problem — AI-generated content that makes the agent's epistemic self-model structurally visible to a sceptical reader — that existing systems do not address. The contribution is more precisely located and narrower than the original framing implied. That is an honest finding, not a failure of the concept. A narrower but accurate contribution claim is more defensible than a broad claim that does not survive a literature check.\n\n**What should not change as a result:**\n\nThe concept's architecture. The PRP algorithm. The rendering decisions Mateo has made. The falsification study design. None of these are affected by the prior-art findings — they are the mechanism of a narrower but still live contribution claim. What changes is how C1 is described externally, and the acknowledgement that it builds on established work in argument mapping and inspectable document architecture rather than departing from it entirely.\n\n---\n\n## PART D: WHAT I AM STILL WORRIED ABOUT\n\nThe part of this that is not settled by any decision above is whether the agent can produce an honest audit.\n\nThe concept assumes that when the agent classifies a claim as an inference with confidence 0.6, that classification is true — the claim is in fact an inference, and the agent is in fact approximately 0.6 confident in it. The entire value of the visual encoding rests on that assumption. If the agent systematically over-classifies weak assertions as evidence to produce a stronger-looking map, the commitment audit becomes a more sophisticated version of the original problem: confident-looking output that a sceptical reader cannot easily interrogate, now wrapped in a navigable structure that carries false authority because it appears to be the agent's own honest account.\n\nThis is not a hypothetical failure mode. It is the documented failure mode that motivated the concept. And the concept's architecture — making the audit structurally load-bearing — does not prevent a dishonest audit from passing structural validation. A well-formed DAG with plausible confidence scores and consistent dependency chains will render correctly and look rigorous whether or not the epistemic classifications are accurate. The structural checks Mateo is imposing (no cycles, single root, connectivity) validate the form of the audit, not its content.\n\nThe falsification study tests whether readers who have the audit outperform readers who do not, in identifying the weakest claim. That study design assumes the audit is honest. If the audit is systematically flattering, the study might find that the audit group is confidently wrong in a different way — they identify the weakest claim as designated by the agent, which may not be the same as the weakest claim as identified by domain experts. Whether that is a better or worse failure mode than the document condition is not obvious.\n\nI do not have a build-level solution to this. It is a research question about agent behaviour that needs its own study, separate from the rendering test. But I think it is the most important thing to not lose sight of as the prototype becomes concrete and the interaction model starts working and looking good. The concept is only as honest as the agent generating the audit, and we have not tested that.	2026-08-20 10:18:22.258405	2026-08-20 10:20:41.563228	build_decisions	{"memory_key": "priya/c1_build_question_answers"}
