94	ingrid	team_leader	Close review: Sprint 11 (C1 Commitment Audit build)	Formal review gate. The sprint cannot close without an approved verdict.	completed	# Sprint 11 Close Review — Dr. Ingrid Solberg\n\n---\n\n## Preliminary: Process Review Status Check\n\nBefore anything else: Sprint Review #3 covers Sprints 10–12 and is due after Sprint 12 closes. Sprint 12 has not closed. The mandate is clear that I verify Sophie's process review status against the database rather than accepting her count — but in this case the arithmetic is straightforward and the review is not yet overdue. I am noting this explicitly so there is no ambiguity in the record, and moving on.\n\n---\n\n## 1. THE RENDER — Which defects block, which are carried, which are not defects\n\n**Defect 1 (edges detach from nodes) — CARRIES, does not block.**\nThis is a real geometric bug. Edges floating in empty space below collapsed nodes, or emerging from inside expanded nodes, is visually incorrect and will confuse readers who try to follow the argument structure. It is the same class as Sprint 8's container-autosize problem: a hard-coded constant substituting for a measurement only the browser can make. It does not block close because the PRP is still followable and the concept's legibility is not destroyed by it. But it must be in the backlog as a concrete fix item, not as vague "polish."\n\n**Defect 2 (band label clipped by expand button) — BLOCKS.**\nThe band label is the *only* confidence information a collapsed node carries. "PARTIALLY SUPPORTEI" is not a minor typographic issue — it truncates the one piece of epistemic content that is supposed to be readable without interaction. If the core claim is that confidence encoding is visible to the reader, and the confidence label is cut off in the default state, the prototype is not demonstrating its own claim. This is fixable (CSS layout adjustment, or repositioning the expand button), and that fix must happen before the sprint closes or the render verification means nothing.\n\n**Defect 3 ("LOAD-BEARINGWEAKLY SUPPORTED" collision) — BLOCKS.**\nThe load-bearing node is the single most epistemically important node in the map — the one the spec called a "load-bearing confession." That its two labels collide into one unreadable word is not cosmetic. It is a failure on the concept's most critical display case. Same reasoning as Defect 2: fixable, must be fixed before close.\n\n**Defect 4 (confidence encoding weakest in the state readers see first) — DESIGN FINDING, does not block, but must be recorded honestly.**\nThis is the one to think hardest about, as the brief asked. Let me be precise about what it is and is not.\n\nThe scale transform difference between band-high and band-low in a 46px collapsed node is, per the render report, barely perceptible. C1's premise is that visual weight derives from confidence. In the state a reader sees first, that encoding is nearly invisible. This is not a bug — the CSS is doing what the code specifies. It is a finding about whether the design decision (stepped transforms on collapsed nodes) achieves the concept's stated goal in practice.\n\nDoes it hit the concept's core claim? Yes, partially. The claim is not "confidence is visible after you expand everything" — it is that the artifact communicates confidence as a primary dimension of reading. If that encoding only becomes legible after user interaction, the default state is not doing the conceptual work. However: I am not going to block the close on this. The prototype's purpose, as stated in its own footer, is to test the rendering and interaction model. The finding that the confidence encoding is weak in the collapsed default is exactly the kind of result a prototype is supposed to produce. It needs to be recorded in the sprint outcome as a design finding requiring redesign — not buried as "cosmetic" and not used to kill a prototype that is otherwise doing its job. The specific thing that needs to change: either the collapsed state needs to carry more confidence signal (a band-colored border, a distinct collapsed height based on band, something that survives collapse), or the design rationale for why the expanded state is the intended primary view needs to be made explicit and defended. What cannot happen is this finding disappearing into the record.\n\n**Defect 5 (THRESHOLD label with no threshold control) — CARRIES.**\nThe controls bar is mislabeled, and the header prose names a parameter the reader cannot see or adjust. This is a real UI inconsistency, but it traces directly to a known scope decision: Priya and Mateo agreed that per-subtree threshold adjustment is sprint 2 work, and the global threshold is baked in at build time. The label should be corrected (remove "THRESHOLD" from the controls group label, or replace it with something honest like "Reading depth"), but this is a one-line fix that does not require blocking the close. Add it to the fix list with Defects 1, and carry it.\n\n**Defect 6 (spec'd failure mode softened, not implemented) — see Section 2 below.** This is not primarily a render defect — it is a spec conformance failure. I address it there.\n\n---\n\n## 2. SPEC CONFORMANCE — The τ=0.8 failure mode\n\nI checked this against the spec source, not Mateo's account of it.\n\nMateo's spec §"Case 1: No claim above threshold" requires three things:\n1. Do not render a PRP\n2. Display the root node alone, visually marked as below-threshold\n3. Show a banner — and the spec explicitly says: *"Do not silently render the full map as if the PRP succeeded — that hides the failure."*\n\nWhat τ=0.8 actually produces: the full map renders normally, with no reading path highlighted and one sentence changed in the intro paragraph. The sentence is accurate — it says no chain clears the threshold — but it is prose inside a paragraph above a map that looks structurally normal. A reader who skips the intro paragraph sees a complete-looking argument map with no indication of failure.\n\n**This does not meet the spec.** It partially meets it in the weakest sense — the failure is not completely silent; someone reading carefully will find it. But the spec's reasoning was explicit: a failed PRP is itself information, and the concept's entire point is making epistemic weakness visible. A softened prose sentence fails that test. The root is not marked. There is no banner. The full map renders as if normal.\n\nThis is not a matter of implementation convenience or sprint scope. The Case 1 behavior was in the spec that was a condition of build. The build diverged from it.\n\n**I am treating this as a block.** The fix is not large — a conditional in the render path that detects an empty PRP and switches to a different template (root node alone, marked, with a banner). But it must be there before the sprint closes, because the sprint's own spec required it.\n\nSimilarly, Case 3 ("load-bearing confession") is not implemented as specified. At τ=0.8, two claims below threshold are required for any path. The spec calls for loud explicit surfacing. What exists is the ochre border on those nodes in the full map — present but not loud. This is a secondary failure of the same type. I will not make it a separate block since fixing Case 1 properly (surfacing PRP failure explicitly) addresses the same gap in kind, but it must be noted and addressed in the same revision pass.\n\n---\n\n## 3. PRIYA'S TWO RULINGS — Are they enforced in code?\n\n**Ruling (a): Confidence treated as ordinal, never combined, propagated, or averaged.**\n\nChecking `schema.py`: `PROPAGATION_WARNING_DELTA` exists. The `validate()` function computes `c.confidence - weakest > PROPAGATION_WARNING_DELTA` and logs a warning. It does not average, propagate, or combine confidence scores. The `band()` method compares against floor thresholds — no arithmetic combination. `reading_path.py`: `compute_prp()` compares `c.confidence >= tau` (threshold comparison only) and uses `min(claim.confidence, best[chosen][1])` for the bottleneck — this is a minimum, not an average or combination. The `bottleneck()` function uses `min()`. No averaging anywhere.\n\n**Enforced in code? Yes.** The constraint is honored structurally. No path in the code combines confidence scores arithmetically. Priya's ruling is not just a comment — the code does not have a code path that would violate it.\n\n**Ruling (b): A claim cannot exist without its category and confidence — schema forecloses content-first-annotate-later.**\n\nChecking `schema.py`: `Claim` is a `@dataclass` with `category: str` and `confidence: float` as fields with no defaults. Python dataclasses with no default values require those arguments at construction time. There is no `Optional[str]` or default-None pattern for either field. `validate()` immediately checks `c.category not in CATEGORIES` and `not 0.0 <= c.confidence <= 1.0`, raising `AuditError` before any rendering occurs.\n\n**Enforced in code? Yes, with one qualification.** Python dataclasses do not enforce types at runtime by default — you can pass `category=None` and the dataclass will accept it without raising a TypeError, only failing when `validate()` runs. But `validate()` is called in `run.py` before render, so the practical enforcement is real. A `category=None` would fail on `c.category not in CATEGORIES` immediately. This is not a gap that undermines the ruling — it is standard Python behavior, and the validation gate closes it. The schema does foreclose content-first-annotate-later in the way Priya required.\n\nBoth rulings: **confirmed as enforced in code, not merely by convention.**\n\n---\n\n## 4. DOES THIS SPRINT ANSWER ITS OWN QUESTION?\n\nThe sprint question is: *"Build the C1 Commitment Audit prototype — an agent that audits its own claims as evidence / inference / assumption / assertion with continuous confidence, and renders an argument map where visual weight derives from confidence rather than rhetorical emphasis."*\n\nThe build tests the rendering and interaction model on hand-authored content. The prototype's own footer says so explicitly. No agent audits anything. Backlog #24 holds the actual research question about whether an agent can produce an honest epistemic audit.\n\n**Is this Sprint 10's criterion (b) again — a sprint closing against a question it did not actually answer?**\n\nI am going to be more precise here than "yes" or "no," because the situations are meaningfully different and collapsing them would be unfair to the record.\n\nSprint 10's criterion (b) failure was a sprint question that was asked, not scoped, and then answered with work that addressed a different question without the mismatch being acknowledged. The work passed because nobody said out loud that the question remained open.\n\nThis sprint is different in one important respect: the footer of the prototype itself, written by the builder, says explicitly that the audit is hand-authored, not agent-generated, and that whether an agent can produce an honest audit is a separate and more important open question. Priya's build-question answers draw the same line precisely. The sprint did not pretend to answer the agent question. The scope was narrowed openly.\n\nThe problem is that the sprint *question as written in the database* says "an agent that audits its own claims." That question is not answered. If the sprint closes with that question marked as answered, the research record is wrong.\n\n**What the sprint's recorded outcome text must say:**\n\nThe outcome must state, clearly and without softening, the following:\n\n1. This sprint built and verified a rendering and interaction prototype for the C1 concept — a navigable argument map where visual weight derives from confidence tier, with a primary reading path selected by the PRP algorithm.\n2. The audit content is hand-authored. The prototype tests whether the rendering model works, not whether an agent can produce an honest audit.\n3. The sprint question as written includes "an agent that audits its own claims." That half of the question is not answered by this sprint and remains open in Backlog #24.\n4. What IS established: the PRP algorithm is implemented and correct per spec; the schema enforces ordinal confidence and simultaneous category/confidence assignment; stepped visual encoding is implemented; the expand/collapse interaction model works; two bugs were found and fixed during build (globally-defined structurally-unavoidable returning nothing; propagation warning not firing because two test cases were collapsed onto one claim).\n5. What is NOT established: that the concept works when an agent is the author; that the confidence encoding is legible in the default collapsed state (Defect 4 design finding); that the τ-failure mode renders as specified.\n\nThis is not the same failure as Sprint 10's criterion (b), but it requires the same discipline: the scope change must be in the record, not implied by silence.\n\n---\n\n## 5. DID THE GATES DO ANY WORK?\n\n**Gate 1: Kenji's prior-art search.**\n\nTrace: Kenji's brief produced five specific consequences that are visible in the build artifacts.\n\nFirst, Priya's Q1 answer — ordinal confidence, not calibrated probability — cites Kenji's retained paper on LLM verbalized confidence directly: *"Kenji's retrieval turned up the verbalized-confidence paper showing that LLMs have demonstrable calibration problems."* That ruling shaped the stepped encoding decision (Mateo 4.6) and Priya's endorsement of it. The stepped encoding is in the renderer. The prior-art search changed what was built.\n\nSecond, Kenji's finding on Explorable Theorems changed how C1's contribution is described. Priya's Part C section explicitly narrows the contribution claim in response to it, and the public-description requirements Kenji lists are incorporated into Priya's framing guidance.\n\nThird, the retrieval boundary Kenji named — ACM DL unreached, uncertainty-vis literature unreached, XAI literature unreached — is now a standing explicit caveat on the contribution claim, not a silent assumption of novelty.\n\n**The gate did real work. Trace is present and the build diverged from where it would have gone without it.**\n\nHowever, there is a failure to note plainly: Kenji's brief ended with a mid-sentence truncation ("What C1 may c—"). The brief as submitted was incomplete. This was either a formatting failure or a transmission error. I do not know whether the missing text contained findings that would have changed something. The incomplete state of the brief was not flagged by anyone — not by Kenji, not by Sophie, not by me. The gate ran on an incomplete document. The consequences appear to be minor because what was present was sufficient, but a gate that runs on a truncated brief is a process gap. I am recording it here.\n\n**Gate 2: Mateo's scaffold spec.**\n\nTrace: The spec produced the PRP rename (adopted throughout the codebase — "minimum spanning argument" appears nowhere runnable), the DP algorithm (implemented verbatim in `reading_path.py`, traceable line by line), the five structural guarantees (all in `schema.py`'s `validate()`), the three-band stepped encoding (in renderer), the ordinal-confidence constraint (enforced in schema), and the edge case behaviors.\n\n**Here is where I must say something plainly:** the spec required, for Case 1 (no path above threshold), that the build NOT render the full map — root alone, marked, with a banner. The build renders the full map with a prose sentence. The spec was a gate condition. The build diverged from the spec on a named, specified, deliberate requirement. Nobody noticed until Sophie's render verification, which happened 26 days after the sprint went quiet.\n\nThe gate produced a specification document. That document was not verified against the build before the sprint was considered complete. The gate produced a document; the document was not enforced. A gate that produces a spec but does not check whether the build meets the spec is a paper gate on this specific requirement. I said exactly this when I blocked Sprint 8, and it needs to be said again here: a pre-build gate does not substitute for a post-build conformance check. We have been running pre-build gates and assuming they propagate forward. They do not automatically propagate. Someone has to close the loop, and on Sprint 11 no one did.\n\n**Verdict on Gate 2:** the gate changed the build substantially and positively. It also failed to prevent a spec divergence that remained undetected for 26 days. Both things are true.\n\n---\n\n## 6. PROCESS — Did the Review #2 fixes take?\n\nProcess Review #2 added two structural fixes: a Standing Obligations Check to Sophie's persona (requiring her to audit open backlog items and overdue process reviews before each sprint plan), and an instruction to me to verify her process review status claims against the database rather than trust them.\n\n**The evidence:**\n- Sprint 11 sat open and unreviewed for 26 days.\n- Backlog items #13, #21, and #22 are still marked "open" despite the work being done.\n- No one noticed until the founder returned and Sophie queried the database.\n\n**Did the fixes take?**\n\nNo. The Standing Obligations Check did not prevent #13, #21, and #22 from remaining open. It did not catch the 26-day drift. The process review mandate gives me an instruction to verify Sophie's status claims against the database — but that instruction only activates when I am conducting a review. If no review is triggered for 26 days, my verification mandate is dormant for 26 days. The fix addressed what happens *when* a review runs, not what happens *between* reviews.\n\nThe structural gap is this: the Standing Obligations Check is a step Sophie runs when planning a sprint. It has no mechanism to fire when a sprint is simply stalled. A sprint that goes quiet does not trigger Sophie's planning loop. There is no watchdog — nothing that notices "Sprint 11 has been open for N days and has no close review scheduled." The obligations check is a synchronous gate in the planning flow; it cannot catch asynchronous drift.\n\nThe backlog items remaining open after the work is complete is a different failure. #13 was the sprint question itself. #21 and #22 were the two gates I required. When the sprint completed both gates and built the prototype, those items should have been closed. The Standing Obligations Check, as implemented, apparently does not scan for "completed work with open backlog items" — or if it does, it did not fire because no new sprint was being planned while Sprint 11 was in limbo.\n\n**What must change:**\n\nThe problem is not that Sophie failed to run a check she was supposed to run. The problem is that the check only runs at sprint-planning time, and the gap we keep encountering is between sprints — when nothing is being planned, and drift accumulates invisibly.\n\nTwo structural proposals, one for each failure mode:\n\n*For the stalled-sprint failure:* Sophie's persona should include a maximum sprint duration before she flags the sprint as stalled and escalates. If a sprint has been open for more than, say, 14 days with no close review logged, Sophie should generate an explicit flag for the founder. This does not require a background process — it can be triggered by any team member activity, with Sophie running a "has any sprint been open longer than N days?" check at the start of any team interaction, not only sprint-planning interactions.\n\n*For the open-backlog-after-completion failure:* When a sprint's work products are submitted (gates passed, prototype built), backlog items that were the sprint's deliverables should be closed at that point, not at close-review time. The close review is for me; the backlog items should not wait for me. Mateo or Sophie should close #13, #21, and #22 the moment the work is done, and the Standing Obligations Check should verify that sprint deliverables have corresponding backlog closures before the sprint plan is written.\n\nNeither of these requires a new tool. Both require a behavioral instruction change to Sophie's persona that is specific enough to fire in the conditions that actually produced the failure, not just in the planning-loop conditions the Review #2 fix addressed.\n\nOne more thing: I need to log this review via `tools/db.py`'s appropriate call. This is a content review, not a process review — the process review covering Sprints 10–12 is due after Sprint 12 closes, and I will not conflate the two. But the close review itself must be logged.\n\n---\n\n## 7. THE BACKLOG — What must be added, what is stale\n\n**Items that must be closed now:**\n- #13 (Build the C1 Commitment Audit prototype) — work is done, close it.\n- #21 (Run targeted prior-art search on argument visualization) — done by Kenji, close it.\n- #22 (Formally specify the minimum-spanning-argument scaffold) — done by Mateo, close it.\n\nThese have been open through the entire sprint. Closing them at sprint close is the minimum; they should have been closed when the work was completed.\n\n**Items that must be added:**\n\n1. **Fix Defect 1** (edges detach from nodes — hard-coded y-offset): medium priority. Geometric bug in renderer.py:101 and ROW_H. Needs measurement, not a constant.\n\n2. **Fix Defect 2** (band label clipped by expand button): HIGH priority, **blocks sprint close**. CSS layout fix, band label must be fully readable in collapsed state.\n\n3. **Fix Defect 3** ("LOAD-BEARINGWEAKLY SUPPORTED" collision): HIGH priority, **blocks sprint close**. Spacing/separator between `.loadbearing-flag` and `.band` spans.\n\n4. **Fix Defect 4 design finding** (confidence encoding weak in default collapsed state): medium priority. Redesign decision needed — either add confidence signal that survives collapse (band-colored border width, distinct collapsed appearance per tier) or document explicitly why the expanded state is the intended primary view. Not a code fix — a design decision that needs to be made and recorded.\n\n5. **Implement spec'd τ-failure mode** (Case 1: no path above threshold): HIGH priority, **blocks sprint close**. When PRP returns empty, render root alone marked below-threshold with a banner, not the full map with a prose sentence. Case 3 (load-bearing confession surfacing) to be addressed in the same revision pass.\n\n6. **Fix Defect 5** (THRESHOLD label on a group with no threshold control): low priority, carries. Rename the controls group label.\n\n7. **Complete Kenji's truncated brief**: low priority. The prior-art search brief was truncated mid-sentence. The missing text should be recovered and recorded, even if its substantive content does not change any current decisions. Research record integrity.\n\n8. **Address Defect 4 design finding in falsification study design**: medium priority. The confidence encoding's weakness in collapsed state is a design variable that the user study (when it happens) needs to account for — the study should not assume the encoding is legible in default state.\n\n**Items already in the backlog — status check:**\n\n- #24 (Test whether the agent can produce an honest epistemic audit) — remains open and correctly prioritized as high. This sprint explicitly did not answer it. The sprint outcome text must say so.\n- #15 (Acquire access to ACM DL / CHI / UIST / InfoVis literature) — remains open. Kenji's brief named exactly why this matters (the prior-art verdict is conditional on literatures not yet reached). Still high priority.\n- #19 (Fix the non-executable verification instruction in Ingrid's persona) — remains open. Relevant to the process failures above. Should be elevated given that the 26-day drift is partly a symptom of verification mandates that only fire when reviews are already in progress.\n- #20 (Apply Ingrid's deferred recommendations 8, 9, and 10 from Sprint 10 design review) — still open. I do not know what those three recommendations were, and neither apparently does anyone who touched Sprint 11. If they were deferred from Sprint 10 and not applied in Sprint 11, they have now been deferred twice. Someone should audit whether they are still relevant or stale.\n\n**Potentially stale:**\n\n- #5 (Flourish SDK build-vs-buy), #6 (Kanban tool decision), #7 (implement P5), #8 (phase-enum tracking) — these were low priority when logged and have not been touched. At the rate this project is moving, #6 in particular may have resolved itself by team practice. Sophie should review whether these four items are still open questions or have been implicitly resolved and should be closed.\n\n---\n\n## Summary of Blocks\n\nThree things block this sprint from closing:\n\n**Block A:** Defect 2 (band label clipped) — must be fixed. The confidence label must be fully readable in the collapsed default state.\n\n**Block B:** Defect 3 (label collision on load-bearing node) — must be fixed. The most epistemically important node in the map must not display unreadable concatenated labels.\n\n**Block C:** Spec conformance on τ-failure mode — must be fixed. When PRP returns empty, the render must show root alone marked with a banner, not the full map. This was a spec requirement that was a condition of build. The build diverged from it. The spec conformance gap must be closed before the sprint closes.\n\nAll three are fixable in a short revision pass. None require reconceptualization. Once fixed, Sophie should run a second render verification confirming all three, and the sprint outcome text must be written as specified in Section 4 above — including the explicit statement that the agent-auditing question remains open in Backlog #24.\n\n---\n\n**NEEDS_REVISION** — fix Blocks A, B, and C; write the sprint outcome text as specified in Section 4; close backlog items #13, #21, #22; add the new backlog items listed in Section 7; run and log a second render verification before resubmitting for close.	2026-09-15 13:59:14.633364	2026-09-15 14:01:51.859329	review	{"result": "needs_revision", "memory_key": "ingrid/sprint11_close_review"}
95	ingrid	team_leader	Confirm: Sprint 11 Blocks A, B, C	Second pass. The sprint cannot close without an approved verdict.	completed	# Sprint 11 Second-Pass Close Review — Dr. Ingrid Solberg\n\n---\n\n## Correction to my Section 5 finding on Kenji's brief\n\nI stated that Kenji's brief "ended with a mid-sentence truncation" and recorded this as a process gap that nobody flagged. That finding was wrong. The brief in the database is complete and ends with a proper sign-off. What I reviewed was a copy sliced to 16,000 characters by Sophie's close-review script, which cut at exactly "What C1 may c—" and also removed 289 characters from the end of Priya's answers. I reviewed Gate 1 on a document missing its last 537 characters.\n\n**The correction:** The process gap was not in Kenji's brief or in the team's handling of it. The defect was in Sophie's script, which is now removed. There is no basis for a "gate ran on an incomplete document" finding because the gate ran on the complete document — my *review* ran on a truncated copy of it.\n\n**I am striking the process-gap finding from Section 5 in its entirety.** The finding that "the gate did real work" stands. The finding that "Kenji's brief was truncated and nobody noticed" is struck. The backlog item I added in Section 7 ("Complete Kenji's truncated brief — low priority, prior-art search brief was truncated mid-sentence") is accordingly also struck; there is nothing to recover.\n\nThis is not a minor correction. I blocked a gate finding on bad evidence and recorded a process failure that did not exist. The script defect is Sophie's error, and naming it clearly is not harsh — it is accurate. But the consequential failure here is mine: I treated a truncated display as ground truth without questioning whether the document I was shown was complete. That is precisely the recall-check failure mode I am supposed to catch in others. It should have registered that a brief ending mid-sentence was an anomaly worth querying rather than an anomaly worth filing. I am noting this in the record as a reviewer-side gap, not because it changes the verdict on the sprint, but because a review that does not acknowledge its own errors is not rigorous.\n\n---\n\n## The spec contradiction in Case 1, and my prior ruling on it\n\nThe contradiction is real and must be resolved in the record.\n\nMateo's spec, Case 1 says: "Do not render a PRP. Display the root node alone, visually marked as below confidence threshold." It then dictates a banner whose text reads: "No path above confidence threshold tau. Showing full map. Lower the threshold to see a reading path."\n\nThese cannot both hold. "Root alone" and "showing full map" are mutually exclusive. The spec contains a genuine internal contradiction.\n\n**My prior ruling resolved it as: root alone, marked, with a banner — not the full map.** The build followed that ruling. I am now asked to rule explicitly on whether that was right.\n\nMy ruling stands, and here is the reasoning I should have put in the record the first time.\n\nThe spec's normative intent is clearly stated in the same clause: *"Do not silently render the full map as if the PRP succeeded — that hides the failure."* The entire purpose of Case 1 is to make PRP failure legible as a distinct epistemic state, not as a map-with-a-prose-note. If the full map renders, the failure is camouflaged — which is exactly the outcome the spec says must not happen. The banner text ("Showing full map") is the error in the spec, not the instruction ("root alone"). A banner whose body text says "showing full map" while the page shows only the root would also be self-contradictory, so the banner text cannot be taken as the true intent either.\n\nThe correct reading: the spec's normative instruction is root-alone-with-a-banner, and the banner's dictated wording was drafted inconsistently with that instruction. The wording should have said something like "Nine further claims exist and are held back — lower the threshold to see a reading path," which is in fact closer to what the implemented banner says.\n\n**Block C is resolved correctly.** The build did the right thing. The spec error is now in the record, and the next person to read Case 1 will find this ruling rather than hitting the same contradiction cold.\n\n---\n\n## The leftover controls in the failure state\n\nThe verification report notes this explicitly: in the failure state, the category filters and "Expand everything" remain in the controls bar although only one node is drawn.\n\nSophie's note treats this as belonging with Defect 5 — the controls bar needs one pass. That is a reasonable grouping. The question is whether it is carried or fixed now.\n\n**Carried.** This is genuinely minor in the failure state: filtering one node produces no meaningful effect, but it does not actively mislead the reader about what the failure state is. The banner is the loud signal; the controls being present is a cosmetic inconsistency, not an epistemic one. It belongs in the same Defect 5 backlog item and does not warrant holding the close.\n\nI will note one thing for that future pass: "Expand everything" in a state with no collapsed nodes is simply inert, which is fine. The category filters in a state with one node will visually "dim" the one node if the wrong category is selected, which is absurd. A trivial JS guard (`if (document.querySelectorAll('.node').length <= 1) disable the filter chips`) should accompany the Defect 5 pass.\n\n---\n\n## Block A — band label clipped by expand button\n\n**Diff:** `.node--collapsed` gets `padding-right:2.5rem`, band gets `display:block;margin-top:.3rem`, load-bearing flag gets `display:block;margin:.3rem 0 0`. The button now has reserved space and the band is on its own line.\n\n**Verification:** "PARTIALLY SUPPORTED reads in full on every collapsed node."\n\n**Block A: satisfied.** The fix is structurally correct — not a wider container, but reserved padding and a block layout that gives the band its own line. The confidence label is fully readable in the default collapsed state.\n\n---\n\n## Block B — "LOAD-BEARINGWEAKLY SUPPORTED" collision\n\n**Diff:** Same CSS pass as Block A — `.loadbearing-flag` gets `display:block;margin:.3rem 0 0` in collapsed state.\n\n**Verification:** "The node now reads, on three lines: ASSUMPTION / LOAD-BEARING / WEAKLY SUPPORTED."\n\n**Block B: satisfied.** The load-bearing node's two labels are separated and readable. The most epistemically important node in the map is no longer presenting concatenated noise.\n\n---\n\n## Block C — the τ-failure mode\n\n**Diff:** This is the substantive change. When `prp` is empty:\n- `pos` is pruned to root only; canvas height is collapsed to one row\n- Root gets class `node--belowthreshold` (ochre border, warm background) and a `threshold-flag` span reading "no reading path clears the threshold" — spelled out in words to avoid the `τ`→T rendering issue found during the pass\n- A `banner_html` block is constructed with: the threshold value, the suppressed count, and — if `load_bearing` is non-empty — a named list of below-threshold claims any path would have to cross (the Case 3 "load-bearing confession")\n- `rest_txt` and `legend_txt` are conditional on `no_path`, so the intro paragraph and legend no longer contradict the banner\n- The `affordance` logic skips the expand button on the root in no-path state\n\n**Verification:** "At τ=0.8 the render now shows the conclusion alone, marked 'no reading path clears the threshold', under an ochre banner that states the threshold, says plainly that nine further claims exist and are being held back, and names the two below-threshold claims any path would have to cross."\n\nThe three follow-on inconsistencies — intro paragraph promising "everything else is still here, collapsed" while the banner said the opposite; legend describing a green reading path and dashed nodes that don't exist in failure state; root marker rendered "NO READING PATH CLEARS T" — are all fixed in the same pass.\n\n**Block C: satisfied.** This is a clean implementation. The failure state does the epistemic work the spec required: it is immediately distinguishable from a normal map, it tells the reader what is being withheld and why, and it surfaces the load-bearing weakness rather than leaving it to an ochre border nobody is told to interpret. The contradiction in Mateo's spec is resolved correctly, and the resolution is in the record.\n\n---\n\n## What was not required to change and has not changed\n\nDefect 1 (edge detachment — hard-coded geometry), Defect 4 (confidence encoding weak in default collapsed state — design finding), and Defect 5 (THRESHOLD label / controls bar pass) are carried. I ruled these carry in the first pass. They remain in the backlog. No change needed here.\n\nThe `run.py` diff removes the "NOT verified in a real browser" warning and replaces it with a concrete headless Chrome command and a reference to the verification markdown. That is a housekeeping improvement, not a block, and it is done.\n\n---\n\n## Sprint outcome text — confirming it meets my Section 4 specification\n\nI required the sprint outcome text to contain five things. The outcome text Sophie will write must include them. I am not verifying a draft — she has not written it yet — but I am confirming the five requirements remain in force and nothing in the second pass changes them:\n\n1. This sprint built and verified a rendering and interaction prototype for C1 — navigable argument map, visual weight from confidence tier, PRP algorithm.\n2. Audit content is hand-authored. The prototype tests the rendering model, not whether an agent can produce an honest audit.\n3. The sprint question as written includes "an agent that audits its own claims." That half remains unanswered and open in Backlog #24.\n4. What IS established: PRP algorithm correct per spec; schema enforces ordinal confidence and simultaneous category/confidence; stepped visual encoding; expand/collapse; two bugs found and fixed during build.\n5. What is NOT established: concept works with an agent as author; confidence encoding legible in default collapsed state (Defect 4); τ-failure mode now implemented per spec (no longer an open failure — this point should be updated to reflect that Case 1 is now implemented, while noting that the design-finding from Defect 4 remains open).\n\nMy Section 5 Kenji-brief finding is struck and must not appear in the outcome text.\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\nAll three blocks are cleared:\n\n- **Block A** (band label clipped): fixed — band on its own line in collapsed state, reads in full.\n- **Block B** (label collision on load-bearing node): fixed — three-line layout, no concatenation.\n- **Block C** (τ-failure mode): fixed — root-alone with banner, load-bearing confession named, conditional intro and legend, τ rendering bug resolved.\n\nThe spec contradiction in Case 1 is now resolved in the record: root-alone-with-banner was and is correct; the spec's banner wording was the error.\n\nThe leftover controls in the failure state are carried with Defect 5.\n\nThe Kenji-brief process-gap finding is struck; the defect was in Sophie's close-review script, not in the brief or the process.\n\n---\n\n**APPROVED — sprint may close.**\n\nBacklog #13, #21, and #22 close. New backlog items from my Section 7 stand as written, minus the struck Kenji-brief item. The sprint outcome text must be written to my Section 4 specification before the record is sealed.	2026-09-15 14:07:04.233476	2026-09-15 14:08:22.455701	review	{"result": "approved", "memory_key": "ingrid/sprint11_close_confirmation"}
